{"@graph":[{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/","identifier":"content:getharsh-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"Harsh Joshi","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"Harsh Joshi builds products, explores intelligence and makes things, with AI since 2012.","dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Harsh Joshi\nI like building products and meeting new people. This is my decentralised garage for engineering, intelligence and stories. Snoop around and Reach out if something catches your eye.\nHarsh Joshi, smiling, rendered as fine monochrome computer glyphs.\nExplore the projects\nHarsh Joshi laughing and turned to the left, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs.\nEverything in the garage: tools for people and agents, research into intelligence, and a graphic novel.\nExplore projects\nThe person behind the work\nHarsh Joshi laughing and turned to the right, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs.\nFrom Lucknow and Almora to hospital plant rooms and agentic AI: the people, places and turning points behind the work.\nAbout me\nQuestions that stay open\nHarsh Joshi laughing and turned to the left, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs.\nHow does understanding become useful? What should a working system remember? What can a story let us see that a model misses?\nRead my Thoughts\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/renditions/harsh-portrait/rest.webp","description":"Harsh Joshi, smiling, rendered as fine monochrome computer glyphs."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/renditions/harsh-portrait/peek-left.webp","description":"Harsh Joshi laughing and turned to the left, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/renditions/harsh-portrait/peek-right.webp","description":"Harsh Joshi laughing and turned to the right, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/renditions/harsh-portrait/peek-left.webp","description":"Harsh Joshi laughing and turned to the left, rendered in the same monochrome glyphs."}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/about/","identifier":"content:getharsh-about","@type":"AboutPage","name":"About Harsh Joshi","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"description":"The curiosity, experiences and people behind my practice.","text":"I grew up in Lucknow, with roots in Almora.\nI studied Electronics and Communication Engineering at Dehradun Institute of Technology, under Uttarakhand Technical University, from 2012 to 2016. I also studied remote sensing and satellite navigation through IIRS. My formal training began in electronics, but my reading never stayed there. Questions about time led into philosophy and texts such as the Katha Upanishad. Building circuits and reading a dialogue between Nachiketa and Yama were different ways of spending an afternoon, yet both could leave me wondering whether I had understood the question at all.\nI have bought more books than I can read, played piano, made art, trained for MMA tournaments and hiked through much of India, spending time in monasteries along the way. You could call it an extensive period of regression testing on a personal front. Work has been just as exploratory. The people I could learn from often mattered as much as the job. In an early team, I could be cleaning the office floor, testing a PCB, designing an algorithm, writing firmware, talking to a vendor or helping someone else find their way into the problem. That breadth was an education in how much surrounds the apparently technical task.\nBuilding with other people\nMy first research grew from an undergraduate thesis, Project Netra, with NEFORD and ICAR-CSSRI's Regional Research Station in Lucknow. We were investigating inexpensive image analysis and embedded measurement for reclaiming sodic soil. It put the question of what a measurement means beside the people who needed to use it. During those college years I was a Google Student Ambassador and received NRDC Young Innovator and Intel Innovator recognition. The research also took me into guest lectures at IISR, IWMI in Laos, IIRS Dehradun, Pantnagar University and Allahabad University: another way of finding out whether I could make the work intelligible to somebody else.\nSmart Joules grew from idea stage to Series A. I joined as its founding engineer in November 2016 and progressed to Product Development Lead, working across DeJoule, JouleLabs and JouleMind through March 2020. I also represented the work outside the plant: on the panel at NREDCAP’s Powering Andhra Pradesh summit and at Shell’s Make the Future in Singapore.\nField work changed what I expected from engineering. The operator needed a usable control, not our internal picture of the network. At the same time, building the team meant explaining the purpose of the work and making room for people to contribute. I founded and ran JouleLabs around that connection: students and mentors working on real problems, with support that continued beyond handing over a brief.\nAt Zenoti, customer evidence pushed me in another direction. Support, onboarding and taxation for small businesses needed a simpler experience inside a much broader suite. Understanding more of the system let us ask less of the customer. It also changed my sense of ambition: removing the right complexity can be harder, and more useful, than adding another feature.\nFinding a way to build\nWhile I was discovering myself, I was also putting bread on the table: building ventures as an adviser and consultant, building two startups from zero as a founder, and working as a product manager in a startup that had already scaled. AI ran through all of it. I was lucky in the people I got to learn from, including people who had spent longer in the industry than I had been alive.\nMy work with Sachait Technologies Pvt. Ltd. grew from helping friends into cofounder responsibility for product and engineering. Emergency response made the gap between detecting an event and helping a person impossible to overlook: false alarms, human review and the next responder all belonged to the product. That work continued as YOJN Care. In October 2022, I co-founded DAO Studio with Shashwat Pandey. Amul and Lijjat Papad inspired its original ambition: help people create decentralised autonomous organisations. Exploring how AI could support that ambition led into making intelligence easier to use. I led its product, technology and company work through September 2024, and built YojnAI as an individual contributor. Composing model and tool work, inspecting the path and learning from feedback brought earlier product questions into a new technical setting.\nIn September 2024 I built a reflection agent for code and for structured OpenAPI and gRPC specifications with Specmatic. Later work brought me into engineering leadership at Accacia and GPU-cloud product work at AION. The settings changed what I needed to understand: an agreement between services, the meaning inside a building-material document, the workload behind a compute purchase. I kept product responsibility close to hands-on engineering. Organising a team did not remove my desire to understand the mechanism; it made explaining its consequences to other people more important.\nMy interests widened with those changes. Compute became a question about useful capacity and the economic arrangements around it. I also found myself building explanations alongside products. A writer arriving at a technical subject needs more than a title; an engineer making a product decision needs more than a summary. Finding the connection between the predicament, the mechanism and the argument became another part of the work.\nA practice beyond the job\nTeaching has stayed with me. I volunteer with and help lead UPAI, the Uttar Pradesh AI community. Lucknow AI Labs, or LAI, is its founding Lucknow chapter. A student's question or a collaborator's objection can reveal something I had stopped noticing. I am interested in education that gives curiosity a rigorous path, including the patience and support to follow it after the first explanation stops being enough.\nShared research asks something beyond explaining an answer. People need a question they can actually work on together, with room for different contributions and a way to examine what those contributions changed. My own curiosity has moved through geometry, time, learning and the organisation of intelligence. Across those forms, I keep asking what causes what, how I could tell, and what that understanding lets me change. Keeping an ambition alive while becoming less attached to its first explanation has been an important part of that movement.\nPersonal life changes the work as well. A venture that occupies every hour cannot be judged only by the elegance of its product. Relationships, family and the possibility of continuing matter. I have become more interested in what can endure, and in what remains useful when an experiment ends, without pretending that every detour was part of a plan.\nToday I bring that practice to consulting at RawThoughts. Alongside it, I am building Pale so people and agents can continue work across their applications, and Gazette so connected writing, research and documentation can become publications people can explore. Agents decide far more often than people, so by the time one writes, what it read has usually changed; I am building Weave and Scope so the people and agents who share a piece of work can still act on a current picture of it. Causality takes the inquiry into adaptive models and architectures: how small reasoning units might learn to organise, and how a system can change its description of a problem. Post-AGI Economics follows what cheaper machine agency changes in production and ordinary life. The environment I want to work in is part of the construction, not simply a set of tools around it.\nArt, music and cultural reading are not decorations added after the serious work. An image can make a relation visible; a sequence can change what it means; a tradition can resist the interpretation I arrived with. My graphic novel Sleepwalker gives those encounters a world to develop in. Working through different forms lets another kind of question appear. There are questions for which a working system is the right response, and others that need a proof, a conversation or an image. I would rather find the right form than make every curiosity serve a professional label.\nI'm also open to the right role: AI work with real ownership, inside a larger organisation. If that sounds like a conversation worth having, write to me. My résumé is available on request. For a longer conversation in another form, I have spoken on The Naked Product and Strategy of Finance.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/about/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"mentions":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/#entity","identifier":"project:gazette","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"Gazette","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/","identifier":"content:gazette-introduction","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Gazette","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/first-publication/#article","identifier":"content:gazette-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Your first publication","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/first-publication/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/mentions/","identifier":"content:gazette-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Gazette mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/gazette/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/gazette/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/#entity","identifier":"project:pale","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"Pale","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/","identifier":"content:pale-project","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/#article","identifier":"content:pale-docs","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Introduction to Pale","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/understand-your-world/"},{"@id":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/","identifier":"content:pale-mentions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Pale mentions","url":"https://rawthoughts.in/pale/mentions/"},{"@id":"https://docs.rawthoughts.in/pale/overview"}]},{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/#entity","identifier":"project:post-agi-economics","@type":"ResearchProject","name":"Post-AGI Economics","subjectOf":[{"@id":"https://post-agi-economics.org/","identifier":"content:post-agi-economics-home","@type":"WebPage","name":"When intelligence gets cheap","url":"https://post-agi-economics.org/"}]}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/","identifier":"content:getharsh-articles","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Blog","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":15,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/#article","@type":"BlogPosting","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/","identifier":"content:getharsh-compute-economic-question","name":"When the compute question became larger"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","identifier":"content:getharsh-an-analogy-can-start-an-argument","name":"The vocabulary of an unfamiliar world"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/","identifier":"content:getharsh-research-becomes-a-reading-path","name":"How research becomes a reading path"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/","identifier":"content:getharsh-an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model","name":"From a model answer to a useful product"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":5,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/","identifier":"content:getharsh-every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager","name":"Every digital product manager must become an AI product manager"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":6,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/","identifier":"content:getharsh-making-a-product-smaller","name":"When customers need less product"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":7,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-first-decade","name":"Gravity was still acting on the magnet"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":8,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","identifier":"content:getharsh-knowing-understanding-and-realising","name":"Knowing, understanding and realising"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":9,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/","identifier":"content:getharsh-holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it","name":"Holding an idea without becoming it"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":10,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","identifier":"content:getharsh-breath-of-cosmos","name":"Sneak peek into infinity: the breath of cosmos and its duration"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":11,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/#article","@type":"BlogPosting","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/","identifier":"content:getharsh-navigating-the-feedback-loop","name":"Navigating the feedback loop"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":12,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/","identifier":"content:getharsh-vision-strategy-agility-and-culture","name":"Vision, strategy, agility and culture"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":13,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/","identifier":"content:getharsh-teachings-of-the-mirror","name":"Teachings of the mirror"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":14,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/","identifier":"content:getharsh-responsibility-is-not-a-title","name":"Leading beyond the title"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":15,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/","identifier":"content:getharsh-figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time","name":"Figuring things out, one book at a time"}}],"itemListOrder":"https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending"},"description":"Essays that stand on their own, from leadership and product work to comedy, forgiveness and the scale of a breath.","text":"","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model","@type":"Article","name":"From a model answer to a useful product","headline":"From a model answer to a useful product","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["AI product strategy","language-model applications","conversational interfaces","diagnosing AI failures","cost per completed task","confirmation and human review"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/","@type":"WebPage","name":"From a model answer to a useful product","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/"},"description":"Product judgment connects model behaviour to a user's task, the surrounding operation and a way to recover.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-04-07","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"From a model answer to a useful product\nMy earlier encounters with AI came through image processing and embedded systems. The model did its part inside an arrangement of sensors, software and people. It didn't usually offer to explain the arrangement back to me.\nA language model does. Ask it a question and the component suddenly sounds like the person who understands the whole operation. It can interpret a request, explain a bill and recommend a next step. That is an extraordinary entrance to a product. But what happens when we follow the recommendation past the answer? The bill has to be the right one. The proposed correction has to reach a process that can make it. Somebody still has to live with the result.\nThe customer didn't arrive for our demonstration\nIn a demonstration, we can provide the right information, ask an intelligible question and stop at the impressive answer. A customer arrives with a situation. They may have the wrong record, misunderstand the procedure or ask for something the business cannot do. They may be returning after a failed attempt, or need somebody else's approval before acting. Their starting point is part of the product problem.\nConsider a hypothetical small business trying to understand an unexpected charge. A model can explain a bill, but I would first want to decide what kind of help the product is offering. Is it an explanation, a prepared dispute or a change to the account? These are different promises. The prepared dispute might be a useful first product: identify the bill, show the disputed amount and its basis, let the customer correct the draft, then put it into an existing resolution process.\nThat choice changes what we build and measure. A fluent explanation of the wrong bill cannot pass merely because it reads well. A correct draft that never reaches anyone able to resolve it has also left the customer with unfinished work. Before expanding the conversation to every question about the account, I would want the narrow operation to have a recognisable end: the customer can inspect the proposed dispute and knows what happened after submitting it.\nWhat becomes possible when the entrance changes?\nThe new capability can change the process around it. A business that previously required customers to know its internal categories might let them begin with an outcome. A worker might examine a larger range of alternatives because preparing each one costs less. This changes how people ask for help, what work is economical to offer and where human attention is needed, rather than merely making the old screen faster.\nThe move from broadcast to on-demand gives a useful comparison. Distribution technology changed what people could consume and how creators could reach them. A conversational system could likewise change the entrance to a service: the person states an outcome instead of first learning the provider's categories. The product opportunity includes that new relationship. But possibility is not adoption. A useful behaviour has to meet an actual need, and the organisation needs a way to sustain it. A feature that customers try once because it is surprising is different from work they return to because it helps.\nA smaller product around a larger capability\nAt Zenoti, customer evidence led me towards a simpler experience for a small-business segment within a much larger suite. Understanding what the customer needed mattered more than displaying everything the product could do. AI introduces different mechanisms, but a similar product decision: a model may make many responses possible while the valuable operation remains narrow. The user might need to settle one charge, not converse about the whole account. Three consequential choices can be more useful than a seemingly unlimited assistant that cannot complete any of them.\nSupport, onboarding, data migration and handoffs are part of that operation. They determine whether the person can begin, whether the context is coherent and whether an exception has somewhere to go. A conversational front desk still needs those connections behind it; fluent conversation does not remove the work of completing a request.\nWhich part needs to get better?\nA poor result can come from several places: the model misunderstands, retrieval selects the wrong source, a tool returns stale information or the business process is undefined. Treating every failure as a model problem lets us spend more on the component while leaving the cause untouched. Better training can address a recurring capability gap; a clearer source can address a retrieval problem; an explicit rule can resolve a decision the business has already settled. A narrower task or a person's judgment may be the right intervention for an exception. Technical fluency helps product work choose among these mechanisms instead of translating every objection into a demand for a more intelligent model.\nResearch, adaptation and operation have different work to do. Research may explore whether a capability is possible. Adaptation makes a chosen capability fit a task and its examples. Operation has to keep the resulting application usable as sources, users and dependencies change. A roadmap connects these activities through the outcome it seeks, while giving each an appropriate way to learn. Treating them as one uninterrupted process of “the AI getting better” makes both costs and responsibilities harder to see.\nThe economic comparison also has to follow the completed work. A cheaper call can create more retries and support, while a more expensive model can be worthwhile if it reliably removes a costly part of the process. Neither conclusion follows from the price per token alone. Specialised capability, different models within one application and deterministic software each have a place where they improve the work. The model does not need to become everything for the product to become useful.\nWhat am I confirming when I press the button?\nWhen an application suggests a correction to the hypothetical bill, the person needs to know what is being changed and why. A confirmation button without that context only makes the ambiguity clickable. Improving the system presents a related problem: a rating can report dissatisfaction without locating its cause, and a trace can record steps without explaining which statement is justified. In both cases, the interface needs to make the consequential parts available to judgment. That may call for a comparison, an editable draft, a source reference or a handoff, rather than another conversational turn. Reducing effort helps when it leaves the person better able to complete the task; concealing a consequential mistake takes something else away.\nBeyond the impressive answer\nAn AI product brings a capability into an outcome someone wants, at a cost the operation can carry, with a way to recognise and repair the failures that matter. Agents and conversation contribute through the useful work they make possible. Product work, architecture and engineering meet in making that promise specific enough for somebody to try and the result specific enough to learn from. What interests me is the work that becomes possible, the people it helps and the arrangement around the model that makes it real.\nThe broader argument about how this changes product management follows the research, the new user behaviours and the organisation needed to build for them.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-an-analogy-can-start-an-argument","@type":"Article","name":"The vocabulary of an unfamiliar world","headline":"The vocabulary of an unfamiliar world","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["mathematical vocabulary","spherical geometry","changing descriptions","time and dependence","analogy","AI composition"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The vocabulary of an unfamiliar world","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/"]},"description":"A triangle, a clock and an operating-system image open questions that change as I follow them. What must a new vocabulary let us see?","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-25","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The vocabulary of an unfamiliar world\nOkay, imagine joining a conversation where nobody shares your vocabulary. You can point, draw and demonstrate, but even those gestures need some agreement. A circle might mean a place, a person, a boundary or a journey. Where do you begin? Before arguing about the answer, you have to discover what the other person thinks the question is.\nI keep running into a version of that predicament when I think about time. We have clocks, equations and a perfectly serviceable language for getting to a meeting. Then somebody asks what time is, and the meeting suddenly seems easier. What have those words and symbols allowed us to describe? What did we leave outside them?\nA triangle with three right angles\nDraw a triangle on a flat sheet. Its angles add to 180 degrees. Now start at the North Pole, follow a meridian to the equator, travel a quarter of the way around the equator and return along another meridian. The three angles of this spherical triangle are all right angles. The result is 270 degrees. Did the triangle break mathematics?\nAn equilateral triangle on a flat plane, with all three angles marked.\nOn the plane: 60° + 60° + 60° = 180°.\nGreat-circle arcs join the North Pole to two equator points ninety degrees apart; each surface angle is marked as a right angle.\nOn the sphere: 90° + 90° + 90° = 270°.\nThe right angles are measured on the spherical surface. Its projection onto this page changes how those angles look.\nOf course not. We changed the setting and kept speaking as though we hadn't. The familiar statement came with a surface, a meaning of a straight path and a set of assumptions. On the sphere, the sides follow great-circle arcs. The surprise is useful because it makes the setting visible again. What looked like a fact about every possible triangle was a fact about a particular geometry.\nThat does something to my intuition about time. Perhaps a familiar picture has become so comfortable that I no longer notice what it assumes. A line orders events. A clock compares intervals. A dependency diagram shows which event relied on which other event. Waiting for a reply gives me yet another encounter with duration. These are useful descriptions, but they do not all make the same thing visible.\nIt is tempting to jump from the spherical triangle to a different geometry for time. I have felt that temptation. But the triangle has not told us what such a geometry would be, much less what it would predict. It has given us a better question: am I dissatisfied with the physical account, the picture I use to understand it, or the way it meets experience? Those lead to rather different work.\nMy early essay More than a line pushed the image into a deliberately strange thought experiment: suppose time had a fractional dimension and could be described in a non-Euclidean geometry. What would a line through that space look like? I imagined it bending, looping, disappearing and returning before it began. The image led me towards retrocausality, the possibility of an effect preceding its cause, and towards a hunch that time, chaos, entropy and consciousness belonged in a connected inquiry.\nI had not constructed that geometry. Curvature, fractional dimension and causal ordering name different properties; choosing either of the first two does not establish the third. That is where the mathematical work has to begin: define the space, the dimension being used, the relation between events and what “before” means within the construction. A second, a minute and an hour give me units for comparing duration. They do not, by themselves, settle which of those questions I meant by “what does time look like?” I still want to follow the hunch far enough that its assumptions can meet a real test.\nWhen the picture becomes the explanation\nMathematics gives us an extraordinary vocabulary. A derivative makes a rate of change something we can work with; a coordinate system lets us describe a position; a transformation lets us ask what survives a change of description. Learning the notation is not quite the same as learning to see through it. A beautifully written equation can remain as opaque as that conversation in an unfamiliar language.\nI am interested in the point where a representation becomes something we can think with. Could a better description of dependence make an otherwise confusing process intelligible? Could it expose that two accounts agree only because we have forgotten how their variables were measured? Those are questions about the work a representation does, before any grand claim about discovering a new law.\nMy questions have become more particular as I have followed them. The early image asked whether a different vocabulary could make a difficult world intelligible. The research question now asks what a description lets me distinguish, what a change of setting does to those distinctions and how I can recognise that its language has become too small. Changing the description itself is a larger ambition than improving an estimate within it. A perfectly accurate answer can still concern the wrong-sized question.\nTake two exchanges that overlap in time. A timestamp tells me when each request and reply occurred. It may leave out which reply answered which request, whether the answer resolved the question, and which later decision relied on it. A different picture can restore those relations without changing the physical intervals. If I then ask what would remain valid after a reply were corrected, the picture has another job: it has to preserve the dependencies through which the correction matters. The useful vocabulary changes as the work demands more of it.\nThat is the progression I want to keep following. An image suggests a question; a construction makes one part of it precise; a counterexample shows where the construction stops helping. I do not want to turn an awkward question into a polite instruction to stay within familiar categories, or ask the first metaphor to defend every explanation I attach to it. A different formalism would need to let us do something we could not do as well before. The reach of the image can survive the work of finding out what that something is.\nA lifetime in a breath\nNow change the observer. Imagine a lifetime passing in a breath on another scale. From inside that life, there were years of waiting, work, love and loss. From outside, barely a moment. The image unsettles me because nothing about the smaller world feels small to the person living there.\nThat is part of what draws me towards Markandeya recognising his own place within an impossible frame. A story can let the reader inhabit the shift. An equation would need to define its quantities and their relationship; the scene can stay with what the change of scale does to a person's sense of a life. It has a different kind of precision: which detail remains recognisable when the world around it becomes impossible?\nI want both forms available. Sometimes the next step is to calculate. Sometimes it is to sit with an image until I understand why it will not leave me alone.\nAn operating system for intelligence?\nThe same habit follows me into product work. What if intelligence could be composed the way we compose software? The operating-system image opens a whole vocabulary: capabilities, interfaces, memory, applications. Suddenly there are places to ask where a useful piece belongs and how something else could use it.\nSome of that has an ordinary software answer. Retain the result of a step, expose an interface and resume when the missing information arrives. That was part of the work of composing agents and playbooks at DAO Studio. Another part of the ambition reaches inside a learned model: can we find a capacity, separate it and use it elsewhere? A callback does not answer that question. We would have to understand what the capacity depends on and what changes when we move it.\nStill, I would rather follow the discomfort than discard the image. Perhaps the useful abstraction is an application interface. Perhaps the capacity we hoped to compose depends on an exchange the interface never described. The comparison has helped me find both questions, provided I let the unfamiliar work change the familiar name's meaning.\nThat is what interests me about vocabulary. It can give a thought somewhere to begin, and then quietly become the wall it cannot get past. The answer is not always another word. Sometimes we need a different picture, an experiment or a story. Sometimes we need to return to the conversation and admit that we have been answering different questions all along.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/triangle-plane.svg","description":"An equilateral triangle on a flat plane, with all three angles marked.","caption":"On the plane: 60° + 60° + 60° = 180°.\n"},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/triangle-sphere.svg","description":"Great-circle arcs join the North Pole to two equator points ninety degrees apart; each surface angle is marked as a right angle.","caption":"On the sphere: 90° + 90° + 90° = 270°.\n"}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-breath-of-cosmos","@type":"Article","name":"Sneak peek into infinity: the breath of cosmos and its duration","headline":"Sneak peek into infinity: the breath of cosmos and its duration","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["Breath of Cosmos","pranayama","breath and consciousness","cosmic time","infinity and limits","Vishnu","control-system analogy"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Sneak peek into infinity: the breath of cosmos and its duration","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/","https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/","https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/"]},"description":"A breath opens a thought experiment in control systems, cosmic time and unbounded ratios, leading to the possibility of collective consciousness.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-01-11","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Sneak peek into infinity: the breath of cosmos and its duration\nIn the quiet moments of deep breathing, have you ever felt a subtle shift in your perception of time and reality? This experience is where I want to begin an inquiry into consciousness, reaching beyond the scale of an individual life.\nIt begins with a breath.\nIn our daily lives, breathing is an automatic function, a simple necessity of life. But when we attend to it, especially through practices such as pranayama, something ordinarily unnoticed becomes available to us. We can change the rhythm and notice how the encounter changes. It is not only meditative mumbo-jumbo: breathing practices are also used to help bring about a relaxation response. What interests me here is the connection between a physical process, our state of mind and the way time is experienced.\nGive the breath an input\nAn engineer reaches for a model. Perhaps the duration and quality of a breath can be treated as a control signal, with a transfer function representing the process through which breathing influences consciousness. Something like\n\nY(t)=T\\cdot X(t),\n\nwhere X(t) represents the breathing pattern, Y(t) the consciousness level we would like to describe, and T the relation between them. The simplest version makes T=k, a positive constant: change the input and the output changes proportionally.\nSomeone with a neuroscience background could model the mechanics better than I can. I clearly do not have that background. The mind is non-linear and considerably more complicated than this constant gain; even the quantities would need research before we could measure the relationship. For now, the sketch gives me a way to ask how a physical breathing pattern might influence consciousness.\nIf you are particularly curious about the time variable, much like I am, the question soon becomes larger than this little equation. How do we describe all the different ways an interval can be encountered? What might a vocabulary for that question let us see? That curiosity also runs through my questions about geometry and description.\nBut you get the general idea of what I am trying to communicate here, right? I digress. Coming back: a change in breathing is an ordinary physical act through which we can examine a change in experience. I want to follow that connection into a much less ordinary scale.\nIt expands to cosmic scales\nBegin with the human scale: each breath, each moment. Seconds become minutes, hours become days, and years open into yugas, mahayugas, manvantaras and kalpas. These names belong to a larger account of recurrence, eventually taking us to the life of Brahmā and the world associated with it.\nNow meet the image of Mahā-Vishnu's breath. In the Brahma-saṁhitā, the lives of the Brahmās presiding over universes fall within one exhalation. A universe's entire history becomes part of another rhythm. Its creation and dissolution fit within a breath; another breath opens the possibility again.\nTale, wisdom, fiction, interpretation: whatever your angle, the image asks something rather startling of our attention. One breath contains a lifetime of worlds.\nWhere, in this grand scale of breath, are you? Let us choose seven seconds for a human breath. It is an arbitrary interval for the thought experiment. How many such intervals belong within the cosmic breath? What fraction of the larger duration does your breath occupy?\nFor any specified finite duration, we can divide by seven seconds and obtain a finite count. That is one calculation. The thought I want to follow goes further: take the cosmic scale as unbounded. There is no largest duration in the comparison I am making. The names can be variables for you; the question is the relation between an interval we can hold and a scale without a final bound.\nHow many zeroes are contained in an infinity?\nHow many twos are there in ten? Five. How many ones? Ten.\n\n\\frac{10}{2}=5,\\qquad \\frac{10}{1}=10.\n\nNow ask the deliberately awkward question: how many zeroes are there in an infinity?\nYour intuition probably says infinitely many. Trust your intuition, then look at what we have actually put into the fraction. The human breath was never zero. We chose seven seconds. It only began to look like zero when placed beside an ever-larger duration. And infinity is not a very large real number waiting to be divided; it names the absence of a bound.\nWrite B for the duration being compared, measured in seconds. The number of seven-second intervals is B/7. As the duration grows without bound,\n\n\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\frac{B}{7}=\\infty.\n\nLook in the other direction and the fraction represented by one human breath tends to zero:\n\n\\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\frac{7}{B}=0.\n\nIt is no longer an indeterminate form. The denominator is finite and nonzero; the duration in the numerator grows without bound. The answer in this construction is infinity.\nWhat would that mean?\nWith breath and time as proxies for consciousness in this thought experiment, I picture consciousness expanding towards infinity as the imagined breath extends towards the cosmic scale. Looking back towards the human scale, our individual awareness becomes a near-zero part of that expanse. The ratio gives me the scale; what follows is the possibility I see in it.\nWithin that picture, cosmic consciousness can be a superset of individual experiences: all forms of life, all the different encounters through which existence becomes known. Our awareness is a drop in that ocean, but a drop belongs to the ocean. Being small in the comparison has not placed it outside the whole.\nYou know the monkey typing on a keyboard for long enough to produce Hamlet? That is the image I reach for: an immense space of possibilities in which something as particular as a play can occur. A cosmic or collective consciousness could contain the individual forms through which we encounter it. The superset, or universal set, if you will.\nBut calculating the ratio and experiencing that connection are different things. Knowing, understanding and realising can take us through the same words and leave us somewhere quite different.\nMy early generative-art explorations for Sleepwalker gave this question another form. An image of Markandeya could hold the encounter with another scale while I worked through the calculation separately. It helps me separate the myth from the maths. The creative inquiry that grows from the breath has room for a witness, a world and the bewilderment that a ratio cannot draw for us.\nThe universe in our breath\nThe attraction of this idea is not only the size of the number. It is the possibility of meeting something immense through an act so ordinary that I usually do not notice it. The traditions of breath and meditation invite that encounter from experience; the mathematical construction gives one relation within it a form we can examine.\nI see in it a connection between individual awareness and an unbounded possibility of consciousness. A small experience can remain intimate while belonging to something beyond the frame in which I first understood it. The cosmic scale does not make the breath disappear. It changes what I can imagine the breath participating in.\nSo, take a deep breath and remember: it begins with a breath.\nThe US National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health describes breathing exercises among relaxation techniques. That evidence concerns particular bodily and psychological effects; it does not supply a scale of consciousness for this thought experiment.\nBrahma-saṁhitā 5.48, in the Bhaktivedanta edition. This is the textual image behind the comparison. Treating the cosmic duration as unbounded is the thought experiment's additional premise, not a value measured from the verse.\nThe probability argument requires the desired sequence to keep having a chance to occur, with appropriate assumptions about the repeated trials. Duration alone does not establish that every experience occurs; the monkey is an analogy for the possibility considered here.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence","@type":"Article","name":"Debauchery, in the name of existence!","headline":"Debauchery, in the name of existence!","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["problem solving","compassion","cycles","science and tradition","consequences"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Debauchery, in the name of existence!","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/the-future-we-create-and-condemn/"]},"description":"A year-end provocation about solving problems, transferring their consequences and making room for compassion and unfamiliar ways of knowing.","datePublished":{"@value":"2019-12-29","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Debauchery, in the name of existence!\nA provocation from the end of 2019.\nHistory. Sound. Ideas. What do they have in common? I kept seeing cycles: peace and violence, crests and troughs, a return that our confident accounts of progress had not prepared us to recognise. Periodicity seemed almost a property of existence itself. Life was a montage of the ripples that existence left behind, and we were rarely still enough to notice what repeated.\nIt remained important to strive for peace. Oblivious to the ripples, the cycle continued.\nAre we solving the problem?\nWe take pride in calling ourselves problem solvers, innovators and explorers. But if a wave washes the sand from beneath my feet while I watch the sunset, does witnessing the spectacle make me an explorer?\nWhy ask such an absurd question? Meh. No reason. Except that I wanted to challenge the flattering premise beneath those names.\nWhat if much of what we called solving a problem was a transformation of its presentation? We took an unpleasant present and moved it into a less familiar, more enticing future. The future inherited the consequences, while the present celebrated the ingenuity of the translation.\nThat was not necessarily easy work. It could require extraordinary engineering, organisation and imagination. The objection was that being difficult did not settle whether it had solved the problem. A civilisation could become very accomplished at changing the form in which its difficulties arrived.\nBusinesspeople, engineers, doctors, lawyers, teachers, scientists and statespeople all participated. We divided the planet with fences, colours, weapons and beliefs, then kept fighting the wars of people no longer alive. Generations of martyrs raised further generations. When trust became scarce, cooperation looked increasingly like another game. Somehow, like cockroaches, we survived.\nThe uncomfortable possibility of being wrong\nI thought of Phoebe challenging Ross in Friends: could he leave even a little room for the possibility that he was wrong? The appeal was in the challenge to his certainty. A sitcom exchange is a poor history of science, but it can be an excellent mirror for intellectual vanity.\nArtists seemed more willing than many of us to admit that they did not know what the hell they were doing. An artist could stay inside a question without first dressing it as a result. I found that courage valuable.\nEnlarge the window beyond one lifetime and our professional confidence can begin to look peculiar. We understand some intricate details and still struggle with the elementary questions. What is consciousness? Do the needs of the many outweigh those of the few? What is the nature of existence? We move between quantities, convictions and competing ways of giving the question meaning.\nI was frustrated by how quickly that difficulty could become a reason to stop listening. Citations could be treated as a substitute for understanding the claim they supported. A reference to a Vedic practice could be dismissed before anyone had asked what kind of account it offered.\nWhich vocabulary gets to enter the conversation?\nConsider the five koshas, or sheaths, described in Vedantic accounts of a person. Or the yogic vocabulary of ida, pingala and sushumna. These names do not become anatomical structures simply because someone draws a parallel with nerves or glands. Yet I wanted room to ask what the descriptions were doing before deciding that their failure to fit an anatomical vocabulary made them worthless.\nMy provocation was that science, too, was another belief system, albeit one in which consistency and elegance were paramount. I wanted to challenge the confidence with which we could dismiss an unfamiliar account while treating our own frame as the only one worth inhabiting. The comparison did not establish that every inherited claim was true. If we could be sceptical of an unfamiliar claim, surely we could also examine the certainty with which we had decided what was worth hearing.\nThe question of vocabulary irritated me: how could this conversation recognise its own assumptions if its words already settled what could count as an answer?\nThe thing we let loose\nWe create technologies and ideologies, then release them into a world whose responses we cannot fully anticipate. Greed can outrun compassion. Confidence in the evidence we possess can outrun our understanding of what will happen once other people begin using the creation.\nShould we create something knowing that it could be used for great evil? The question deserves more than a template answer. A calculation can illuminate a consequence, but somebody still chooses what belongs in the calculation. A belief can sustain a commitment, but somebody still lives with what that commitment permits.\nWe no longer have to stone a philosopher to make a difficult question unwelcome. There are quieter ways to keep it out of the room. Oblivious to the ripples, the cycle continues.\nThe silence between the vibrations\nWe seek symmetry in history, sound and ideas. I was interested in the stretches of silence between the vibrations: what the impression of continuity made us overlook. A coherent story could offer a sense of knowledge and control while hiding how much we had simply carried forward into another form.\nThat was my worry about innovation. Beyond the question of securing the next investment, what had we understood about the problem's survival? Had we changed the conditions producing it, or transferred the burden to a person or generation outside the frame?\nA game organised around survival does not automatically give us exuberance. Yet we keep rolling the dice, enjoying the next little rush, while the possibility of losing remains somebody else's difficulty. Debauchery, in the name of existence.\nAll that anger came back to a modest wish: more compassion, and a more peaceful new year. Solving a problem ought to include the lives that continue after our account of the solution ends. I wanted us to look at those lives before celebrating how cleverly we had moved the problem.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-dumb-comedy","@type":"Article","name":"Dumb comedy: existentialism for the twenty-first century","headline":"Dumb comedy: existentialism for the twenty-first century","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["sitcoms","dumb comedy","existentialism","education","art","Phil Dunphy"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Dumb comedy: existentialism for the twenty-first century","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/","https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/"]},"description":"Why sitcoms, their awkward heroes and the small pleasure of a joke gave me a way back into larger questions at the end of 2019.","datePublished":{"@value":"2019-12-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Dumb comedy: existentialism for the twenty-first century\nWritten at the end of 2019, with a new decade approaching.\nYup. That is exactly what it sounds like. I root for Ross. I root for JD. I root for Marshall, Leonard and Phil. Different people in different sitcoms, yet there is something familiar about their predicament: the outlier, the geek, the person everybody is comfortable laughing at. The adorable ones.\nTheir plight makes me think about education and its place in society. Its fall feels like our biggest unpatched bug. We leave room for a character to be foolish, persistent and oddly perceptive on television. Around our water coolers and balconies, we are less patient with somebody whose question does not fit the conversation. We discuss proposals, law, ethics, experience and philosophy as though the boundaries between them were obvious. Meanwhile, the supposedly dumb ones keep trying to clear the haze for whoever comes next.\nWhy am I talking about this? Because it may be one of the few things on which I can agree with a lot of people. Stretch most other conversations over a decade and they start to resemble horror stories.\nNew year, same difficult whys\nThe point of “New Year, New Me,” surely, is to look at what we have created before deciding what we will create next. Get your priorities straight, as the old man would put it. Have your whys in order.\nThis little nugget comes from a Certified Scrum Master. If you have no idea what that means, you lucky bastard. In a nutshell: consensus is tricky. 😉\nOne of my largest lessons of the decade was that a teacher need not have the form I expected. There are sayings about the student becoming ready and the teacher appearing. A sitcom could supply a small version of that encounter. It could show me something I had become too serious to notice in myself.\nAnd if you don’t believe the horror-story bit, look around at what is happening to democracies. Not just between people and governments, but between corporations and governments, and corporations and people. Don’t even get me started on the last one. I agreed with Socrates’ criticism of democracy: he had good reasons to distrust our collective judgement. That unpatched bug in education kept making the old fellow look right.\nWho makes the monsters?\nBut there is a ray of hope too. The war on ego and ignorance is on all fronts; the hardest is often the one I have with myself. Conflict can produce art. Artists exploring this existence are leading the change, reaching millions in ways more profound than institutional policy. People are finding art in all sorts of things.\nWe have been so busy looking for monsters and heroes in our electronics-powered algebraic intelligence (God damn Electricity as a Service) that we forget how we create both every day. It is a privilege to remain insulated from what people do to other people and their data, then find worse monsters and better heroes in a world made from pixels. We pay for that world every month. The same day, the same time. Exercising our privilege when it matters is a choice too.\nThe privilege is not itself the whole moral question. What do I do with it? When does it let me avoid something I should face, and when does it give me enough room to continue?\nIsn’t that what war comes down to: the ruthless calculus of survival and submission? We work to feed ourselves and our families, to have an occasional drink and a little room for pleasure. Then comes the old question: who saves the hero?\nEnjoy the little things\nPerhaps this is where the memes help. Zombieland gives me rule 32: “Enjoy the little things.” I keep returning to it.\nDumb comedy is not necessarily easy. Sometimes it performs the hardest job: giving a person a small reprieve without demanding that they solve existence first. A joke lets me live and fight another day for whatever the larger work was about. It need not pretend that the work has gone away.\nHistory, I kept thinking, is written by the victors. What we do tomorrow will be part of it the day after. A little reprieve lets us return to that struggle instead of being swallowed by it. You can be a monster or a hero, both or neither. Just don’t be an asshole to the ones who choose differently, and we can sit back to watch this little documentary play out a decade from now.\nSome rules are made of glass, not rubber. Be careful when you break them. A reminder to enjoy a small thing is one I would rather keep.\nWhat would Phil Dunphy do?\nFor me, entertainment is supposed to entertain. Sometimes I want it to take me away from the reality I took a break from, rather than return that reality in a more elaborate costume. The heist of a heist of a heist can wait.\nWhen I feel the familiar anxiety gathering, I take a breath and ask myself what Phil Dunphy would do. I learn from the master, then return to the what and the how.\nPro tip from the holiday version of me: leave the whys to the holidays. And remember, nothing good ever happens after 2 a.m. If you have a PhD in sitcoms, be kind and pass the references along.\nI had finally got my hands on Jean Baudrillard's Simulacra and Simulation. I planned to go through it over the holidays, hoping to clear some of the haze. Also, what’s your take on Rick and Morty and existentialism?\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager","@type":"Article","name":"Every digital product manager must become an AI product manager","headline":"Every digital product manager must become an AI product manager","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["AI product management","consumer behaviour","technical fluency","product research","personalisation","human review"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Every digital product manager must become an AI product manager","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/","https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/","https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/"]},"description":"Consumer empathy, technical fluency and research must meet when AI changes how people use a product and how a business delivers it.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-09-13","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Every digital product manager must become an AI product manager\nThe argument began in September 2024 as the second part of a reflection on building products. Its predictions describe the direction I was arguing for then; the model examples belong to that period.\nA product manager who understands the customer but cannot follow what the technology makes possible is increasingly working with half the picture. The reverse is just as limiting. Knowing a model's architecture does not tell us why somebody would return to the product tomorrow.\nMy argument is that every digital product manager now needs to become an AI product manager. Not because every product needs a chatbot, but because AI can change the behaviour the product serves, the work behind it and the business that sustains it. Treating the subject as a feature that another team can add later leaves those choices to chance.\nFrom flipping pages to scrolling feeds\nRemember when the programme schedule told us when to watch, and television ratings helped decide which programmes could continue? Compare that with opening YouTube or Netflix and finding a selection arranged for you. The viewer's choices, the distribution system and the business's measures have all moved.\nThe newspaper-to-feed transition makes the change almost comically physical. We moved from turning pages to scrolling down a screen. A thumb's new habit rested on touchscreens, networks, ranking systems, recommendation research and years of adoption. It was not just a designer choosing a different direction for the same page.\nPersonalised feeds made relevance and engagement part of a continuing interaction. Product work had to translate frustrations with static or irrelevant content into questions that engineers and researchers could address. Which content should appear, in what order, for whom, and what would count as a better result?\nThe shift was visible in use. In a survey conducted in 2020 and published in January 2021, Pew Research Center found that 86% of US adults got news from digital devices at least sometimes, including 60% who did so often. Those are distinct measures, not a single interchangeable adoption percentage.\nStreaming had grown too, without already becoming all television. Nielsen's August 2020 account put streaming at a quarter of television viewing in OTT-capable homes; Netflix and YouTube accounted for 34% and 20% of that streaming time. The denominator matters. The change in the product opportunity does not need an inflated account of how complete the transition was.\nThe research changes what can be built\nMy own route through AI began closer to image processing and embedded systems than to a conversational window. Looking across the field, the sequence from LeNet's digit recognition to AlexNet, BERT and large language models shows how research changes the kinds of product questions we can ask.\nAlexNet's 2012 paper described a convolutional network with 60 million parameters and an efficient GPU implementation. BERT, introduced in 2018, made bidirectional language representations useful across tasks such as question answering and language inference. GPT-3 in 2020 explored a 175-billion-parameter model whose tasks could be specified through instructions and examples in text. The first LLaMA family in 2023 ranged from 7 billion to 65 billion parameters and made a different scale-and-efficiency comparison possible.\nThose are changes in architecture, training, data and use, not a league table in which more parameters automatically means a better product. By September 2024, the arrival of reasoning-oriented models was pushing my expectations further. I saw an expanding space of tasks that software might handle. A result on a particular benchmark still cannot establish that a model reasons better than a human at everything, or that its result can be trusted in the workflow we are building.\nThe product manager needs enough understanding to ask where the advance matters. Can the user begin with an intention instead of a category? Can a worker examine alternatives that were previously too expensive to prepare? Does a smaller specialised model make a useful operation economical? A technical change becomes a product opportunity when we can connect it to somebody's work.\nThe iPhone was a product argument\nThe iPhone is useful to me as an example of empathy meeting technical construction. The 2007 introduction brought phone, media and internet functions together around a large multi-touch interface. It changed what the screen and software could ask of the user's fingers. The opportunity was larger than making a physical keyboard slightly better.\nThat does not require pretending Apple invented every touchscreen or that the entire ecosystem appeared on the first day. The App Store followed in 2008. Hardware, software, developer tools and distribution became parts of a larger arrangement through which other people could build.\nWhat interests me is the work concealed by the experience. Research, interface design, patents and engineering have to meet before a complicated system can feel natural. The customer does not need a tour of each component to benefit from it. Product judgement has to understand enough of those components to make the natural experience possible.\nThe same thought appears in Nike's appeal to the person becoming an athlete. The material and construction matter because of the experience they enable. The product does not become meaningful merely by reciting its technical properties.\nWhat new habits might AI make possible?\nPersonalisation is one direction. In its 2018 survey, Accenture reported that 91% of respondents were more likely to shop with brands that recognised them and supplied relevant recommendations. That is a reported preference in a particular survey, not a guarantee that every attempt at prediction will be welcomed.\nThe ambition I was describing goes beyond ordering a familiar catalogue. A service might anticipate the next useful step, prepare a relevant option or carry the context of an unfinished task across devices. Imagine beginning on a phone and continuing in a car or at home without reconstructing the intention each time. That is a product hypothesis about continuity; making it useful also requires the person to understand and control what is carried across those settings.\nAnother direction is less visible technology. Think of a service such as Swiggy meeting the operating system through an action or notification instead of requiring every interaction to begin with a tour of its app. AI could make more capabilities available where the need arises. The further possibility is an “intelligence store”: capabilities that other products can call and compose, rather than a separate app for every narrow interaction.\nThese possibilities ask product managers to guide research towards behaviour worth enabling. Seamlessness is not a substitute for that judgement. The fact that two systems can connect does not establish that a person wants them connected, or that the resulting service helps.\nThink through the system, not only the feature\nIn an enterprise product, a model may connect processes that already have their own rules, records and users. It could help interpret a request, prepare an analysis or move information between teams. The apparent intelligence sits inside a system that still needs consistent identities, compatible versions, current data and a way to handle exceptions.\nThis is why I want product managers to practise systems thinking. The engineering remains engineering; a product manager does not become its substitute. But a roadmap needs to recognise dependencies instead of treating each visible feature as an independent promise.\nA framework such as the V-model offers one useful habit: connect a requirement to how it will be checked, from the whole system down to the parts. For an AI-assisted operation, that means deciding what the user must be able to complete and what evidence would show that the model and surrounding software support it.\nThe teams also need a shared vocabulary. We already translate a business concern such as daily active users into engineering questions about traffic, capacity and latency. Model performance needs a similarly explicit relationship to product objectives. Accuracy on which cases? A response time measured from which event? An improvement for which customer task? If research, engineering and the business each mean something different by “better,” the roadmap can advance while the product stands still.\nHuman review must have something to review\nControl belongs in the experience. A person may need to inspect a recommendation, correct an assumption, stop an action or recover from a bad result. Validation and rollback cannot remain reassuring words attached after the main flow has been designed.\nNor does a model's fluent explanation necessarily reveal how it reached the answer. Useful transparency gives the reader the evidence, assumptions and proposed consequence they can actually examine. In the bill-dispute example, a person needs to see which bill, which amount and why a correction is proposed. A generic confirmation button asks for trust without providing the basis for it.\nThe appropriate review depends on the decision. A suggestion and an irreversible change need different controls. Product management has to make that distinction part of the operation, working with the people responsible for its risks and recovery.\nA roadmap has to outlive the demonstration\nThe first useful version should leave room for the system to grow. That means understanding which capabilities can be composed, which assumptions are tied to one model and what later integrations would need. The App Store analogy matters here because the larger opportunity came from an environment in which other products could operate.\nIt does not mean predicting every future model. A modular design gives us places to learn and replace something without pretending the surrounding workflow has no dependencies. We still need to know what a change does to the user's experience, cost and ability to recover.\nFeedback supplies one route to improvement. Reinforcement learning from human feedback is one technique; collecting reactions is not itself a training process. The product has to make the feedback interpretable, decide what it should influence, and compare a proposed change before releasing it. A complaint could point to a model weakness, a wrong source, a confusing interface or a business rule the user dislikes. Training on all four as though they were the same problem would hide the work we needed to do.\nThis is the move towards research management that I meant. Product managers need to help researchers choose questions tied to real customer difficulties, while keeping room for work whose value is not yet a feature on next month's plan. The conversation goes both ways: research can reveal a possibility the original roadmap did not know to ask for.\nThe business result is part of the product\nRetention, efficiency and operating cost give the technical work an economic purpose. In their 2015 account of Netflix's recommender system, Carlos Gomez-Uribe and Neil Hunt estimated that personalisation and recommendations together saved more than a billion dollars a year, through effects including reduced churn. That was their company estimate for the combined system, not the value of one algorithm or a return another business can assume.\nThe relationship is the useful lesson. A recommendation matters because it helps a member find something worth watching and return to the service. A developer tool matters because it helps the developer complete work. A cheaper model call is not an operating saving if it creates enough retries and support to outweigh it.\nConsumer empathy and technical fluency meet at that level. One helps us understand the work somebody values. The other helps us shape a system that can deliver it. Research expands what might be possible; engineering and the business have to make the result usable and sustainable.\nThat is why I do not think an “AI-powered” label is an adequate ambition for a product manager. The work is to help shape new behaviour, connect the capability to a real outcome and keep the technology from becoming the customer's burden. Don't be a generic “AI-powered” product manager. ;)\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time","@type":"Article","name":"Figuring things out, one book at a time","headline":"Figuring things out, one book at a time","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["forgiveness","attachment and identity","acceptance","friendship"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Figuring things out, one book at a time","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/","https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/"]},"description":"A February 2020 reflection on forgiveness, attachment, friendship and taking time to find the next part of a life.","datePublished":{"@value":"2020-02-03","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Figuring things out, one book at a time\nFrom February 2020: a pause, a new place and a great many unread books.\nIn hindsight, there is no other way but to be okay. Some experiences define you; some refine you. All of them teach you something, even when you would rather have received the lesson in another form.\nI kept returning to the idea of choosing love over hatred. It led me towards forgiveness, and towards a question I had not dealt with as patiently as I imagined: what about forgiving myself before trying to forgive what had wronged me?\nI had only skimmed the Heart Sutra. I was reaching for a connection, not claiming to have understood the text. There was still a book to read, a life to examine and a considerable distance between recognising an appealing sentence and knowing what to do with it.\nWhen the remedy becomes another injury\nHow far can I go to fix something that seems broken? Until the remedy becomes worse than the disease? Until the broken thing consumes the person trying to repair it?\nStraightforward questions, slippery ground. Before deciding how far to go, I had to ask why I felt compelled to fix it. Why this attachment? Why this insistence on deciding that something was broken, and that I was the person who needed to make it whole?\nThe answer I could reach was a willingness to conform to what I loved: a person, a relationship, an idea or the self I wanted to maintain. Love could have many forms. It could be directed towards a partner, a teacher, parents, friends or family; it need not have a person or even a form as its object. What I called true love asked for something less possessive than making the object agree with my idea of it.\nThat was easy to write. Living it was another matter.\nAcceptance and forgiveness\nStraightforward is often mistaken for easy. The two are not reliably correlated, let alone causally joined.\nI wanted to get beyond the exhausting court in my head: right and wrong, good and evil, fair and unfair. Yet I could not simply float above it. First I had to distinguish accepting what had happened from forgiving it, and both from approving it. A fact could be undeniable while my relationship to it remained unsettled.\nI found attachment to identity at the centre of that difficulty. I worked hard to maintain an account of myself. Every wound then threatened more than the immediate relationship; it threatened the account. Explaining myself again could become another attempt to make the other person preserve it for me.\nThere was a difference between closing my heart to stop the pain and forgiving so that the pain could fade. Neither arrived because I had found a sufficiently neat paragraph about it. I was trying to understand what I could release without pretending that the experience had meant nothing.\nPeople remember how an encounter felt as well as what was said. Another explanation is not always another kindness. That was a difficult lesson for somebody who kept reaching for words.\nThe choices inside the grand explanation\nWhy bring trouble upon ourselves when life is already complicated? Why press the self-destruct button? A television monologue can make the question funny, or at least recognisable: we keep doing the thing that hurts, then celebrate the relief when it stops.\nThe image I kept from The Matrix was Morpheus showing Neo a path he could not walk for him. Yes, another reference. But it got at something I needed: somebody could offer an insight, and I could still have to choose what to do with it.\nAdd self-obsession, a little narcissism and a healthy dose of the God complex, then look again. The grand account of existence dissolves into small choices. What do I do next? Which response do I repeat? What do I let another person be without requiring them to support my explanation of myself?\nThose easily overlooked choices mattered to the pursuit of happiness more than the size of the theory I built around them.\nThe family I discovered\nI was grateful for the friends and family who had stayed beside me. I had not thought very hard about friendship before. I assumed it was mostly a matter of choice. Looking back, it felt more like something we had discovered over time: neither person could simply appoint the other to the role and make it true.\nLiving away from our families, I had not noticed exactly when friends became family. I was lucky. And, as a sitcom mother would remind us, families are embarrassing. If they never embarrass you, have they really completed the qualification?\nAt that moment in 2020, I was taking time off to work out the next part of my journey. A new place, the same family and extraordinary friends. The books, at least, were in abundant supply.\nSo I decided it was time to forgive, and to keep figuring things out one book at a time. The later question of detachment would take another form. Here, it began much closer to home: what could I stop demanding from a hurt, so that the rest of life could continue?\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-compute-economic-question","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"When the compute question became larger","headline":"When the compute question became larger","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["compute economics","AI infrastructure","GPU pricing","economic complements","Post-AGI Economics","One Yardstick"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When the compute question became larger","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/"]},"description":"Following useful AI capacity from a workload into the organisations and economic arrangements around it.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-29","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When the compute question became larger\nI began looking at AI infrastructure partly through the market: what was being built, where the constraints were, and what an investment in that change might really be exposed to. The question became harder to hold at that distance. To understand the market, I needed to understand the work the machines could complete. To understand that work, I had to look beyond the machines again.\nAn hourly GPU price is an appealing place to begin because it looks comparable. But a team does not want an inexpensive hour in isolation. It wants enough suitable capacity, at the right time, to carry a workload to a useful result. The memory, network, schedule and recovery path all become part of what it buys. The completed job is a different economic object from the advertised hour.\nThat was the first change in my own inquiry. I had started by looking for an investment account of the AI-infrastructure market. Following procurement forced me to change the unit I was comparing. A reservation and an elastic allocation could quote the same kind of hour while asking the buyer to carry different kinds of risk. Availability, a deadline and the ability to recover unfinished work could reverse the apparent bargain. I became more interested in understanding that reversal than in producing another list of attractive providers.\nThe machine sits inside a process\nConsider an illustrative team preparing a model evaluation for Friday. Capacity available on Saturday is not equivalent to capacity ready on Thursday, even if it is cheaper and faster once it starts. A wait changes the sequence of experiments, the time colleagues can use the result and the work that follows. Buying compute begins to look less like buying a commodity and more like arranging a process.\nInference adds a different pressure. A person waiting for an answer cares about how the service behaves under arriving demand. The same total throughput can accompany very different waiting times. The service around generation changes which capacity is useful, how much must be held ready and which apparent optimisation simply moves the cost to someone else.\nThese questions made engineering and economics harder to separate. A better execution arrangement can change the cost of an experiment. A better purchasing arrangement can make an otherwise impossible experiment available. Neither tells us whether the resulting capability will be adopted, who can afford it or how an organisation will change around it.\nA cheap capability can meet an expensive complement\nSuppose, as a thought experiment, that producing a competent document analysis becomes almost free. Its use can still depend on access to the document, a person able to judge an exception, a customer willing to change a process and an institution that accepts the result. The scarce part may have moved rather than disappeared.\nAt national scale, power, cooling, connectivity, access and the ability to turn output into productive work become harder to leave outside the account. That is the tension behind my India infrastructure inquiry. An announcement can describe an input while leaving the arrangement that makes it useful unresolved. Following the arrangement led me into questions about who captures value, which dependencies endure and what an expanding capability actually changes.\nThis is where the subject widened into organisations and their complements. Lowering the cost of one task can change the division of work. It can also leave the existing organisation largely intact because another constraint still matters more. I became interested in telling those situations apart before declaring a new era on the strength of the component.\nThe infrastructure collection I was exploring did not stay one collection. Some questions became technical writing, others remained procurement and policy questions, and the larger economic ones grew into Post-AGI Economics. What had begun as a way to understand a market became a way to question the account of growth itself. I no longer wanted the engineering to be background colour for an investment thesis. I wanted to know which economic conclusion the engineering could actually support.\nA transition has more than one clock\nThe attraction of a single measure is real. A clock would offer a way to locate ourselves inside a change that otherwise feels too large to see. One Yardstick grew around that ambition. Working through the question made the differences between frontiers more important: a model can gain a capability before a firm has access, and a firm can gain access before it can rely on the capability in a valuable workflow.\nThe time between those frontiers depends on what changes next. An improved model, a price change or a different institutional arrangement can alter the path itself. A clock has to account for those different transitions to make the larger change easier to examine. Which measures can do that remains part of the inquiry.\nThe economic consequence also depends on who owns the relevant assets and how income moves. Increased productive capacity is one part of that account; the ability to buy, access or share what it produces is another. Productivity and purchasing power meet in a real economy, but they do not become the same quantity because the technology connecting them is impressive.\nThe inquiry has taken me further from selecting winners and closer to understanding which question a model can answer. Sometimes a changing parameter is enough. Sometimes a different constraint becomes binding. Sometimes the actors or arrangements have changed so much that the old description leaves something essential out. That movement, from the price of a machine to the adequacy of an economic account, is what keeps this work open for me.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/from-compute-to-an-economic-question/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it","@type":"Article","name":"Holding an idea without becoming it","headline":"Holding an idea without becoming it","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["detachment","capitalism","identity and ideas","criticism","business collaboration","commitment"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Holding an idea without becoming it","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/"]},"description":"Why detachment complements capitalism: hearing the objection, changing the plan and making room for work neither side could do alone.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-06-08","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Holding an idea without becoming it\nWhen I wrote about a school of unlearning, detachment from the outcome was central to the idea. I wanted to follow that thought into a less expected setting: capitalism. My argument is that detachment can make business more cooperative, because it lets us notice possibilities that a contest over our identities keeps closing.\nBring an idea into a room and watch what happens. Someone says it will never work, and suddenly you are defending much more than the proposal. There were months behind it, perhaps a career decision, perhaps the hope of finally building something of your own. They seem to have dismissed all of that in one sentence. Or did they? They may have noticed a dependency you missed. The conversation has become personal before either of you has worked out what the objection was.\nThat ordinary, expensive confusion is part of why detachment interests me. I want to care deeply about the work without making it responsible for proving who I am. It sounds straightforward until the idea on the table is mine.\nThe drive that becomes attachment\nIn the original 2024 essay, I called trauma the root of human motivation and creativity, and argued that it could drive extraordinary work when channelled well. That was the psychological hypothesis behind my business argument. I was trying to understand what happens when an experience shakes the beliefs a person has built their life around: a betrayal, a humiliation, a loss, or a rejection that looks trivial to everybody else.\nThe size of the event from outside does not tell us how deeply it reaches into that person’s beliefs. Calling someone a snowflake avoids that question. Hurt can become a reason to make something happen, to prove oneself or to build a different life. Attachment to that purpose can supply enormous persistence. It can also make an unprofitable or unsustainable path feel impossible to leave, because changing the plan now feels like surrendering the person it helped us become.\nThat is the tension I wanted detachment to address. The drive can remain while the plan changes. I can want to build something without asking its success to settle every earlier hurt. Otherwise a business disagreement inherits a much older argument, and the person across the table may have no idea they have joined it.\nWhat did they actually say?\nImagine showing someone a plan for a new product. They say, “This will never work.” Perhaps they see a genuine distribution problem. Perhaps they have seen something superficially similar fail and are importing that history. Perhaps they are frustrated with the way the proposal was presented. The response can concern the idea, reveal the speaker's assumptions and comment on the person receiving it, all within the same sentence. Separating those possibilities gives the discussion an object we can examine.\nThere are two tempting shortcuts: dismiss the speaker because they do not understand, or treat the force of their response as evidence that the proposal is wrong. Both avoid separating the objection from the story surrounding it. Asking “What specifically would prevent it from working?” gives us something to examine. An answer about customer behaviour can be investigated; an answer about a missing capability can change the plan. Even a vague objection can reveal that the idea has not been communicated well enough. Detachment creates room for that examination without supplying its answer.\nCalibrate for the error\nRobotics gives me a useful comparison. The command I send and the motion an actuator produces can differ. If I insist that my command is ground truth about the machine, the machine will never work as I intend. I need to observe its response and understand the error. The point of calibration is to let the difference change what I do next.\nA conversation also has a transmission, a medium and a reception. I know what I meant to say; the other person received something through particular words, timing and circumstances. Their response is evidence about that encounter. It is not a direct readout of my intention, any more than my command is a measurement of the actuator's motion. People bring histories and meanings that the machine analogy cannot contain, but the comparison gives me a useful place to start: what crossed the gap, and what did I assume had crossed it?\nI cannot diagnose the person from their objection. I need enough distance to distinguish criticism of the idea, the speaker's assumptions and a comment on me. They are three different things, even when I initially hear them as one attack.\nThat is the calibration I want to practise. Asking which part of the proposal failed to convince someone gives the conversation something more useful than defending the whole of myself. Not everything is about me. An uncomfortable truth, and occasionally a considerable relief.\nListening without preparing the defence\nThere is a peculiar form of listening in which I hear only enough to construct my reply. I am present in the conversation, but most of my attention is already in the argument I intend to make. The cost is informational as well as interpersonal: the objection might contain the part that would have saved the team weeks of effort, and I am too busy defeating it to notice.\nA similar investment can gather around an explanation. If a picture of time made a difficult question feel intelligible, finding a flaw in it can seem to threaten the question itself. I might protect the picture by explaining away the flaw, or dismiss the whole inquiry because its first formulation failed. Both responses give that formulation too much power. I can let the explanation change while continuing to ask what made the question worth following. The distinction gives curiosity somewhere to go after the attractive answer stops helping.\nI can disagree with an objection while still letting it finish. I can revise a mechanism without surrendering the purpose. I can retain an intuition while admitting that the current explanation is inadequate. Not everything has to be a zero-sum game, or a choice between being entirely right and entirely wrong. As Obi-Wan would have it, only the Sith deal in absolutes. Humans are more than zero and one.\nTwo firms, one useful possibility\nDetachment also changes how I think about cooperation in business. A transaction can divide something that already exists, but a collaboration can make something possible that neither side could produce alone. Suppose one small firm understands the customer's operation and another can implement a missing component. Combining those strengths could produce useful work, yet each may worry that contributing knowledge will make the other stronger at its expense.\nThat concern can be real: a contract, a commercial boundary or the decision not to collaborate may be necessary. There is also a different obstacle, when each firm becomes so committed to being the complete solution that neither recognises the other's contribution. They protect their standing and lose the opportunity. Distance helps me distinguish what I need from the identity I have built around how I obtain it. Owning every component, winning every argument and receiving every credit are not always the same as achieving the outcome I care about.\nThis is the sense in which I say detachment complements capitalism. It makes room for a non-zero-sum game: the useful work we can create together may be larger than the advantage either of us can protect alone. I do not need everyone to become a hermit for that to work. I need the question “What can we make possible?” to remain audible when “What is mine?” starts shouting over it.\nRecognising that possibility does not mean trusting every promise. A collaboration can displace costs, reflect unequal bargaining power or conceal exploitation behind apparently generous cooperation. Whether it creates additional value, who receives it and what each participant knowingly accepts need examination. Those questions ask more of us than either “competition is bad” or “the market will sort it out.”\nKeeping the purpose, changing the plan\nDetachment is sometimes presented as withdrawal from ambition. I find that too simple. A leader who can change a plan may be more committed to the mission than one who cannot. A founder who lets a vehicle end may preserve more of the original purpose than one who keeps it going on promises it can no longer keep. The form can change while the commitment survives.\nChanging direction at every objection is not openness either. An idea needs enough persistence to encounter reality; some criticism is mistaken, and some resistance reflects a worthwhile disruption. Distance should make those reasons clearer rather than make commitment impossible. I have to listen, separate the claims, return to the purpose and decide, then stand behind the decision without turning it into a permanent portrait of who I am.\nThe person across the table\nPeople speak from different histories. A past failure can explain the urgency of an objection without settling whether it applies to this proposal. I can take that urgency seriously and still ask what, specifically, it reveals about the work. The person does not have to be reduced to a psychological explanation for the idea to receive a fair hearing.\nCooperation needs participants who can distinguish the shared work from a contest over themselves, as well as compatible capabilities. Making room for the other person's account, asking for the concrete concern and returning together to the purpose are everyday ways of practising that distinction. Detachment does not remove the stakes; it gives us a chance to see what they actually are.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-knowing-understanding-and-realising","@type":"Article","name":"Knowing, understanding and realising","headline":"Knowing, understanding and realising","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["knowing and understanding","realisation","abstraction","darshan","murti","lived experience"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Knowing, understanding and realising","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","relatedLink":["https://causality.in/blog/the-ineffable-geometry/"]},"description":"Why possessing an explanation and making its meaning part of one's life are different encounters.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-07-02","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Knowing, understanding and realising\nSuppose, for a thought experiment, I tell you that a thousand children die every day for want of clean water. It is a terrible number, but a number you can put down and continue with your day. Now imagine those thousand bodies on the road you take home. Then hold that scene across ten years: ten times 365 times a thousand. Finally, imagine recognising somebody you love among them. The information has acquired a relation to your own life. What happened to its meaning?\nKnowing, understanding, realising. We use the words almost interchangeably, yet there is a whole distance between those encounters. I can repeat an accurate explanation and remain strangely untouched by it. Another encounter can make that distance impossible to maintain. I am trying to find words for the change, not a universal staircase everyone climbs in the same order.\nThe useful distance of an abstraction\nKnowing can be accurate, useful and uneventful. I can remember a number, repeat a definition or recognise a pattern without having a profound experience. Much ordinary work depends on precisely that ability. An engineer needs to reason about a plant without experiencing every consequence of every component all at once.\nBut an abstraction gives us something by leaving something else out. A map can let me find a place without giving me its life. A description of a ritual can name its sequence without telling me what participating means to someone. I can read an explanation of a passage and retain its conclusion while missing the question that made the passage important. Each representation makes a particular encounter possible. Its usefulness can also make the omitted part easy to forget.\nThe omitted part sometimes changes the decision. A command that looks successful on a screen matters because equipment and people depend on the physical result. A resolved ticket matters because a person was trying to finish something. Recovering that context is not sentiment added after the technical work; it can change what the technical work is for. In another setting, returning to a familiar book with an unfamiliar concern can change which passage holds my attention. The material did not need to change for my relationship to it to become different.\nUnderstanding can become a hiding place\nUnderstanding gives the fact a structure. Why did the temperature change? What happens if the pump is turned down? Why does a passage return to an image that seemed incidental on the first reading? How does the order of a story change what I inferred from it? An explanation lets us predict, compare and identify missing connections instead of merely repeating what we have heard. The relation gives the remembered fact somewhere to work.\nYet an elegant explanation can become a place to hide. I can explain how an organisation overwhelms its team and still reproduce the pattern. I can describe what makes a product difficult while continuing to value it mainly for its sophistication. Understanding the arrangement does not automatically settle my relationship to it: which costs I accept, which consequences I overlook, what I am willing to change.\nBy realising, I mean the point at which that relationship becomes harder to evade. The meaning is no longer merely an object I can describe. I am implicated in it. Emotion may accompany the change, but intensity is not what makes the interpretation true. A vivid experience can mislead as well as illuminate. The important movement is from holding a description to encountering what the description means in a life.\nWhen the explanation comes back to me\nSmart Joules and Zenoti brought technical systems and the people using them into focus for me in different ways. Components communicating mattered, but so did what an operator could understand and do with the result. A roadmap mattered, but so did the customer trying to run a business rather than navigate the product. The technical account becomes stronger when it can recognise those purposes.\nRealisation need not produce an immediate visible action. A person may need time to change a habit, may lack the power to act or may discover a conflict they cannot yet resolve. An action can express an inward change without measuring all of it. Turning realising into doing would lose the very difference I am trying to name.\nThat distinction also makes room for patience with others. Explaining something more clearly may help them understand it without giving them my relationship to it. They have their own history, responsibilities and conditions for acting. Repetition alone cannot make those differences disappear.\nMurti, darshan and the missing translation\nThe distinction draws me towards darshan and the experience of a murti. Translating the latter simply as “idol” can leave out the encounter I am interested in: how meeting a form might become something other than knowing its description. The person brings familiarity, practice, expectation and history; two people can stand before the same form and have different encounters. I am interested in that accumulated relationship, in how a form can hold more than the particulars a stranger sees at first glance. The visible object can remain while the encounter changes through a person's life.\nI take that difference seriously without making the experience certify every explanation attached to it. Something can be transformative and still invite examination. Meaning is diminished both when we dismiss the encounter as nothing and when we demand that it prove a universal claim.\nThis is one reason I move between technical explanation, reflective writing and creative forms. A diagram can reveal a dependency. A scene can make its consequence present. A question can expose the assumption that held both together. In Markandeya's encounter, recognising his own hermitage within the impossible world gives its cosmic scale an intimacy that a nested diagram would omit. I can understand the containers without meeting that recognition. The scene gives me another way to approach what the description means.\nWriting can make the distance between a statement and its meaning harder to overlook. It need not make every fact emotionally overwhelming. It can recover enough of the world behind an abstraction that our decisions are not made entirely inside it.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-making-a-product-smaller","@type":"Article","name":"When customers need less product","headline":"When customers need less product","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["product simplification","customer discovery","Zenoti","SMB onboarding","support analysis"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/","@type":"WebPage","name":"When customers need less product","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/an-ai-product-is-more-than-a-model/","https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/","https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/"]},"description":"Customer evidence can make removing complexity a stronger product decision than extending the feature list.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-09-04","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"When customers need less product\nImagine buying software to make running your business easier, then discovering that your first task is to understand how the software company thinks. Its categories, configuration and navigation all make sense to somebody. Unfortunately, that somebody isn't you yet. The capability you need may be in there, surrounded by decisions you never expected to make.\nAdding the missing feature gives a roadmap something obvious to say. Removing a burden asks a more awkward question: why did we make the customer carry it in the first place?\nAt Zenoti, I owned support, onboarding and taxation for the US and Canadian small-business market. Customer evidence led the work towards a simpler experience, rather than a better demonstration of the suite's breadth.\nThe work hiding in the support queue\nI brought five years of support data together with product analytics, surveys and customer conversations. The breadth of the queue helped expose recurring friction; the conversations helped explain what customers were trying to accomplish when it appeared. The two forms of evidence could challenge each other. A frequently reported issue might look like a missing feature until the customer's account revealed a confusing decision or an external dependency.\n“I cannot get started” brings several possible problems into the same queue. A customer may need an integration, be waiting on another provider or face incomplete imported records. They may also be able to complete the operation but struggle to find it. The diagnostic work connects the complaint to those causes: what exists, what is discoverable and which dependency prevents completion? Grouping tickets makes a recurring area visible; understanding the customer's task gives the roadmap a reason to change it.\nEnough for this customer\nThe suite served a broader range of businesses than the SMB experience needed to expose. A capability useful elsewhere could impose configuration and navigation here without enough benefit. The segment's work gave us a way to decide which complexity was necessary, rather than treating the suite's full breadth as the natural starting point for every customer.\nThe evidence changed our roadmap in that direction: retain the requirements that mattered and reduce the surrounding burden. Buddy, the AI-first design system behind the revamped SMB experience, brought assistance and analytics into that narrower arrangement. The product reached customers, with self-onboarding and simpler taxation work among the changes I drove. The point of the revision was a more usable operation for this segment, rather than a smaller display of the same decisions.\nTwo routes to the same business operation place either the suite's categories or the customer's necessary decisions first.\nThis conceptual comparison keeps the necessary choices and external dependencies in both routes.\nThis was not a general campaign for minimalism. A business with more complex operations can genuinely need a broader arrangement, and a smaller interface can conceal a necessary dependency. The product decision is about fit: the customer should not have to absorb the whole suite merely because the company knows how to build it.\nWho is doing the onboarding now?\nTurning an implementation team's work into a customer checklist can relocate the burden instead of removing it. The queue disappears from one part of the organisation, but the customer still has to interpret the configuration and coordinate its dependencies, now alone. Useful self-onboarding changes the work: avoid an unnecessary decision, explain the context of a necessary one and provide a route through an exception. The customer gains a way to begin, rather than an obligation to perform the implementation team's ideal procedure.\nPayment onboarding makes the distinction concrete. The software can be ready while the provider still needs to complete its part. Data migration has a different dependency: a customer's email or phone may appear in several records, so a convenient match can merge people who should remain distinct. Preserving source customer IDs helps carry identity across the import; balances and appointments then need to remain attached to the right person. The customer encounters all of this as one business operation, even though the causes and remedies sit in different places.\nLearning the system just to file the tax\nCountry-, case- and tax-code-specific work can ask a business operator to become an interpreter of the system's internal requirements. Streamlining that work meant reducing unnecessary translation between the customer's task and the product, so they could spend less time on manual filing and compliance inside it. Removing fields helped only if the remaining experience made the important decisions intelligible. A default that changes an obligation or payment still needs to be understood; an exception still needs a navigable path. Fewer screens can follow from that understanding, but they cannot supply it.\nAfter the ticket closes\nA resolved ticket can mean that a workaround was supplied, a configuration changed or a defect repaired. To understand the customer's experience, we need to follow what happened afterwards: could they finish the operation? A falling ticket count might reflect less friction or people giving up on asking. A faster setup among those who finish might coexist with more people abandoning it. Each measure sees one part of the work.\nThe useful comparison follows the customer through a defined task. How many begin, where do they stop, which exceptions require help and can they complete the work they came to do? The task, customer group and period give the measures a meaning. The revised experience reached customers, but understanding its effect requires following their work through those stages rather than treating the rollout itself as the result.\nKnowing enough to ask less\nChoosing what the customer should not have to understand is part of product judgment. Technical fluency exposes the dependencies; customer understanding helps determine which of them belong in the experience. The two forms of work meet when a simpler path keeps consequential choices intelligible while letting the person spend their attention on the business they came to run.\nThat lesson has stayed relevant as models make more responses possible. A more capable component does not require a more expansive product surface: the valuable operation may remain narrow, even when the model can talk about almost anything. Sometimes the stronger roadmap is the one that understands enough to ask less of the customer.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/customer-and-complexity.svg","description":"Two routes to the same business operation place either the suite's categories or the customer's necessary decisions first.","caption":"This conceptual comparison keeps the necessary choices and external dependencies in both routes.\n"}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/making-a-product-smaller/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-navigating-the-feedback-loop","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Navigating the feedback loop","headline":"Navigating the feedback loop","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["awareness and context","metadata","transfer learning","causality","engineering and fiction"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Navigating the feedback loop","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/","https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/"]},"description":"How awareness, metadata and reusable intelligence connect a personal inquiry through YOJN, engineering, mathematics and Sleepwalker.","datePublished":{"@value":"2023-10-30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Navigating the feedback loop\nAn exploration of causality and awareness in AI, first published in October 2023.\nI have been keeping busy following my own advice since I last published. IYKYK ;)\nI've set up an experiment across publications, ventures and digital identities. Each gives me a different way to approach the same inquiry: the fundamental nature of causality, and what understanding it could make possible for artificial intelligence. A practical application, a mathematical question and a story can reveal different parts of that problem. The point of connecting them is to let what happens in one change what I ask in another.\nAwareness is all you need\nThe practical starting point is YOJN and DAO Studio's ambition to make AI accessible, usable and available for more people to build with.\n“Awareness is All You Need” is the thought I am following. Here, awareness concerns context: how a model is being used, what the task means and why particular training choices or hyperparameters have been selected. Making that setting available to the system could make its response more relevant to the work. This is a proposition about useful context, not a claim that supplying it gives a model conscious experience or settles everything an AI system needs.\nThe question changes what I want from an application. Producing an answer is one thing; fitting that answer into the situation that gave rise to the question is another. YojnAI gives that inquiry a practical home.\nMetadata and meta-awareness\nWe keep hearing that data is the new oil. Perhaps the metadata is where some of the value is hiding.\nData arrives with circumstances: where it came from, what it describes and what someone intended to do with it. Those circumstances can be lost when everything becomes another training example. My interest in metadata is the possibility of carrying more of that setting into learning, so that the model has a finer account of the task rather than merely more material to process.\nThat is the sense in which I am exploring meta-awareness. The information about the information becomes part of the question. Whether it helps has to be shown on the task.\nIntelligence that can travel\nThe larger ambition is to commoditise intelligence: make it as accessible and ordinary to use as electricity. Transfer learning offers a route towards that ambition, because learning need not begin from nothing for every application.\nThe promise is that something learned in one setting can help in another. It is not a guarantee of seamless transfer across arbitrary domains; transferred knowledge can also make the new task worse. The research on negative transfer makes that limit concrete. The interesting work is discovering what can travel and what the receiving task still needs.\nThe Red Pill follows the economic possibility: what changes when intelligence itself can become something people exchange? The engineering question underneath it is how that reuse could work.\nEngineering the unknown\nAn application gives an abstract idea consequences. Something is built, someone tries to use it, and the result returns information that the original explanation did not contain. That feedback is part of the exploration, not merely the last stage after the thinking is finished.\nSleepwalker, myths and mathematics\nSleepwalker approaches causality through fiction, mythology and scientific ideas. Its protagonist's journey through feedback loops offers another way to encounter awareness: a story can let someone inhabit a question before they can express it formally.\nMathematics asks something different of the same curiosity. Natural phenomena have repeatedly prompted new mathematical descriptions. Causality is where I want to investigate the tools available for describing these relationships, their limits and the language we might still need.\nThe crossing between myths and mathematics matters to me. A myth can open an unfamiliar way of seeing; a mathematical account has to make its relationships precise enough to examine. Sleepwalker can explore the space between them without the story becoming proof of the scientific claim. Each form gives the inquiry something the other cannot supply on its own.\nDAO Studio: the practical playground\nDAO Studio is where the questions meet engineering and where an attractive account has to confront what can actually be made useful. Ideas from these different explorations can be put to work, and the next attempt can begin with something the previous one taught us. The abstract and the concrete keep meeting here.\nA place to follow the crossings\ngetHarsh is the personal digital identity through which I catalogue and share these experiments. I think of it as decentralised in that the work has several homes: DAO Studio's practical work, Causality's research, Sleepwalker's fiction and RawThoughts' writing and discussion. Their connection is the inquiry travelling among them; each keeps its own purpose.\nThe loop comes back to the person asking the question. A practical difficulty can send me towards mathematics. A story can make a question vivid enough to pursue. An explanation can invite another person to notice what I missed. Publishing makes those crossings available for discussion, so that the next question need not come only from me.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/navigating-the-feedback-loop/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-research-becomes-a-reading-path","@type":"Article","name":"How research becomes a reading path","headline":"How research becomes a reading path","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["technical editorial strategy","research-led content","writer briefs","search intent","technical communication","reader journeys"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/","@type":"WebPage","name":"How research becomes a reading path","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/"]},"description":"An editorial programme connects a reader's problem to explanations, evidence and useful artifacts without turning each article into the same sales pitch.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-12-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"How research becomes a reading path\nA person searches for a cheap GPU because that is the part of the problem they know how to name. The problem may be a delayed experiment, a growing inference bill or a model that will not fit the available hardware. A page can answer the price query accurately and still leave that person almost where they started. The more interesting editorial question is how to help them recognise what they are actually trying to decide.\nAt AION, I worked across GPU-cloud product questions, technical research and developer experience. I also drove a research-led content programme, connecting audience positioning, article briefs, writer coordination and technical review. The work made me think about how infrastructure becomes useful to the people building with it, and how that understanding can travel through a product or an explanation.\nThat made me interested in content as a programme rather than an output queue. Research has relationships inside it: a claim depends on a mechanism, a comparison depends on a common unit, and a useful recommendation depends on conditions that may not hold for this reader. Writing gives those relationships a form somebody can encounter. A programme gives them somewhere to continue when the first answer changes the question.\nI find the important editorial choice comes before the writing assignment. Is this piece helping someone choose, explaining a mechanism, or giving them a method they can try? If I cannot answer that, commissioning more words only postpones the confusion. Once I can answer it, the same research may call for a short argument, a careful technical explanation or a worksheet rather than three articles competing to say everything.\nThe query is an entrance\nImagine planning an independent set of articles for a team trying to understand its growing AI infrastructure bill. Search results suggest a familiar inventory: hardware prices, inference optimisation, training costs and provider comparisons. The inventory is useful evidence of the language people use. It is a poor substitute for understanding their predicament. A reader comparing accelerators might need a rate card; another might need to discover that their cost is dominated by repeated preparation, idle commitment or an unsuitable workload.\nAn editorial choice begins to emerge once those situations are separated. One piece can make the unit of comparison intelligible. Another can follow the memory and scheduling mechanism behind a serving constraint. A worked calculation can let the reader substitute their own assumptions. A procurement question can expose a condition that a published table leaves unanswered. These are different contributions, even when they share the same search vocabulary.\nThe distinction changes the use of search research. It can reveal unclear terminology, recurring questions and explanations that leave a gap. It need not dictate a collection of near-identical articles built around every variation of a phrase. Google's people-first content guidance asks whether the work adds original value and helps its intended audience, rather than merely summarising existing material for search traffic. That gives the programme a useful editorial test.\nSearch brings a reader to a question. The writing should leave them with a better account of the question, even if that account is less convenient for the publisher's initial pitch. Otherwise the programme rewards entry while neglecting understanding.\nDifferent readers can meet the same mechanism\nA technical subject does not require a choice between an opaque engineering manual and an executive story with the mechanism removed. The same piece can begin with a consequence that matters, develop the mechanism producing it, and show what follows for a decision. The transitions need to be real. Appending a business paragraph to a technical explanation does not automatically connect them.\nFor example, a discussion of serving memory becomes commercially interesting when it explains how memory constrains concurrent work. A discussion of waiting becomes operationally interesting when it identifies the point at which the whole allocation is usable. A discussion of cost becomes technically useful when it states what workload and output the money buys. The reader does not have to inspect every implementation detail to understand the consequence, but the reasoning must be available to someone who does.\nThis also permits different forms. A short essay can unsettle a misleading comparison. A deeper explanation can carry the mechanism. An editable worksheet can let a team test its own case. An experiment can show where the explanation fails. The questions around useful compute deserve that range because they are not all answered by the same kind of evidence. Calling every form a blog post can conceal how much work the programme is actually asking its contributors to do.\nI would rather give a reader one satisfying explanation and a meaningful continuation than stretch every piece into an encyclopedia. The first answer should be complete enough to help on its own. The continuation is for the new question it has made visible, not for information withheld to force another click.\nA brief carries the thought across people\nOnce other writers enter the programme, a title and a word count carry very little of the research. They identify an assignment without explaining what would make it worth reading. A writer needs to understand the reader's situation, the particular argument, the evidence available and the unresolved question. They also need room to find a better formulation than the one the initial brief supplied.\nConsider a brief for an article comparing two infrastructure arrangements. “Explain why our option is cheaper” fixes the conclusion before the comparison exists. “Find which costs change when the workload is interrupted” gives the writer a real investigation. They can discover that a lower rental rate is outweighed by recovery, or that interruption is inexpensive for a restartable workload. The resulting article can teach something specific. If it contradicts the hoped-for pitch, that is editorial information the publisher needed.\nHere is how I would turn that independent comparison into a brief, using invented assumptions for the example.\nPart of the brief\tThe work it gives the contributor\t\nReader\tA team whose experiment can restart, but whose deadline cannot move\t\nDecision\tWhether a lower hourly rate is worth interruptions and repeated setup\t\nComparison\tCost and completion time for the same workload, including failed attempts\t\nEvidence needed\tThe interruption conditions, restart time and billing treatment, rather than a rate card alone\t\nUseful artifact\tA two-option worksheet with visible assumptions and a deadline column\t\nOpen question\tWhen does the recommendation change if restart time doubles?\t\nThe worksheet gives each contributor something concrete to improve. A specialist can challenge whether the restart assumptions fit the workload. A writer can make the decision intelligible before introducing the arithmetic. A designer can reveal which cost belongs to which attempt. If nobody can fill in the deadline column, the missing information becomes obvious before it is concealed by a persuasive conclusion.\nThe brief is therefore a meeting place, not a frozen script. Its purpose is to let research survive the handoff without making the contributor a transcription service. The writer's judgment and attribution remain part of the work.\nReview follows the claim\nThe most polished paragraph can be the least trustworthy part of an article. It may contain a number borrowed from a different population, a comparison with mismatched units or an impressive example whose underlying source cannot be found. Reviewing only the bibliography misses these transformations. The question is what the source supports at the point where the article uses it.\nIn the hypothetical infrastructure programme, a cheaper rate does not mean a cheaper completed workload. A faster local operation does not mean a faster service. A proposed feature does not become a customer result because it has acquired a convincing narrative. An editor can make the writing more useful by making each claim smaller or more particular. The reader can then use the explanation without inheriting the publisher's wishful thinking.\nReview also asks what the reader can now do. Perhaps the article gives them a question to take into a purchasing conversation, a way to interpret a measurement or an experiment they can adapt. A download or a long reading session can suggest interest, but the useful contribution is more specific than either. A programme learns when readers bring back a confusion, a counterexample or a new problem that its current explanations cannot carry.\nThe programme acquires a shape\nThere is no need for every article to repeat the whole argument. Once the pieces have distinct work, their connections become more natural. A broad economic question can lead into a calculation; a calculation can expose a technical constraint; the constraint can lead to an operational comparison. Some readers will take that path. Others will arrive at the calculation directly and leave with what they needed. The programme should accommodate both without pretending that every visitor is taking one prescribed journey.\nThe writing can support a commercial conversation by making it more informed. That is a different ambition from pushing every piece toward the same offer. Sometimes a reader learns that the product is suitable; sometimes they discover a limitation that should change the purchase. Trust grows in that encounter with reality, not in the absence of inconvenient information.\nWhat I want from research-led publishing is that accumulation. An article answers a question well enough to become useful. Another gives the answer greater reach or exposes its limits. The artifacts allow the reasoning to travel beyond the original prose. Eventually the programme contains more than a record of publishing activity: it contains a way for people to think through a difficult part of their work together.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/research-becomes-a-reading-path/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-responsibility-is-not-a-title","@type":"Article","name":"Leading beyond the title","headline":"Leading beyond the title","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["leadership","team autonomy","delegation","trust","decision-making under uncertainty"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Leading beyond the title","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/"},"description":"The uncomfortable work of earning trust, making a decision and helping a team outgrow its dependence on you.","datePublished":{"@value":"2020-02-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Leading beyond the title\nI have had amazing mentors, colleagues, friends and family, and one hell of a team. These are lessons from working with them and watching leadership emerge, including the times I got it wrong. I expect to learn better. But one thing has stayed with me: there is no nine-to-five switch for the responsibility. The people and consequences do not disappear when the meeting ends.\nThat is what makes leadership hard. I think of it as an art, with the balance to be found for yourself and your team. I want the work to succeed, and I want to be useful to the people doing it. Those wishes can pull in opposite directions when being useful starts to mean being needed for every decision. The harder achievement is helping the team grow beyond that dependence, then being glad that it has.\nWhose ambition are we building?\nA vision can be wonderfully clear inside my head and mean very little to the person I am asking to spend their life on it. What are they hoping to become? What are they giving up to be here? The company has objectives, but a team is made of people with purposes of their own. The interesting work is finding a direction they can recognise themselves in, not making my explanation so persuasive that their differences disappear.\nAt Smart Joules, my responsibilities grew from engineering into building and leading a product-development team. Hardware, software, research, testing, production and design could not each succeed in isolation. A technically correct component could still leave another team with an unusable system. People needed to understand the larger consequence of their decisions, not only the task assigned to them.\nThat kind of shared context takes time. It includes explaining why something matters, hearing where the plan does not fit and changing the division of work when the original assumptions were wrong. It cannot be replaced by a more inspiring slogan.\nI think of the leader as the guardian of that vision, rather than its owner. The people doing the work give it life. Making the objectives clear is necessary management; leadership also asks whether the arrangements we built yesterday can carry today's problems, leaving the team room to build for tomorrow. If everyone is permanently fighting the present emergency, another ambitious roadmap has not given them a future.\nTrust makes that possible. I have described leadership as an act of love, which may sound out of place beside a roadmap. But people are giving their time and hope to the work. A policy cannot substitute for the trust between them. Build it through honest conversations, with your guard down; if there is none, be willing to walk away. Empathy includes recognising that everyone brings experiences and boundaries of their own. It takes practice, and getting it wrong sometimes is a reason to keep learning, not to pretend the personal part can be removed.\nSomebody still has to decide\nThere is always another projection we could prepare, another framework we could consult. Sometimes it would genuinely improve the decision. Sometimes it is how I postpone admitting that the available information will not make me certain. Waiting has consequences too. Experience can suggest a connection I cannot yet articulate, and it can make an old pattern feel applicable where it isn't. I need that intuition, but I also need to tell the team which part is a judgement and what would make us reconsider it. Otherwise my confidence becomes somebody else's unexplained risk.\nConfidence that hides uncertainty borrows trust from the people who will bear the result. Endless hesitation asks those same people to carry the absence of a decision. The commitment needs to fit what the situation warrants now, with a way to learn quickly enough for the next decision to be better. Neither confidence nor hesitation removes that responsibility.\nAgreement about a direction also differs from giving everyone the decision they wanted. A difficult choice can leave real disagreement. Buy-in means people understand why the choice was made, have had a chance to surface what it misses and can see how their work contributes from here. Seeking that commitment asks more of a leader than demanding consensus. It takes some distance from the plan to hear an objection without treating it as disloyalty.\nWhen the result is embarrassing\nFailures belong to the leader and success to the team: I hold that as an ethic about where to stand, rather than a literal account of who caused every event. People still have responsibilities, and causes still need examination. The leader's part is to remain present when the result exposes the direction or support they provided.\nSuccess makes generosity easy. I can give the team credit and still enjoy how well the outcome reflects on me. Failure asks something less flattering: can somebody tell me that my direction was wrong without first taking care of my feelings? A requirement may have been unclear, the support insufficient or the promise unreasonable. If people have to protect my image before they can explain the failure, I have made the next decision harder for everyone.\nAbsorbing the blame helps little if everyone leaves reassured but carrying the same broken arrangement. We still need to understand what happened and who can change it. I want accepting and learning from failure to become inviting enough that people do it, including me. Run away from every failure and it begins making the next decision for you. Celebrating what we learned is part of making it possible to act again, rather than waiting until we can promise never to be wrong.\nThe team that no longer needs me for everything\nBeing needed can feel like proof that I am doing a good job. It is also an excellent way to become the bottleneck. Suppose somebody owns onboarding but has to ask me before changing a confusing step, speaking to support or investigating why customers leave. What have I actually given them? Perhaps the responsibility for reporting the problem, while keeping the useful decisions for myself. Delegation asks me to share the context and room to act, then stay available when the problem exceeds that arrangement.\nDelegation becomes real when the person can carry the decision and understand where its limits are. Review can then examine the work instead of reclaiming every choice. Over time, the team should develop enough shared context and capability to do important things without the leader being the indispensable interpreter of everything. Making myself less necessary is part of building something that can continue.\nThere are people outside the mission, too\nLeadership can involve difficult choices, personal sacrifices and work that will not fit a tidy routine. Family, support and the ability to ask for help belong in that picture; they are part of what lets someone carry the responsibility. That does not make exhaustion a measure of devotion. People have lives outside the mission, including the leader, and heroic availability can hide weak planning or inadequate capacity.\nI have also worried about the difference between stopping work and recovering from it. For an entrepreneur, time away can leave the same demands running in the background. The question is whether that time actually restores the person who has to return, not merely whether the calendar calls it a break. I have no universal routine to offer; the distinction I want to keep visible is between being off the clock and being recharged.\nSome commitments demand more than a comfortable routine; continuing others in the same way destroys the people making the work possible. I cannot settle that boundary for every situation. I want to know whether the sacrifice serves a purpose people understand, whether it is shared honestly and whether there is a path beyond the emergency. The leader may need support rather than another occasion to appear strong, and the people providing it bear consequences of their own.\nAfter the title\nAcross employment, consulting and ventures, my formal role has changed. The recurring difficulty has been connecting a direction to the system, the team and the people affected by it. Leadership does not guarantee that an organisation survives, and a failed organisation does not make every contribution within it meaningless. The way people and work were treated determines what can still be learned and carried forward. A title can end while its consequences, relationships and example continue.\nAll of this can sound like the ramblings of a troubled mind. It is also why asking for help matters. Friends and family care for us when we are all out of love to give ourselves; they should not have to carry the consequences in silence.\nWhen another opportunity arrives, the questions are personal again. Am I up for it? Will I make responsible and ethical choices when it gets too much? Am I willing to ask for help, rather than let the people closest to me carry a responsibility I refuse to name? Being bold is easier to admire from a distance than to live with every day.\nIt is a responsibility you earn, and you pay with scars. That is why I do not want exhaustion mistaken for having done it well. People often find enough time and money to do things twice. Would it not be better to take the time to do them properly the first time? There is no virtue in burning everybody out on the way. Straightforward, perhaps. Easy, no.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-teachings-of-the-mirror","@type":"Article","name":"Teachings of the mirror","headline":"Teachings of the mirror","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["teacher as a mirror","teaching","Shiva","Mahashivratri","self-understanding"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Teachings of the mirror","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/","https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/"]},"description":"A Mahashivratri reflection on the teacher as a mirror, the difficulty of an unbiased encounter and the work of finding a path.","datePublished":{"@value":"2020-02-21","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Teachings of the mirror\nA reflection written for Mahashivratri in February 2020.\nThere is a saying that the teacher appears when the student is ready. There is another that there is no knowledge without a teacher. What interests me is that the teacher can be formless. A person can teach, but so can an encounter that refuses to fit what I already believe. That was one of the largest things the preceding decade had taught me.\nAt the time, I looked forward to a day when I could retire as a professor. Every wakeful moment felt like part of becoming a good teacher. Before that, though, I had so much to learn and do. I did not want to hand future students a half-baked perception of reality. I wanted to bring them something factual, experiential and broad enough to have survived contact with more than my own explanation.\nIt was a long journey. I was in no rush.\nA festival, and a teacher\nMahashivratri and Gurupurnima were the festivals I felt closest to. On Mahashivratri, alongside other devotees, I celebrated Shiva and his beauty. In the yogic legends, Shiva is Adiyogi, the first yogi and a great teacher. The figure drew my attention towards what a teacher might make possible.\nNow, what does any of that have to do with a mirror? Mere ramblings of a madman, or an underlying symmetry? Of course I had a reason for going there.\nA true teacher, in the ideal I was reaching for, is an unbiased observer who gives you methods for discarding the notions that hold you down. The teacher does not simply put a more impressive set of notions in their place. They help you see the choices that your account of yourself and society had made invisible. Like a mirror, they show you something you have to encounter for yourself.\nThe teacher can help reveal the path. Walking it remains your own doing. That was the meaning I found in Shiva's bhakti: a relationship that could liberate the seeker into discovery, while leaving the work of living with the seeker.\nMethods in the middle of the chaos\nAmid the chaos, I found myself reaching for methods rather than a rule for every possible situation. You follow a practice. Distractions and hurdles arrive. Your picture of reality changes as you make your way through it. Faith can keep you moving when the path is no longer as obvious as it looked from the beginning.\nThe mirror remains beside you. What it shows may be uncomfortable. There is no guarantee that the person you meet in it resembles the person you have been explaining to everyone else. The choice to respond to that encounter is yours. In this image, the methods do not change to flatter the person I would prefer to see. I am the one being asked to change.\nThat is a demanding image of teaching. It asks for more than passing on an answer. It asks whether I can help somebody examine what stands between them and their own understanding. It also turns the question back on me: what am I still unable to see because I need my present account to remain intact?\nThe difficulty of being the mirror\nIn ordinary life, I am hardly an unbiased observer. I have more biases than I can count and much to learn. Calling the teacher a mirror does not suddenly make me one. It makes the distance between the ideal and my own practice harder to overlook.\nWhat I kept finding in the teachings of Shiva was an image of balance: an unbiased consciousness pervading existence. I found it almost poetic. Survival and exuberance are different ambitions. The habits that get us through a day may not be the habits that let us encounter it fully.\nI did not know what God was, or whether God existed at all. I was still discovering things with every wakeful moment. Yet the idea of a supreme consciousness that could remain balanced and unbiased gave me something to contemplate. Seen through that ideal, the austerity associated with Shiva's path began to make sense to me. The simplest statement could contain an extraordinarily difficult way of living.\nBack to the hazy borders\nI returned to the borders between history, science, art, myths and legends. The festival gave me an occasion to stay with the questions, and to be grateful for the teachers I had already encountered. Their forms had been different. So had the things they made possible.\nThe aspiration to teach belonged to that moment in my life. The question it carried continues through learning with a real problem: what must I understand, and what support must I offer, before another person can make the work their own?\nA mirror cannot take the journey for the person standing before it. It can make the next act of looking possible.\nAnd be careful what you wish for. It just might come true. 😉\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/teachings-of-the-mirror/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-first-decade","@type":"Article","name":"Gravity was still acting on the magnet","headline":"Gravity was still acting on the magnet","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["first decade of work","founding engineer","learning through work","personal and professional life"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Gravity was still acting on the magnet","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/every-product-manager-is-an-ai-product-manager/","https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"]},"description":"A first-decade reflection on the people, experiments and personal life that shape professional judgment.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Gravity was still acting on the magnet\nThis reflection began in August 2024, looking back at my first decade of work. Its sense of standing at a turning point belongs to that moment.\nI have been lucky in the people I have worked with. Academia, research, startups and communities put me beside minds that made me want to understand more. Who I would be working with often mattered more to me than what the work paid. That choice changed what I got to learn.\nThe work itself was rarely confined to the title. Cleaning the office floor, making PCBs, writing firmware, building a model, finding a vendor, supporting the customer, making a sale, raising money and helping a team grow could all belong to the same undertaking. A company starting from nothing does not politely wait for the correct department to exist.\nI tried my hands at a great deal of it. As I thought you should. The first ten years seemed like a chance to discover what I was good at and what I was spectacularly capable of being bad at. Boy, did I use the opportunity.\nAn extensive period of regression testing\nThe experiments were not confined to work. I trained for MMA and entered tournaments, played piano, made art, hiked through different parts of India and spent time in monasteries. I read a lot, and bought more books than I could read. There were the pleasures and confusions of being in my twenties, too.\nEat, pray, love, if you want the familiar catalogue. I preferred to think of it as an extensive period of regression testing: eventually I might understand what all those Imtiaz Ali films had been going on about.\nNone of this fitted neatly around a career plan. It was the life in which the work was happening. A new interest could change the questions I brought back to an engineering problem. A difficult period could change how much I could carry, or what I thought was worth carrying. The person doing the job did not disappear when the job began.\nBread on the table, questions on the way\nWhile I was discovering myself, I also had to put bread on the table. I joined Smart Joules as a founding engineer, moved into product development and support, worked as a product manager at Zenoti, and took part in ventures as an adviser, cofounder and consultant. The career journey follows those different responsibilities in their own settings.\nAcross them, artificial intelligence kept appearing. I had the good fortune to watch the distance between image-recognition work and language-model applications become part of ordinary product conversations. Being a nineties kid had already given me one version of that sensation: dial-up modems and floppy disks giving way to streaming on a phone. Suddenly the thing that had required effort and patience became something you expected to be there.\nTiming mattered. I did not create the moment in which these possibilities arrived. I got to work while they were changing, and I got to learn from people trying to do something with them. I cannot tell that story honestly without saying how lucky I was.\nThe lessons also arrived outside the projects I was formally building. Presenting work, meeting someone after an event or sharing a few beers could open a conversation I would otherwise never have had. I learned from people just leaving college and from people who had spent more than forty years in industry. At barely thirty, I could learn from someone who had been doing the work longer than I had been alive.\nComparing those conversations, I kept coming back to leadership. Technical ability mattered, but so did how someone carried uncertainty, worked with other people and stayed with a problem. I was interested both in people who had made something work and people who had not made it work yet. That “yet” left room for the person to continue.\nTwo forces, one life\nThe analogy I liked was a pair of magnets dropped from a building. You might be studying gravitation, but electromagnetism has not agreed to stop operating for the convenience of your experiment. The magnets still attract or repel while they fall.\nThe professional and personal are like that. Calling one of them the subject of your attention does not suspend the other. The relationships, hopes and difficulties around the work still act on the person doing it. The work acts back.\nIn 2024, I felt myself reaching a turning point in both. I was imagining a life organised around more than my own next move. The decade behind me had felt like a sprint through experiences; I hoped the next one would become more of a marathon. I was projecting, of course. Time would have to tell me how well that description fitted.\nBut the possibility was already changing my priorities. I wanted to understand which parts of the first decade were experiments I had needed to try, which had become a practice worth sustaining, and what I would do differently if I began again.\nThat is where the next argument starts. The changing work of product management is one of the things those years taught me to care about. It comes from the engineering and the customers, certainly. It also comes from the people, the interests outside work and the life in which all of it had to make sense.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-first-decade/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-future-we-create-and-condemn/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-future-we-create-and-condemn","@type":"Article","name":"The future we create, and condemn","headline":"The future we create, and condemn","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["capitalism","technological futures","education","ethics","identity"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-future-we-create-and-condemn/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The future we create, and condemn","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-future-we-create-and-condemn/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/debauchery-in-the-name-of-existence/","https://getharsh.in/blog/holding-an-idea-without-becoming-it/"]},"description":"A January 2020 reflection on a father and son imagining the same future, the economics of making it real and the identities that shape our response.","datePublished":{"@value":"2020-01-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The future we create, and condemn\nFrom January 2020. The original title was “The only way to be certain of the future is to create it. A surer way is to condemn it.”\nWhat do we know about the past beyond the fact that it is not the present? Its ghosts haunt us, but so do the ghosts of the future's past: things that have not happened yet, which we already imagine remembering with regret. Somewhere between them, we can become the very thing we condemn.\nIf I wanted an image of collective consciousness, I would look at society. We are social animals, although we seem to have wandered very far into the woods around the tree of life. We create things we do not quite seek, then discover that they can organise our lives around themselves.\nWelcome to capitalism, the unsaid religion of the twenty-first century. The comparison interested me because it concerned more than money. It concerned the arrangements through which we agreed that something deserved to exist, the things that received support and the questions that were easier to ask in favour of the arrangement than against it.\nMy father imagined this future too\nMy dad grew up with images of flying cars, personal robots and space. When I imagined a technological future, I kept finding some of the same objects there.\nRoughly half a century separated the two imaginations. The intervening work mattered: science, engineering, manufacturing, markets and the effort of making a possibility usable. But the recurrence still unsettled me. Why could a technology remain vivid as an image for so long before it became part of ordinary life?\nCapitalism solves problems, not bare-metal mathematics. A scientific possibility does not organise its own adoption. Someone has to find a use, gather resources and make the arrangement work. That is one place where the delay enters. The science is not thereby finished, nor the engineering merely waiting for permission; the path from an idea to a shared way of living has several kinds of work inside it.\nPeople, society and nature keep introducing the variable or two that the projection left out. We might succeed in pulling one over the chaos. I would hesitate to make a plan on the assumption that the odds were permanently in our favour.\nWhat receives the blessing?\nCapitalism’s latest rebound, in the metaphor I was carrying, was STEM. I was glad to see science and technology attract resources. We needed the tree to bear useful fruit. We had left an extraordinary mess on the planet, and hoping that somebody else would eventually clean it up was hardly an adequate response.\nYet that was also the question. Were we solving the problem, or delaying it long enough for a later generation to inherit the bill? A more sophisticated way to move the burden could look like progress from the place where the burden had disappeared.\nPhilosophy and ethics seemed less fortunate in the competition for resources. They could ask what prosperity meant and who paid for it, yet struggle to make their own work economically sustainable. The profitable imitation of moral concern could fare better than the patient inquiry. That contradiction made me angry.\nCalling capitalism a religion was my way of asking which concerns it could hear. If an ethical question had no clear way to make money, did it still have a place at the table? If it challenged the table itself, who would pay for the chair?\nSilence is not peace\nIf capitalism could create a broad consensus around useful work, why not welcome it? Because consensus and peace were not always the same thing. People could be silent because they agreed, because they were exhausted or because the cost of speaking was too high.\nThe possibility of an all-seeing system concerned me for a similar reason. Convenience and coordination could become attractive ways to accept a power whose consequences were difficult to contest. Orwellian anxiety did not require every part of an Orwellian world to have arrived already.\nWe could continue fighting the wars of our forefathers in new institutions. We could also let corporations acquire a power over ordinary life that public debate struggled to understand. The question was not settled by declaring the institution benevolent or the technology inevitable. It returned to the room we had left for ethics, philosophy and dissent.\nEducation was central to that room. It produced the people who would lead every other institution, yet so often entered the discussion as an afterthought. I wanted education that was not captured by one partisan account, one soil or one school of thought. The work of examining an idea should not require swearing loyalty to it first.\nBecoming what I condemn\nA conversation brought the question closer to home. We were talking about the things we did, the things we did not do and the things we once thought we would never do. The response to what had happened was the part we could work on most directly. For much of the rest, we could try; we could not command the outcome.\nAttachment, fear and jealousy could gather around an identity until insecurity began making its decisions. A society could do this too. People defended inherited accounts of themselves, and the defence reproduced the behaviour they condemned in others.\nSo we went looking for cheat codes. Ways to notice the trap before the next response completed it. The difficulty was that even the effort to escape could feed on the same pride and insecurity. I could become very attached to the identity of somebody who had overcome attachment.\nThat is where the long-lost art of listening mattered. It asked me to let something enter that my identity had already decided was unwelcome. Detachment and capitalism meet in that uncomfortable discovery: my response to the world is helping to make the world I will have to live in.\nToo close to the blue pixel\nWe went looking, and I found myself thinking that we had been too close to the blue pixel to see the picture. An enormous conversation could be taking place while I argued with the one point nearest my eye.\nThe only way to be certain of the future is to create it. A surer way is to condemn it: respond from the same fear, reproduce the same arrangement, then recognise the thing I dreaded as something I helped make.\nIt was a provocation about participation, not a promise of certainty. What did I really know about the present? Enough, I hoped, to examine my response before sending another version of the same problem into the future.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/the-future-we-create-and-condemn/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-vision-strategy-agility-and-culture","@type":"Article","name":"Vision, strategy, agility and culture","headline":"Vision, strategy, agility and culture","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["leadership","agility","cone of uncertainty","team culture","strategy","trust"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Vision, strategy, agility and culture","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/responsibility-is-not-a-title/"]},"description":"Why a plan needs time to understand the problem, room to change and a team whose trust carries more than a process can specify.","datePublished":{"@value":"2020-02-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Vision, strategy, agility and culture\n“Agile is broken.” “Culture eats strategy for breakfast.” “Vision matters.” We hear the phrases so often that they begin to resemble explanations. But what do they help us change on Monday morning?\nI do not think agility is broken. I think it is frequently misunderstood. Vision, strategy, agility and culture have different work to do, and the useful question is how they meet inside a team trying to deliver something.\nA vision needs the surrounding detail\nA bold vision should make me a little uncomfortable. It asks the team and me to go beyond what we already know how to do. That reach can be valuable, provided I also take responsibility for the details around it: what, why, how and when.\nStrategy is the work of making choices around the ambition. Inspirational quotations can remind us to choose, make trade-offs, work hard, revise after failure or look at the result. They cannot do those things for the particular problem in front of us.\nThe real world will introduce variables the plan did not account for. The larger the undertaking and the longer the timeline, the more room there is for that to happen. A roadmap is drawn while the target and our understanding of it are still moving.\nLeadership has to give people something stable enough to work towards without pretending that the surroundings have become static. The tools and the explanation matter. Repeating the vision more emphatically is not a method for absorbing a changed assumption.\nDefining the problem takes time too\nI have found planning along a logarithmic scale useful: greater uncertainty calls for coarser estimates, with more detail becoming available as the work progresses. The attraction is the recognition that the first estimate and the later one are made with different knowledge.\nDevelopers can estimate the time required to design a solution while overlooking the time required to define the problem. Those are connected pieces of work. A missing constraint can change the solution; an attempted solution can reveal that the problem was described incorrectly.\nMy rule of thumb is simple: do not make a promise at the first encounter with a problem. Ask enough to understand what the promise would include. As details emerge, the account becomes more granular. Defining and solving the problem proceed back and forth.\nThat is where agility matters. It gives us a way to learn without treating every discovery as somebody's failure to obey the original plan. The cone of uncertainty is one picture of this changing relationship between knowledge and estimation. It is useful as a reminder to examine uncertainty, not as a guarantee that uncertainty will shrink on schedule.\nA plan with room to move\nDesign and mathematics can help a plan absorb change while preserving the team's ability to execute. A dependency can be made visible. A decision can be postponed until the information needed for it arrives. Work can be divided so that revising one part does not require everyone to abandon what they were doing.\nI think of this as elasticity. The target moves, the plan absorbs some of the movement, and the team does not lose all its momentum. A pivot that repeatedly discards the team's context consumes more than the time shown in the next schedule.\nA cross-functional, self-organising team needs enough shared understanding to participate in that adjustment. People should be able to recognise why something changed and what still holds. Otherwise, self-organisation becomes a polite name for asking them to guess which instruction matters today.\nCulture carries what the process cannot repeat\nProcesses help make work consistent. They can also become mechanical. A team cannot stop to consult a policy for every small act of cooperation, every admission of uncertainty or every moment when somebody needs help.\nCulture carries some of that work. It gives people expectations about what happens when they raise a concern, revise an estimate or discover that another part of the team already solved their problem. A framework can name the behaviour; the everyday response teaches whether it is safe and useful to do it.\nWhen the stakes are high, people-operations expertise can help a team design its own working arrangements. The point is not to impose a second strategy in the name of culture. It is to help the team develop the practices through which the strategy can become ordinary work.\nIn that sense, strategy is homework I do to help the right culture become possible. The design of the work and the life of the team keep affecting one another.\nUse the information to reach an agreement\nNine women cannot deliver a baby in one month. The old project-management analogy survives because the mistake survives: adding people does not divide every duration. Some work depends on a sequence, some on accumulated understanding and some on a process that cannot be accelerated by changing the headcount.\nCollecting data is not enough. I want to use it to help the team agree on what is happening and what to do next. An observation can make a discussion more concrete than an argument over what someone intended by a phrase in a meeting.\nBut data does not interpret itself. We choose what to collect and which comparison to make. Confirmation bias can follow us into the dashboard. The useful discipline is to let the evidence change the shared account, including the leader's account, rather than use the numbers as another way to win the room.\nThe thing the framework needs\nVision supplies direction. Strategy makes choices around it. Agility lets the work learn. Culture shapes how people live with those choices and discoveries.\nNone of this works well without trust. Leadership includes building a shared vision that the team can actually carry, then supporting the people who carry it. They need room to contribute their knowledge, including knowledge that makes the original plan less comfortable.\nAs with most good things, patience is part of the job. A framework can help us practise it. It cannot practise it on our behalf.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/vision-strategy-agility-and-culture/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-a-decade","@type":"Article","name":"What a decade! Makes me wonder what is to come","headline":"What a decade! Makes me wonder what is to come","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["2010s","failure","enjoying the little things","teaching","music and art"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What a decade! Makes me wonder what is to come","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/","relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/dumb-comedy/","https://getharsh.in/blog/figuring-things-out-one-book-at-a-time/","https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/"]},"description":"Looking back on the 2010s through failure, friendship, teaching, art and the questions that survived the decade.","datePublished":{"@value":"2019-12-24","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What a decade! Makes me wonder what is to come\nA reflection from December 2019, looking back over the 2010s.\n2010. Failure first got to me. I was in class eleven.\nFailure presents a choice, although the choice depends on the circumstances. I can stay where I am, trying to escape the failure, or find another path towards what I wanted. Perhaps the encounter makes me see the situation clearly for the first time. I had seen a fair amount of failure in the years that followed. What hurt most was often the greed to have success without any of it.\nThat becomes more relevant as the thing I want to create becomes larger. But the decade was not merely an account of successes and failures. It was memories, experiences, people and ideas. It was life.\nFerris Bueller's reminder to stop and look around had stayed with me. Life was perfectly capable of moving on while I was busy preparing the account of it.\nRule 32: enjoy the little things\nSomewhere in the middle of the decade, things became particularly interesting. Zombieland supplied the plainer version of Bueller's advice: rule 32, “Enjoy the little things.” My favourite rule. At the end of 2019, I still had not watched the sequel. It had a place on the list, and I was looking forward to it.\nThe little things mattered partly because I was rather good at making the questions enormous. A couple of weeks before writing this, I had followed a train of thought through meditation, creativity, realisation, hallucination and existence. Following the thought was one capacity; keeping the distinctions clear was another. Somewhere among those hazy borders, I could lose the thread I had set out to understand.\nThat would not make me a good teacher. Clearing the haze was supposed to be the job, not adding another impressive-looking cloud to it. I needed to work on the difference. I suspected I would need rule 32 even more in the coming decade than I had in this one.\nWhat matters changes as I follow the thought\nI often follow a thought well beyond the place it first seemed useful. As I do, things I expected to matter do not always matter in the same way. Other things, initially peripheral, become difficult to ignore. There are outliers. There are always outliers.\nI wanted to be aware of what happened to me and why, without letting every event determine the entirety of my response. That is an ambition, and sometimes a privilege. Writing it down does not make it easy. Someone once told me never to underestimate nature. I try not to. A reflex can respond before I have assembled a sophisticated explanation of what needs a response.\nThen the questions grow again. Maybe nothing matters. Maybe everything does. Wait. Where was I?\nThe more I followed them, the less the decade resembled a progression towards fewer doubts. I had more questions than when it began. Existence and experience had once seemed easier to hold apart. The world had seemed simpler. That simplicity had been useful, but I was no longer sure it had been understanding.\nWhen thoughts meet the soul\nOne thought kept returning in a more lyrical form:\nWhen thoughts meet the soul, it becomes an art. When art meets sound, it becomes music. When music meets science, it becomes a mantra.\nAn artist pours something of themselves into the work. If only God can create, as the saying goes, what does it mean that people create every day? Did God create a soulmate to show us the science, or did we create science to see the art? Perhaps God poured a soul into science. Perhaps God is an art.\nI put it under a little declaration: #MusicIsJustMyMantra. The inquiry now has other forms in thinking across images, sound and sequence. The earlier words still carry something of the person asking the question before those later forms existed.\nWhat will we create?\nThat was what made the next decade interesting to me. Not merely what would happen, but what we would create within it. Which questions would survive? Which relationships would make them worth pursuing? What would become of the things we had not yet learned to notice?\nI hoped Bueller would stay beside us for as long as we needed the reminder. Stop. Look around. There may be a life happening somewhere outside the plan.\nThere was no other way but to be okay. The question was how soon I wanted to begin. ¯_(ツ)_/¯\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/blog/what-a-decade/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/","identifier":"content:getharsh-collections","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Thoughts","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","name":"Building products and teams"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-energy-efficiency","name":"Energy efficiency"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-learning-and-practice","name":"Learning and practice"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice"}}]},"description":"Questions that develop across product work, learning and creative practice.","text":"","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"Product judgment, technical research, shared responsibility and the systems that let useful work continue.","text":"A product changes the work around it. Somebody has to install it, make sense of its output, handle an exception and keep it useful after the people who built it have moved on. I learned that through physical equipment before encountering it in customer workflows and AI applications. The component was often the part I knew how to build first. Understanding the rest could change what the component ought to be.\nThe work also changed me. Building equipment drew me into the operator's day; leading development drew me into other people's judgment. Later, founding, consulting and research gave me different ways to contribute, and different limits on what a useful contribution could carry. Some of those changes need an ordered account. Others began with a particular problem that still deserves its own argument.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-building-a-place-to-think","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Building a place to think","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-from-document-to-domain","@type":"Article","name":"What does the line item mean?","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention","@type":"Article","name":"The shape of a useful intervention","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-work-that-can-travel","@type":"Article","name":"The work that can travel","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"What remains when an experiment ends","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-research-to-product-decisions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"When research changes the product","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A career through problems","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}}]}],"description":"The movement through field engineering, customer-led products, AI applications and research-informed system building.","text":"My career looks straighter in a list of roles than it felt while living it. Engineering, product management, ventures and consulting overlapped. I followed people and problems, sometimes into an arrangement that worked, sometimes into an ambition I had not understood well enough. The connections became clearer through the work, rather than through a plan I had at the beginning.\nAt first, making a measurement available opened the next problem: somebody had to understand it and act. Building the product meant bringing other people into that work. Later, customer evidence could ask for less of the system, while emergency-response work exposed how much a detector left to the people around it. Language-model applications made the gap between an impressive component and a useful result harder to see, and more important to follow.\nLater responsibilities brought technical inquiry closer to product judgment and communication. Understanding what a system could offer required following its constraints; helping someone else use that understanding required another kind of construction. Hands-on engineering stayed with me while teaching, ventures and research changed what I brought back to it.\nThe reflection that began this account looks at the life around those roles: the people I learned from, the experiments beyond work and the forces acting on both at once.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":4,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-from-the-plant-to-the-team","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"From the plant to the team","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-customer-changes-the-system","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"The customer changes the system","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-making-intelligence-usable","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Making intelligence usable","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-research-working-system","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Research and the working system","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-from-the-plant-to-the-team","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"From the plant to the team","headline":"From the plant to the team","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["Smart Joules","embedded systems","product-development leadership","shared context","JouleLabs"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/","@type":"WebPage","name":"From the plant to the team","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A career through problems","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"From the plant to the team","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/"}}]}]},"description":"A measurement problem became a product problem, and then a problem of helping other people carry the work.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"From the plant to the team\nAt Sant Parmanand Hospital in New Delhi, I could read the chiller over MODBUS and plot what it was doing. That felt like a good beginning. Arjun, Smart Joules' CEO, and I had obtained time and permission to experiment with the HVAC system. The operators, meanwhile, ran the plant through the red and green buttons familiar to them. We were looking at the same equipment through rather different accounts. My chart made a part of the operation visible, but it had not yet given the operator a reason to need it.\nI joined in November 2016 as the founding engineer, working in embedded systems. A reading arriving was an achievement, but it opened another question: what did the reading let somebody understand? The operator did not encounter a collection of protocols. They encountered equipment that served a building and a problem they needed to locate. The product had to connect the engineering account to that situation.\nMeasurement had already been part of my thesis work, Project Netra. I served as its Lead Researcher with NEFORD and ICAR-CSSRI's Regional Research Station in Lucknow from December 2015 to September 2016, then continued as a volunteer and mentor through September 2020. Working with images and embedded measurements made the path from an input to its interpretation interesting to me. Choosing a reference, selecting a region or converting a reading changes what the returned number can mean. At the plant, that path acquired another consequence: somebody needed to use it to keep equipment working.\nWhat does a blank reading mean to the operator?\nA missing value can come from the instrument, its mapping, a controller or a connection further along the path. To the person looking at the screen, it may appear simply as an absent part of the plant. Exposing the technical error is useful to an engineer; locating its consequence is useful to an operator. A diagnostic view has to make the connection between them.\nThat changed how I thought about an interface. Its job was not to present every fact the system possessed. It needed to preserve the distinctions that would change a person's next move. Was the equipment off? Was its reading unavailable? Had a command been sent without its physical result being confirmed? The answers could look similar on a chart and demand different responses in the plant.\nThe turning point for me was that product judgment was already inside the engineering. Choosing what to measure, how to explain a gap and which consequence to follow was choosing what kind of help the system could offer.\nThree months building what we already had\nMy responsibilities expanded into architecture, product direction, hiring and product-development leadership. I recruited seven initial direct reports and, through them, grew a development team of twenty-five across research, hardware, software, testing, production and design. During that period, delivery and operations, which I partly managed, grew to eighty-four people, and the company to 115. The development disciplines needed to meet around the same outcome. A component could be locally correct and still be difficult for another team to integrate or for the field team to maintain. Splitting the work into departments did not by itself carry its meaning across the split.\nOne coordination failure stayed with me: work continued for nearly three months on a duplicate service before the existing one was discovered. The difficulty was not a lack of people willing to build. It was a lack of shared context about what already existed and why another team might need it. A roadmap could name the deliverable without making that relationship visible.\nI became more interested in the explanation around the task. What problem does this part solve? Which other work does it depend on? What will another person need when they receive it? A brief, review or conversation is valuable when it carries those answers far enough that people can make decisions without repeatedly returning to the person who remembers everything.\nI still meet that difficulty when returning to my own work. The remembered answer can be clear while the grounds for it have disappeared. Shared context has to make the next decision easier, not merely preserve the conversation that once made it easy.\nMaking room for somebody new\nJouleLabs came from a related possibility: students and mentors could work on actual product problems while developing the capability to contribute. I founded and ran the programme alongside the development work. A real problem gave learning a purpose, but the purpose did not remove the need for a continuing mentor, a review or a team able to receive the result.\nTeaching also required me to make my own understanding more explicit. Something that felt obvious after months in the plant was not an adequate starting point for a new contributor. Explaining the setting, allowing a question and seeing where the explanation failed could improve the team's context as well as the learner's.\nThe work reached more than forty facilities in ten cities, with reported client energy savings above 30%. I represented Smart Joules at Shell’s Make the Future in Singapore, where the company placed third, and spoke on the panel at NREDCAP’s Powering Andhra Pradesh summit. Smart Joules received an award from Piyush Goyal for the product’s effect on energy waste. Those results belonged to the company and the people delivering and operating the service; my responsibility was to help make the product and engineering capable of supporting it.\nBy the time I left in March 2020, the responsibility had changed. I was still concerned with whether a technical path worked, but also with whether the people around it could understand, extend and sustain it. That question travelled with me. At Zenoti, it would begin from the other end: the customer could need less of the system, rather than a better explanation of all that it could do.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/from-the-plant-to-the-team/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-making-intelligence-usable","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Making intelligence usable","headline":"Making intelligence usable","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["YojnAI","DAO Studio","AI application composition","tracing and feedback","reinforcement learning from human feedback","investment banking pilots"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Making intelligence usable","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A career through problems","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Making intelligence usable","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/"}}]}]},"description":"Composing, observing and revising AI applications brought earlier product and engineering questions into a new medium.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-29","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Making intelligence usable\nA language model makes an unusual first impression: the component seems able to explain the whole system. It can interpret a request, supply an answer and sound as though it knows what should happen next. Building applications around that capability returned me to questions I had met in plants and customer workflows. Which information matters here? What actually happened? What would let somebody inspect an error or continue the work?\nI co-founded DAO Studio with Shashwat Pandey in October 2022 and led its product, technology and company work through September 2024. Alongside those responsibilities, I built YojnAI as an individual contributor. Composition, tracing and feedback were ways to give the application more structure than one impressive answer.\nThe studio began with an ambition inspired by Amul and Lijjat Papad: help people create decentralised autonomous organisations. As we explored what that would require, AI became part of the means. The mission I wrote down was to democratise AI and commoditise intelligence. YOJN, from a Sanskrit word for a unit of distance, became the name through which that idea developed. The destination was bigger than a language-model tool; the tool was a concrete place to work on explainability, controllability, decomposability and interoperability.\nThe individual-contributor and founder perspectives did not take turns neatly. Building the platform meant deciding what a unit of work should expose, then confronting what the application needed that the abstraction had left out. Leading the company meant asking whether someone else could use, maintain and pay for that arrangement. A mechanism elegant enough to explain on a whiteboard still had to survive those questions. I wanted to make the tool and understand the business it might make possible, rather than delegate one side and talk only about the other.\nWe raised ₹0.6 crore, brought in three entrepreneurs in residence and built an advisory board of five. The company reached ₹5 lakh in monthly recurring revenue and cash-flow neutrality during the pilot period. We also made three IP filings; filings are steps in protecting work, not granted patents. A community of more than a thousand people and more than five open-source projects gave the work other routes into use and discussion.\nFollowing the work between model calls\nOne design choice was to expose work before the final response. An application might have already extracted a useful term while still waiting for a clarification elsewhere. Keeping that intermediate state and giving another component a callback into the work could let a person inspect the term without rerunning the whole request. I wanted the application to be somewhere people and software could contribute, rather than an answer everybody had to wait for.\nThat ambition led into the composition model: models and tools inside agents, agents and checks inside playbooks, and an application connecting them to a particular customer's records and workflow. The abstraction had to preserve the useful differences. A missing value, a completed extraction and an action already performed could not all become indistinguishable prompt context.\nThe platform went into three pilots, in investment banking, operations and the CFO office: one application read invoices and agreements, and another worked through financial research calculations. Those were partner-owned products built with the platform, not products whose ownership followed from our having supplied the technology. That gave the application work a setting beyond a disconnected demonstration. The product question was no longer just whether a model could produce useful text. It was whether a person could work with the result: find the part that needed attention, supply a correction and choose what to use next. Tracing and feedback brought that interaction into the design. They also kept returning me to the same tension: how much structure could make the application easier to work with, before maintaining the structure became another job for the user?\nI had to move between the platform and the particular application. A useful abstraction lets different work share a mechanism without making the differences disappear. An invoice and a research question can both pass through a model, but they ask for different sources, interpretations and results. The version of an application, the path through its tools and the point where somebody intervenes give that otherwise fluid process something concrete to examine. Understanding a trace then becomes work in its own right, rather than something the log performs for us.\nThe earlier experiences were useful without supplying a ready-made answer. A plant's independent physical state is different from a language-model application's generated result. A support workflow has different dependencies from a finance task. But following the work beyond the apparent success of a component remained a productive habit. The product around the model needed its own attention.\nThe run that made a year of building feel worthwhile\nWhen asked for a “hell yeah” moment in my career, I returned to a finance-model run through YOJN. We had spent a year building the tool. I was working with finance data for the first time, adapting a Mistral 7B model quantised to four bits through reinforcement learning from human feedback. The application had to do useful work with financial material, where a fluent response could still get the numerical reasoning wrong.\nThe figures I reported from that pilot were MMLU performance comparable to GPT-3.5, a BigBench score above 70% and self-hosting at about $1.75 an hour. They describe my reported pilot result, not a benchmark comparison. The satisfaction was more immediate. The system we had spent that year building had carried the model work into an actual application. It was a litmus test of YOJN, and it remains one of my favourite moments of something first working.\nThe model's score was only part of the product question. A person still needed to inspect an answer, trace it to the financial material and correct a mistake. That was why composition, tracing and feedback belonged together in the tool. Improving a model and making its contribution usable in an application were connected jobs, with different results to examine.\nTaking that habit into another setting\nCompany leadership required a different view of the same work. A promising application needed people who could build, review and sustain it, and an arrangement in which that effort could continue. I could not stay solely inside the mechanism, but I did not want to become so far removed from it that the product was only a promise I could explain. Moving between those perspectives was part of the founder's job.\nWhat I carry into consulting is that combination. I want to understand the problem where it happens, make the important dependencies intelligible, and stay close enough to implementation to learn what the design missed. Sometimes the next useful intervention is technical. Sometimes it is a smaller promise, a clearer decision or a different division of work. The architecture is stronger when those possibilities remain available.\nThe questions that came home with me\nRawThoughts began as writing and exploration during my transition out of Smart Joules and developed into the applied practice I work through now. Some questions from those years have become more concrete there: which context helps, how a responsibility survives a conversation, and what a system needs to retain when someone returns to it. Consulting keeps those questions in contact with work whose value is judged by the people relying on the result.\nThe application question also leaves me curious about the capacities being composed. Connecting useful model calls and studying how learning units might organise are different problems, but the difference makes the crossing productive. A working application shows me where a deeper question begins; it cannot answer that question merely by working.\nTeaching and participation in the UPAI community continue the other side of the practice: helping people enter work, and letting their questions expose what my own account takes for granted. A shared context is valuable because somebody else can contribute through it, not simply because it represents my thoughts more neatly.\nI had come to language models with the habits of someone who wanted to follow a system's path. Their fluency made that habit more important, while their possibilities made the path less familiar. Building around them drew product, engineering and company responsibility together. It also left me wanting to understand more of the systems on which that work depended.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/making-intelligence-usable/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-research-working-system","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Research and the working system","headline":"Research and the working system","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["Accacia","AION","API specifications","bill of quantities","GPU clusters","engineering leadership","developer experience"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Research and the working system","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A career through problems","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Research and the working system","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/"}}]}]},"description":"Engineering leadership and product work on API agreements, building-material documents and GPU clusters, and what each taught about the working system.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-01","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Research and the working system\nA role can widen the view without making the work less technical. Product responsibility asks what is worth building; engineering leadership asks how people can build and sustain it. Research can change the terms of both questions. Those perspectives meet in the same working system.\nAfter DAO Studio, I moved through a short engagement with Specmatic in September 2024, VP Engineering at Accacia from October 2024 to February 2025, and Founding Product Manager at AION from March to November 2025. These were quite different problems. A developer wanted a dependable agreement with another service. A building-carbon workflow needed to understand what a document actually described. An AI team needed compute that could complete its work. Joining each organisation meant entering a history and vocabulary I had not chosen.\nAgreements that can meet the code\nAt Specmatic, I explored how AI could help teams keep an API specification and its implementation together. Generating a plausible specification was the easy ambition to describe. The more useful question was where developers could agree on a change, discover what it would break and test the agreement before other teams depended on it.\nMy product work moved towards the pull request as that meeting place: a proposed code change, its corresponding specification and the checks that made their relationship examinable. That was a narrower entry than asking an agent to absorb every conversation around a project. It gave the ambition a recognisable place in a developer's day. AI could help developers keep that agreement as the code changed.\nI also built a reflection agent for code and structured OpenAPI and gRPC specifications. That implementation and the broader workflow proposals are different parts of the account: building the agent did not by itself establish that every proposed collaboration path had been delivered.\nThe document had another job\nAt Accacia, a bill of quantities sat between a building project and decisions about materials, carbon and procurement. Extracting the rows did not settle what they meant. A quantity needed a unit; a description needed enough context to identify the material; a proposed transformation needed someone able to recognise a wrong interpretation. The line item was a domain object before it was a convenient piece of model input.\nLeading engineering brought that problem together with the less glamorous work that lets a team change a system safely: explicit frontend/backend contracts, technical-debt decisions, tests and release responsibility. I also worked directly on role-based access control and audit logging. The hands-on work mattered to me. It kept the distance between a proposed engineering practice and the implementation carrying it short enough to examine.\nThe bill-of-quantities work covered more than 8,000 lines, with reported conversion accuracy above 90%. Alongside it, testing time fell by 70%, test coverage reached 60%, sprint velocity rose by 35%, and the beta arrived two quarters early. I built an LLM streamer library and led the engineering organisation through SOC 2 Type I and ISO/IEC 27001, issued in January 2025.\nThe domain also changed what a good engineering conversation sounded like. A row could be perfectly parsed and still be the wrong material; a test could confirm the transformation without confirming the interpretation. The team needed places where domain judgment and software behaviour could meet. That made contracts and tests more useful to me as shared working agreements than as a count to improve. The question was what another person could now change with confidence, and what still needed the specialist's eye.\nInfrastructure has readers as well as users\nThe AION work brought two views closer together for me: the machine with its memory, communication and scheduling constraints, and the person waiting for an experiment to finish or an application to answer. Moving between product questions, technical research and developer experience made the distance between those views part of the work itself.\nMy cluster work reached 49.87% Model FLOPs Utilization on H100 and H200 systems: roughly half the hardware's theoretical arithmetic capacity was doing useful model computation. Communication between GPUs mattered too. The cluster reached 472–477 GB/s reported bus bandwidth in NCCL, the library coordinating that communication. A model spread across several GPUs depends on both the work each one can do and how well they work together.\nThe service also needed to keep a promise to the person using it. I delivered Guaranteed Start Windows and procedures for tearing down compute resources when their work ended, built a Go notification service, and defined service commitments and operations tracking. With Customer Success, I led GDPR and SOC 2 compliance work and delivered the terms of service and privacy policy.\nI rebuilt the website twice, reaching about 0.05-second largest contentful paint, the time until the largest visible image or text block appears. I also built analytics connecting campaigns to GPU-hour use and an asynchronous product-management agent. That connected the work of bringing someone to the service with what they actually came there to do.\nI led the research-led content programme, with fifteen article briefs and eight substantive drafts, and wrote the Economics of AI whitepaper.\nIn that programme, the same difficult subject had to become intelligible to a reader, useful to a product conversation and workable for another contributor. A faster isolated operation might still leave the developer's job unfinished. An accurate explanation might still leave a reader unable to choose. The explanation had to connect the mechanism to the decision facing its reader.\nLearning enough to change the decision\nTechnical fluency matters to me because it changes what I can ask. A diagram can make two providers look interchangeable until the workload needs something only one can express. A document-processing pipeline can return neat fields while leaving the meaning of the material unresolved. A simpler developer interface can still ask the user to reconstruct the difficult part of the workflow themselves.\nI want to get far enough into the mechanism to recognise those differences. The aim is not to become the specialist in every part of a system. It is to understand the point at which a technical assumption changes the product, and to ask for the comparison that would make the decision clearer. Research becomes product work when it can change the boundary, the sequence or the promise, rather than simply decorate the proposal with more background.\nCompute made that relationship especially interesting to me. A nominally faster device can leave a job waiting for memory, communication or available capacity. A system can process more work while offering a worse service to the person waiting. Following those differences took my own inquiry from hardware towards the economic arrangement around it. The larger question did not arrive separately from the engineering one; it appeared as I followed what the engineering could make possible.\nThe content programme brought another consequence into view: an explanation has to travel without its original author standing beside it. A writer needs enough of the mechanism to develop the argument; an engineer needs enough of the proposed comparison to challenge it. That connects explanation to leadership for me. A brief, a handover or a research collaboration becomes useful when another person can carry the work and judge what it needs next.\nBringing the questions into my own construction\nMy present consulting practice is through RawThoughts. I stay interested in the whole path: the decision, the software, the people using it and the point at which the result can be examined. A model can be useful within that path without being the right method for every step. A deterministic calculation, a recoverable operation and a person's judgment may need to meet rather than be replaced by one fluent answer.\nAlongside that work, I am building an environment in which context, responsibilities and capabilities can remain available across sessions. Pale gives my goals a path across applications; Scope brings an agent the context it needs from what Pale and Weave hold; Weave connects relationships with accountable work; Gazette makes connected knowledge available to readers. Its purpose is to let work continue across those settings with less reconstruction, while leaving me attention for the decisions and inquiries I came to them for.\nI want my practical work to keep meeting the questions that led me to it. Sometimes that means improving an implementation. Sometimes it means narrowing the problem or changing the representation. The inquiry continues because another setting can reveal a consequence I had not yet understood, not because every previous setting was secretly preparing me for the present one.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/research-and-the-working-system/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-customer-changes-the-system","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"The customer changes the system","headline":"The customer changes the system","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["Zenoti","customer-led product development","Sachait","emergency-response workflows","product simplification"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The customer changes the system","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A career through problems","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"The customer changes the system","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/"}}]}]},"description":"Customer-led simplification and emergency-response work changed where I looked for the useful product.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-19","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The customer changes the system\nAfter Smart Joules, I was exploring different ways to work. Jobs, advice, partnerships and ventures overlapped; I was not yet moving through a settled sequence towards the practice I have today. Two settings from that period made the product question more demanding. At Zenoti, the customer needed a simpler route through a broad suite. At Sachait, a detected event needed an entire response around it. One problem asked me to remove complexity; the other asked me to understand the work a component had left out.\nThe customer who needed less software\nI was the first Product Manager hired into Zenoti’s Small Business Unit, working there from December 2020 to March 2022 across support, onboarding and taxation for small businesses in the US and Canada. Support data, product analytics, surveys and conversations gave me different ways into the customer's experience. A ticket could show where someone was stuck; talking to them could reveal what they had been trying to accomplish before the product made them stop.\nThe suite contained capabilities useful to a much broader set of businesses. Carrying all of them into the small-business experience asked the customer to absorb decisions their own operation did not require. The roadmap therefore changed towards fit: retain the work that mattered and reduce the configuration, navigation and translation around it. The revised experience reached customers in under a year, including self-onboarding and streamlined taxation work. Buddy, the AI-first design system, brought analytics and assistance into that experience. Customer satisfaction rose by 35% across that work.\nThis altered what I considered an ambitious product decision. Removing a feature can require a deeper understanding of the customer than adding one. The team has to know which task remains possible, which exception still needs a path and where an apparently convenient default would hide a consequential choice. Technical fluency helps explain the dependency; product judgment decides how much of it the customer should have to manage.\nThen there was the person responding to an emergency\nFrom December 2020 to July 2022, I worked with the Sachait team, first as an adviser and then as a cofounder leading product and engineering. The work continued alongside Zenoti. The company had been Sachait Elecbits; during my involvement, the Elecbits partnership ended and it was formally renamed Sachait Technologies Pvt. Ltd. I joined an existing service and worked with its operations data, neighbourhood watch, the 112 authorities and pilot customers in the Lucknow–Kanpur region to build a technology-enabled emergency-response workflow.\nHere, making the detector better was only one part of the product. A person needed enough context to judge an event, another person might need to continue from that judgment, and the situation could change while the system was passing it along. The result was experienced as a service, not as the output of an isolated model. Interface, evidence and handoff decisions shaped what the model's result could become.\nThe work covered more than 7,500 emergency scenarios and a model with more than 7,500 parameters; those are separate counts. The reported results included 85% fewer false positives and pilot response in under five minutes. The service reached ₹4 lakh in monthly recurring revenue with 90% retention. A detector’s improvement and a service’s continued use were different kinds of evidence, and both mattered to whether the work could continue.\nThat setting gave a different meaning to simplicity. Fewer steps can help somebody act under stress, but omitting a necessary distinction can make the next decision harder. An operator needs to distinguish an event they have reviewed from one whose evidence they could not obtain. The people coordinating a response need to know what remains unresolved, not only that a notification was sent. I exited through a patent acquisition, with safe returns for investors and cofounders, and the work continued as YOJN Care at DAO Studio. The acquired IP concerned a patent application; the transaction is not a claim that a patent had been granted.\nWhere the useful product ends\nThe connection between these experiences is not that the systems were alike. It is that the useful product extended beyond the component or screen I was initially looking at. Onboarding included migration and provider dependencies. Emergency response included evidence, people and continuity. A better interface had to change the burden of the work, rather than merely hide it.\nBuilding ventures added another set of questions. Who would adopt the system, who would support it and what could sustain the service once an enthusiastic first demonstration was over? A set of proposals from 2022 explored how consulting, education and other programmes might reinforce one another. Not all those arrangements endured. The gap between a useful problem and a sustainable vehicle became a question I had to take seriously.\nProduct management and engineering became harder to separate in my practice. I needed the mechanism to understand what could change, and the customer's situation to understand what was worth changing. A product decision could ask for less software or for a larger service around it. Learning which situation I was in mattered more than starting with the technology I wanted to build.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/the-customer-changes-the-system/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"A career through problems","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-building-a-place-to-think","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"Building a place to think","headline":"Building a place to think","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["working environment","working with agents","Pale","Scope","Weave","Gazette"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Building a place to think","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Building a place to think","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/"}}]}]},"description":"What a working environment must remember so that moving between inquiries does not mean beginning again.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-09-28","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Building a place to think\nI do not want to choose between being an engineer, studying a mathematical question and making something whose value is harder to put in a specification. These activities ask different things of me. The difficulty is returning to each without spending the first part of the session reconstructing the person who was doing it last time. What had I understood? Which explanation had failed? What was the next useful thing to try?\nA conversation can hold an extraordinary amount of that context while it is happening. Later, the important distinction is buried between an abandoned approach and a confident answer that turned out to be wrong. Saving the conversation keeps the words. It does not necessarily keep a usable position from which to continue. My interest in a working environment for people and agents begins with that small, recurring cost of returning.\nThe August 2025 design for the getHarsh engine carried a further ambition. One publishing engine would connect writing across five domains, carrying an idea's context, structure and relationships alongside its words. The hypothesis was that this richer trace could become useful training material for AI, preserving how ideas developed across subjects rather than only their finished expression. The proposed behavioural layer would add which ideas were revisited and how the work unfolded; capturing that behaviour and learning from it were future work.\nThe thought needs somewhere to go\nMy publication work gives me a very ordinary example. An article has a source folder, a place in an authored collection and an address on a site. Those three things can be different. Explaining that in a conversation is easy; keeping it intelligible when another agent resumes the work is harder. If the agent derives the public address from the nearest project folder, it can create an entirely new section I never intended. Saving the discussion is less useful than putting the distinction where the next contributor will encounter it before making that choice.\nThe same difficulty appears when implementation exposes an exception a representation cannot express. The discovery belongs with the representation and its consequences, not solely in the chat where I noticed it. Next week I might arrive through the code; somebody else might arrive through the research question. Both should be able to find the exception without reading everything I said that evening.\nThat does not require every conversation to become a permanent document. A corrected explanation can replace an inadequate one. A failed approach is worth retaining when the reason it failed will prevent the next person from repeating it. An unresolved question needs enough of its setting to remain a question rather than become a free-floating task. The useful result is a smaller account that holds more understanding, not a larger warehouse of transcripts.\nThis is the problem I am working on through Scope. Even a well-organised source can be offered at the wrong moment. Knowing that an agent retrieved it, read it or repeated it does not tell me whether it helped. The context also includes the people around the work: how somebody prefers to communicate, which decision they carry, what a project means to me and what has changed in the relationship. An agent can find the right file and still miss all of that. The interesting feedback comes from the work: was an error avoided, was a decision better grounded, could someone continue correctly? I want memory that becomes useful through that comparison, not memory whose success is measured by how much it has accumulated.\nRemembering and doing are different kinds of continuity\nUnderstanding the position of a project does not finish its next piece of work. A decision may require me; an investigation may be something an agent can take on; a commitment may belong to another person. Their connection needs to remain intelligible when the people or tools change.\nI want to know, when I come back, whether the conversation produced a suggestion, a decision or work somebody agreed to carry. They can sound similar in a transcript and lead to quite different mornings. I might be able to start a small implementation immediately, while an investigation needs a better question and an obligation is waiting for someone else's answer. Weave grows around that transition, bringing the organisation’s commercial relationships, evidence and work into one world for people and agents. The conversation can remain informal without making the responsibility disappear inside it.\nThen there is the work itself. Understanding the next step is useful only if I can reach the capability that can perform it, inspect the result and reconnect it to what I was trying to do. I am building that operating side through Pale. I want the experience to feel like working with a calm, organised team: asking for help, carrying a goal across applications, examining a result and returning to the decisions that need me. The complexity should buy that ordinary experience, not become the activity I spend the day performing.\nThe world includes more than my projects. People close to me have their own responsibilities and decisions, and some consequences land on more than one of us. A company and a client each have their own work and private context. I want to move among those settings without asking one account, one employer or one application to own the whole picture. Their connections should make collaboration easier while leaving each person room to act as themselves.\nPaleUniverse is how I want to inspect that picture: see the world the description produces, move back to its sources and examine what a proposed change would affect. A task card can help me continue one responsibility; sometimes I need to see how the whole arrangement fits. That is the experience the structure has to serve.\nI can begin by declaring the people, responsibilities and capabilities this environment may work with. A compiled generation gives those choices a frame I can inspect; the work inside it can still change. When it reveals a distinction the frame cannot express, I want to examine that change rather than let a fluent answer quietly redefine the situation. That makes this part of the environment buildable now while the larger question of changing descriptions remains open. HENA takes the ambition toward intelligence whose units, communication, memory and hierarchy can organise through learning. If that architecture becomes useful, some structure I currently have to describe explicitly could be learned beneath the same working experience. That is a reason to keep the practical system and the deeper research in conversation.\nEnough structure to stop rebuilding the room\nThere is a danger in this ambition. I could spend more time describing the working environment than working in it. The structure has to repay the attention it asks for. A frequently used relationship may deserve careful representation; a one-off question may need no more than a clear note and a link.\nThat is why the cost of understanding interests me as a comparison, not just a slogan about saving tokens. Preparation, maintenance and correction count alongside the time saved on the next return. A beautifully declared but stale world is another reconstruction problem. It can be worse because its apparent order makes the wrong account easier to trust.\nThe same restraint applies to the intelligence inside it. Sometimes I need arithmetic. Sometimes a search, a rule or a small learned judgment is enough. The interesting choice is the one that lets the work continue well, rather than the one that lets every step announce how intelligent the environment is. Giving judgment a particular place in the design lets me change the method without rebuilding the purpose around one model.\nMaking thought available to someone else\nWriting introduces another return: the reader arrives without the conversations that made an idea familiar to me. The finished argument has to carry its own premises, and it may need to open into a technical explanation, a different perspective or a longer inquiry. Those connections should deepen the thought rather than turn the page into a tour of my folders.\nThis is part of why I am building Gazette. One article can keep its own home while a reader reaches it through several questions. I can develop a personal argument here, a mechanism through RawThoughts and a formal problem through Causality without making three copies of the same work. The crossing becomes interesting when the other form can do something the present one cannot.\nAn executable sketch offers a different kind of encounter. With RawFish, assumptions about interaction can be made explicit enough to run and vary. A result can expose what those assumptions produced. That is useful before it is a prediction about anyone: a way to find the questions hidden inside an attractive account.\nI want an environment in which a question can travel through these forms without losing where it came from or being forced to remain in one of them. The reward would not be thinking about fewer things. It would be spending less of the day putting the room back together, and more of it doing the work that made me enter it.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/building-a-place-to-think/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-from-document-to-domain","@type":"Article","name":"What does the line item mean?","headline":"What does the line item mean?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["bill of quantities","document interpretation","domain modelling","quantity units","human correction","data provenance","AI document workflows"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What does the line item mean?","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"What does the line item mean?","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/"}}]}]},"description":"Turning a document into useful domain information requires units, context, relationships and correction, not merely accurate extraction.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-03-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What does the line item mean?\nAt Accacia, I worked on converting bills of quantities into bills of materials. Reading the rows was one part of the job. A quantity needed a unit, and a description needed enough context to identify the material. Extracting the right characters could still leave us with the wrong interpretation.\nTake the number twelve. It might mean twelve boxes ordered, twelve pieces received or twelve units left after an earlier delivery. Put twelve in a neatly labelled field and it looks ready to calculate with. But which calculation? The unit and the event decide that. This is where a document-processing product has to understand more than the document’s layout.\nTwelve packs of what?\nTake a constructed example. A small workshop orders twelve packs of fasteners. Its planning sheet assumes a hundred pieces per pack, so it derives a total of 1,200 pieces. The supplier's document also says twelve packs, but a product revision has changed the pack size to eighty. The order count still matches. The derived quantity is now 960. A system comparing only the two quantity cells reports agreement while missing the change that matters to the workshop.\nInterpretation\tPack count\tPieces per pack\tDerived piece count\t\nPlanning assumption\t12\t100\t1,200\t\nRevised supplier definition\t12\t80\t960\t\nThe arithmetic is easy once the meaning is settled. The difference is 240 pieces, despite perfect agreement about the twelve packs. Identifying which packaging definition applies, whether it was current for this order, and whether the document describes a request or a delivery determines which calculation belongs in the work. More fluent extraction cannot supply those relationships merely by producing a convincing sentence.\nSuppose the workshop later receives a partial delivery. The planning sheet still needs the full quantity, purchasing tracks what was ordered, and the person preparing the work needs to know what is physically available. These views can contain different numbers without any of them being a bad transcription. A domain model gives those numbers different jobs. Flattening them into a universal quantity field makes the disagreement disappear from the representation while leaving it alive in the work.\nThe example is deliberately small. The same difficulty grows when a line item combines material, dimensions, installation and a service obligation. A description useful to the buyer may not be the description useful to a manufacturing estimate. Translation between them is a substantive interpretation, not a prettier copy of the original row.\nA neat table can conceal the missing relationship\nStructured output is valuable because it makes information easier to inspect, compare and calculate. Its usefulness can also make it persuasive. Once a sentence has become a named field, it begins to look as though the naming question has been settled. A model may select a plausible unit, infer a missing category or expand an abbreviation. Each choice can be reasonable; some choices will matter enough that they should remain inspectable.\nFor the workshop, an unknown pack size should not silently become a hundred because that is the common case. The system can preserve the extracted count, show the missing conversion and ask for the particular product definition. That lets useful work continue without confusing a partly understood record with a completed estimate. It is a better product experience than either making the user distrust every field or presenting all fields with equal confidence.\nThe distinctions also change evaluation. Reading the right characters, assigning the right field, interpreting the unit and deriving the useful total are separate points at which work can succeed or fail. An overall accuracy number can conceal a small family of errors that reverses a downstream decision. A practical evaluation includes those cases, because the question is whether the system helped the work rather than whether most cells looked correct.\nThe application around the model has a large role here. It decides which context is available, how an interpretation is displayed, when a missing relation interrupts the calculation and how a person's correction changes the rest of the result. A more capable model may reduce the difficulty. It does not remove the need to design that interaction.\nA correction has consequences beyond one cell\nIf a person corrects the pack size from a hundred to eighty, the product can treat it as a local text edit. The visible total then changes, but a cached estimate elsewhere may remain at 1,200. Alternatively, it can understand that the total depends on the packaging definition and make the affected results visible. The second response makes the correction consequential rather than cosmetic.\nThe human contribution is more informative still when the reason survives. Perhaps the supplier changed the product. Perhaps the order used an old definition. Perhaps the person corrected an extraction error rather than a domain assumption. Those are different problems to repair. Training on the corrected value alone can lose the distinction, especially if the number eighty becomes a pattern detached from the product and version to which it belongs.\nProvenance supplies part of that structure. The W3C PROV overview describes information about the entities, activities and people involved in producing a result. In this example, the source document, the selected product definition, the transformation and the correction are all relevant to understanding the total. Recording their relationship gives someone a way to investigate the result. It does not make the interpretation correct by itself.\nNor does retaining everything automatically produce a useful account. The reviewer needs to see what changed this result, not wade through every unrelated event in the system. That is why useful context is a harder target than stored context. The package definition is quiet information until the count needs to become a different quantity. Then it can be indispensable.\nThe domain expert changes the product's question\nAn expert may not correct the number at all. They may reject the proposed comparison. Perhaps the fasteners are an incompatible grade, or a different task requires another material even though the dimensions match. The expert is supplying a relationship the current model did not know to ask about. Capturing only whether they accepted the output would make the feedback much less useful than the judgment itself.\nThis is where product work and domain work become closely coupled. The software should make recurring interpretation cheaper without making the expert repeat their whole discipline for every document. The expert should be able to identify a particular ambiguity, qualify a mapping or explain why a seemingly reasonable substitution fails. Over time those contributions can improve definitions, examples, evaluation cases and the model's assistance. Their usefulness comes from preserving what the correction was about.\nThe product also needs a readable result for someone who did not watch that process happen. A downstream person may need a usable materials total, while a reviewer needs the source and derivation. These are complementary views of the same calculation. A clean report should not acquire a different meaning simply because the detailed view was inconvenient to render.\nInformation becomes usable in a relationship\nThe workshop still ordered twelve packs. What changed was our understanding of what those packs contained, which definition applied and what had actually arrived. Now the planner can calculate a materials total, purchasing can follow the order and the person doing the work can see what is available. Their different answers have become useful rather than contradictory.\nThat is the opportunity I see in document systems: help people arrive at an account they can use and correct together. The extracted row is a beginning. The product earns its place when a specialist can explain why eighty applies here, the correction reaches the estimates that depended on a hundred, and the next person can understand the result without repeating the investigation.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/from-document-to-domain/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-research-to-product-decisions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"When research changes the product","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"When research changes the product","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}}]}],"description":"From a technical promise to a useful measure and the developer's complete operation.","text":"A technical explanation can change what a product ought to promise. That is the crossing I want to follow: not understanding the technology for its own sake, or gathering enough background to make an existing proposal sound more convincing, but discovering that the useful product is different from the one we first imagined.\nA hypothetical inference service gives the question something concrete to work on. An interactive assistant and an overnight archive both ask for a machine, while needing different work from it. Once that difference becomes visible, an impressive performance measure may stop describing what the person needs. Following the developer beyond the demonstration then exposes choices about readiness, cost and recovery that the successful response left out.\nThe investigation keeps changing its object. A mechanism changes the promise; a comparison tests the promise; a complete operation asks whether the person can rely on it. Research becomes consequential through those changes, rather than through the volume of explanation the team has accumulated.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-promise-inside-the-interface","@type":"Article","name":"The promise inside the interface","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-the-measure-leaves-out","@type":"Article","name":"What the measure leaves out","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-past-the-first-successful-call","@type":"Article","name":"Past the first successful call","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-past-the-first-successful-call","@type":"Article","name":"Past the first successful call","headline":"Past the first successful call","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["developer experience","inference service","job recovery","provisioning","infrastructure abstraction","technical product design"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Past the first successful call","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"When research changes the product","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Past the first successful call","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/"}}]}]},"description":"The developer's complete operation reveals product choices that a successful inference demonstration can leave invisible.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-12-17","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Past the first successful call\nA successful inference response is a meaningful beginning. It can also be the point at which a product demonstration stops following the developer. The next task may be to run a repeatable evaluation, recover a stopped job, inspect cost or move the result into an existing application. Those tasks can expose a different product from the one the demonstration showed.\nThe hypothetical inference team has understood why an interactive assistant and an overnight archive ask different things of the same machine. It has also found a measure that follows useful work rather than only completed answers. Now the promise has to survive a whole operation, including the parts where nothing useful has happened yet.\nActivity before a usable start\nA particularly revealing case is a coordinated job waiting for all its resources. Several devices may have started while one necessary device or storage connection remains unavailable. The interface can display activity; the intended work still cannot begin. The usable start gives provisioning a more demanding meaning. This is where an apparently operational detail becomes a product decision: what exactly was sold, when can the user rely on it, and what can they do if it is incomplete?\nAn incomplete environment also changes the meaning of recovery. Retrying the operation without knowing which resources already exist can repeat cost or leave another half-formed attempt behind. The developer needs an account of the current state and a way to continue or stop it. A response that only reports success or failure may be simpler to display while leaving the harder decision entirely with the person.\nThe answer is not to expose every internal setting to every user. An engineer debugging the environment, a manager approving the expense and an automated caller composing the next operation need different views. They still refer to the same work. A useful abstraction lets each see the choices relevant to their responsibility and preserves a route into the detail when the simplified account stops being sufficient. Hiding a required choice does not make the choice disappear; it merely moves its consequence to a less convenient moment.\nA useful request has to become a definite choice\nThe same pressure appears in a natural-language interface. “Finish this experiment by Friday” invites a person into the work without requiring them to begin with provider APIs. It still needs to become a definite proposal they can inspect: what will run, at what cost, under which constraints, and where the result will go. I am interested in making that translation useful to an engineer, a business owner and an agent without letting each receive a different account of the same decision. The product abstraction has to carry the choice through to its consequence.\nA deadline alone cannot determine the model, the acceptable quality or the budget. Giving the system more freedom can make the interface inviting, but somebody still has to choose how those constraints relate. The proposal is where that choice becomes available for agreement. The execution record is where the participants can find out what happened to it.\nThat is the part of technical product work I find most alive. A person starts with something they want to accomplish. Research makes an invisible constraint visible. A design then gives the person a better way to work with that constraint, perhaps by changing the workflow, perhaps by making a smaller promise, perhaps by providing a capability that did not previously exist. The technical knowledge has become useful without having to become the centre of every conversation.\nWhat the operation teaches the interface\nIf an apparently portable interface makes every unsupported case difficult to diagnose, the team has learned that its abstraction is carrying too little meaning. If a job cannot resume without reconstructing the whole environment, recovery is part of the product problem, not merely a future infrastructure improvement. Following the operation changes the acceptance conditions for what the team can sensibly offer.\nThe result has to reach the product's supported path, defaults and promise. A polished description of portability cannot substitute for an intelligible unsupported case. A demonstration of one answer cannot substitute for a developer being able to repeat, inspect and recover their work. The experiment has found its way back when those differences alter what the product actually asks of its user.\nI do not see research and execution as opposing temperaments. Research opens the range of possible explanations; execution makes a particular explanation meet a consequence. Product work keeps passing between them. The question is not whether the team has learned a great deal about the technology. It is whether someone can now do something useful that the earlier account made difficult, misleading or impossible.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/past-the-first-successful-call/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-research-to-product-decisions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"When research changes the product","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-promise-inside-the-interface","@type":"Article","name":"The promise inside the interface","headline":"The promise inside the interface","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["technical product strategy","inference workloads","product promises","PagedAttention"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The promise inside the interface","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"When research changes the product","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"The promise inside the interface","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/"}}]}]},"description":"How understanding an inference mechanism changes the workload, comparison and promise a product team can sensibly offer.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-12-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The promise inside the interface\nThere is a satisfying moment in technical research when a mechanism finally becomes intelligible. A diagram that seemed ornamental becomes a working explanation; the strange term turns out to name a problem you already understand. It is tempting to take that moment as the end of the investigation. In product work, it is often the beginning. The next question is what this understanding changes for somebody trying to do something.\nI care about that crossing because product decisions often contain technical assumptions before anybody calls them technical. “Let people bring their own model” sounds like a welcoming promise. It also assumes a way to load the model, enough memory to run it, compatible operations, an acceptable response time and a useful account of what happens when one of those conditions fails. The promise has already made an architectural commitment. Research gives us a chance to notice it before a customer discovers it for us.\nThe same machine, two different products\nConsider a hypothetical team offering model inference. One prospective user wants an interactive assistant whose response begins quickly. Another wants to process a large archive overnight. Both ask for access to the same kind of accelerator. A hardware catalogue makes their requests look similar; the work makes them different. The assistant cares about waiting and interruption as experienced by a person. The archive job can tolerate more delay between individual items if the whole batch finishes within its window. A design that improves aggregate throughput can help the second and frustrate the first.\nNow imagine the team reading the PagedAttention paper. Kwon and colleagues describe how allocating attention-cache state in blocks can reduce wasted memory and allow more requests to be served together. The interesting product consequence is not merely that an engine became faster. Memory management changes how many requests can coexist, while scheduling and the workload still shape the response people experience. A reported improvement belongs to the paper's tested setting. It supplies a mechanism to investigate in our setting, not an automatic multiplier for the service we want to offer.\nThe two users give the team different questions to test:\nProduct use\tWhat the user needs\tComparison the team should make\t\nInteractive assistant\tA useful response beginning promptly under arriving demand\tFirst-response delay and completion behaviour at declared input/output lengths and loads\t\nOvernight archive\tThe declared batch completed within its window\tCompleted work, elapsed time and recovery across that batch\t\nThis changes the next conversation. Input lengths, response lengths and arrival patterns matter alongside the accelerator. A model upload needs a selected execution path, not just a place to store its weights. Interactive and batch demand may require different admission and scheduling choices. The mechanism has given the product team a concrete comparison to ask for before promising one pool for both.\nThe commercial comparison changes too. A low rate for hardware that takes longer, interrupts more often or requires substantial adaptation may produce a costly result. The relevant purchase becomes the completed job, with the time and conditions that make it useful. Technical research and pricing research meet at that object. Neither can settle the comparison alone.\nFar enough into the mechanism\nA product person does not need to become the best kernel engineer in the room to recognise that kernel performance and service performance are different questions. They do need enough understanding to hear when an answer belongs to the wrong question. That middle ground is demanding. It asks for more than collecting explanations and less than pretending to own every technical specialty.\nI find a mechanism most useful when I can follow it into a failure. If memory is the constraint, what happens as the requests become longer? If communication is the constraint, what happens when work spreads across machines? If a benchmark looks excellent, what was actually completed, and which requests never reached the result? Those questions turn a literature survey into possible experiments. They also let an engineer disagree precisely: perhaps the workload is different, the assumption is wrong or the proposed measurement would conceal the bottleneck.\nThe disagreement can alter the scope. A service might begin with a narrow, well-understood model and workload rather than a universal compatibility claim. It might expose a capability check before accepting a job. It might make export and recovery part of the first user journey rather than leave them as later infrastructure work. The smallest useful product is not necessarily the one with the fewest screens. It is the one whose promise has become specific enough to carry.\nThere is a counterpressure worth keeping. Research can also become a way to postpone choosing anything. Every paper opens another literature, every limitation another architecture, every promising idea another future product. A practical investigation needs a live decision: something that would change if one explanation were right and another were wrong. The decision gives the reading a direction without requiring every interesting question to become a roadmap item.\nIn this case, understanding the mechanism has made the decision more definite. Two people asking for the same machine may need different products. We can now test the distinction, rather than let the hardware name settle it for us.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/the-promise-inside-the-interface/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-research-to-product-decisions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"When research changes the product","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-the-measure-leaves-out","@type":"Article","name":"What the measure leaves out","headline":"What the measure leaves out","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["product evaluation","task completion","technical proxies","workload comparison"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What the measure leaves out","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"When research changes the product","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What the measure leaves out","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/"}}]}]},"description":"A worked inference example in which more completed answers produce less useful work, and what that changes in product judgment.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-12-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What the measure leaves out\nA team can improve the number it watches and make the product less useful. The uncomfortable part is that the improvement may be real. More answers arrive. More tokens are generated. Nothing in the measurement has to be fabricated for the conclusion to be wrong.\nFor the hypothetical interactive assistant, suppose the business objective is to help a person finish a task during the conversation. The team uses completed answers per minute as an early technical proxy. Batching more requests improves that number, so the new configuration looks like progress. But a person who has already abandoned the conversation cannot use the late answer. The proxy has stopped following the reason for improving it.\nMore answers, less useful work\nAn invented comparison makes the divergence visible. Give both configurations the same hundred arriving requests and the same observation window. Set the response-time requirement before the test, and review whether each answer helps complete its task rather than counting any returned text as useful.\nResult from the same hundred requests\tFirst configuration\tMore aggressive batching\t\nAnswers completed within the observation window\t90\t95\t\nAnswers arriving within the response-time requirement\t80\t60\t\nAnswers both timely and useful for the task\t72\t54\t\nThe second configuration completes more answers, yet delivers fewer useful answers while the person can still act on them. The comparison needs the whole arriving cohort, including unfinished requests, rather than only the responses that happen to succeed. The revised product measure is timely, useful task completion; throughput, queue time and answer quality help explain what moves it. None of those explanatory measures has to disappear, but none can stand in for the objective on its own.\nThe distinction also changes how a team investigates the result. If timely answers fall, the scheduling choice deserves attention. If timely answers arrive but do not help, the task or answer quality deserves attention. Collapsing those failures into one completion count makes two different product problems look like the same technical problem. Keeping the whole arriving cohort visible prevents the requests that failed to finish from quietly leaving the comparison.\nFor the archive user, the response-time requirement may be beside the point. A batch that finishes within its overnight window can benefit from the same scheduling choice. The discovery should change the offer or the configuration offered to each user, not become a verdict that batching is good or bad. This is the translation I want product research to accomplish: understand the business purpose, make a technical comparison, notice where the proxy diverges, and change the decision while there is still time to do so.\nThe task can be wrong too\nThe task itself may need revision. In an annotation programme, a category can be applied consistently while failing to preserve the distinction the eventual model needs. The research question then concerns what the label lets the next system learn, rather than how rapidly more labels can be produced. That is another place where product judgment enters technical work early: defining the useful output changes the data, the contributor's task and the evaluation together.\nAn experiment that invalidates an attractive product assumption can be valuable even when it produces no launchable feature. If a new serving method helps only the overnight workload, the team has learned how to separate its users more honestly. If a category makes labelling easy while losing the distinction needed downstream, the team has learned that its definition of the work needs revision. More efficient execution of the original task would not resolve either problem.\nThat learning needs a way back into the product. Otherwise the team can preserve the report while continuing to sell the assumption it disproved. A changed acceptance condition, a narrower supported path, a different default or a revised commercial promise makes the investigation consequential. Making a product smaller can then be the result of deeper understanding rather than reduced ambition.\nI want the measure to remain close enough to the purpose that an unwelcome result can still change our minds. It should help us see where the work has become useful, not merely where the machinery has become busy.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/what-the-measure-leaves-out/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-research-to-product-decisions","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"When research changes the product","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/research-that-changes-the-product/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention","@type":"Article","name":"The shape of a useful intervention","headline":"The shape of a useful intervention","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["product advisory","architectural review","booking confirmation","appointment booking"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The shape of a useful intervention","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The shape of a useful intervention","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/"}}]}]},"description":"A booking disagreement shows how advisory work can turn an attractive redesign into a commitment the team can actually carry.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-09-09","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The shape of a useful intervention\nImagine a small organisation struggling with appointments. Its team wants to rebuild the booking system. They have a plausible proposal: separate services for availability, reservations, notifications and payment. The diagram is attractive and each component has a clear technical role. Yet the complaint that started the discussion is that people arrive expecting appointments the organisation cannot honour.\nSeveral different difficulties could produce that experience. Perhaps availability updates too slowly. Perhaps a notification describes a request as a confirmed appointment. Perhaps the organisation accepts reservations faster than staff can complete them. Perhaps people deliberately overbook because the existing cancellation pattern makes an empty slot costly. A new architecture can address some of these conditions. It can also preserve them behind better interfaces.\nA useful review begins by following one booking into the disagreement. Suppose the system has recorded a request, while the visitor believes a place has been confirmed. The organisation has not yet assigned the required person or resource. In this constructed case, the first intervention may be to make the commitment explicit: when does a request become something the visitor can rely on, and who or what can make that transition? The answer affects the message, the data model, the cancellation path and the workload the organisation accepts. It is a product decision with architectural consequences.\nThat is where I would want the review to land. A notification and the allocation of a resource are concrete enough to inspect together. If the mismatch is elsewhere, following them should expose it. The team can investigate the problem without first accepting my account of the whole architecture.\nI have worked as a builder, a team leader and an adviser. Each position changes what I can contribute and what I can carry through myself. My exploratory years included conversations and possible collaborations that did not become enduring ventures. A useful review still matters on its own terms: it can change the next experiment, expose an uncertainty or help a team stop promising something it cannot deliver. The people continuing after the conversation need to know what to do with it.\nA recommendation meets somebody's capacity\nAdvice can become unhelpful by being correct at the wrong scale. A proposal may assume specialist staff the organisation does not have, a budget it cannot sustain or a maintenance obligation nobody has accepted. It is not enough that the design would work under those conditions. The conditions have to become part of the conversation.\nFor the appointment team, a sophisticated real-time scheduler might be appropriate later. A first intervention could instead make the confirmation rule intelligible, reduce the unsupported promise and expose a small set of exceptional cases to a person. That choice does not celebrate permanent manual work. It gives the team a tractable way to discover what deserves automation and what depends on a judgment it has not yet made explicit.\nIn this example, the person covering booking requests checks both the available slot and the person or resource needed for it. Until those are assigned, the reply says, “We have your request; the appointment is not confirmed yet.” Confirmation records what has been reserved and gives the visitor the agreed details. The next shift sees requests still awaiting a decision alongside confirmed appointments, rather than an undifferentiated list of messages marked read. The wording, responsibility and handoff can be tried together before any new scheduler is built.\nThe ordinary rule also needs somewhere to stop. If a resource becomes unavailable, the booking goes to the person able to offer an alternative; it does not silently become confirmed because its preferred time is near. If a visitor asks for something the team cannot yet support, staff explain what must be checked and retain the unanswered question. A cancellation releases the actual allocation as well as changing the message. Trying those cases exposes whether the operation makes sense. Automation can then carry the recurring steps without being asked to invent how the organisation handles the exceptions.\nThe smallest intervention is therefore not necessarily the cheapest visible change. It is the smallest change that can answer the relevant question without moving an unmanageable burden elsewhere. A new confirmation button is not small if a person must spend every day reconstructing the missing context behind it. A larger change can be proportionate if it removes a recurring coordination failure. Product simplification becomes interesting at that point: the reduction is in the difficulty people have to carry, not merely in the number of features.\nThe organisation also has legitimate reasons to choose differently. It might value continuity over a cleaner design, or be unable to interrupt a current service while investigating its future. A reviewer should make those trade-offs clearer rather than interpret every refusal as a failure to understand. The recommendation belongs to a decision, and the decision belongs to the people who will live with it.\nThe advice should survive the adviser leaving\nA conversation can feel productive because one person temporarily supplies all the missing context. They know what the diagram means, remember the exception and can explain why a suggestion was made. If the next team member cannot reconstruct that reasoning, the organisation has borrowed the person's presence rather than gained a useful account of the problem.\nFor our hypothetical booking case, I would want the review to leave four things people can use without me: the mismatch that prompted it, the competing explanations, the proposed confirmation rule and a booking that tests the rule. The next owner can then challenge the diagnosis. Perhaps confirmation wording was only a symptom and capacity is the real constraint. Finding that should improve the work, not become a defence of the reviewer's first answer.\nThis also changes the review's tone. “Your system is wrong” collapses the existing work and the particular concern into one verdict. “This message promises a confirmed place before the necessary resource is assigned” identifies a condition the team can examine. Precision makes the criticism less personal and more actionable without making it less serious. Holding an idea without becoming it matters on both sides: the builder needs room to revise the design, and the adviser needs room to revise the diagnosis.\nAfter the conversation\nAn advisory contribution might be accepted and never implemented. A useful recommendation might become unnecessary because the organisation changes direction. A rejected suggestion might still reveal a constraint that helps the team choose another path. None of these possibilities makes advice inconsequential, but they make its consequence more particular than the success of the whole company or product.\nThe distinction matters for learning as well as credit. If the appointment change is made and the complaints remain, the right response is to investigate the result, not preserve the recommendation's elegance. Maybe the message was only one part of the problem. Maybe capacity is scarce in a way the booking model still conceals. The intervention has earned another question, and possibly a different scale of work.\nSometimes I should stay and help implement the choice. Sometimes another person is better placed to continue. Sometimes the review has done its job by making the decision possible. Knowing when to stop is part of being useful: the team should leave with work it can carry, rather than a larger dependence on the person who came to help.\nI would rather leave the booking team able to explain when an appointment is confirmed, test the exceptions and change the rule when it fails than leave them with a better diagram they need me to interpret.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-shape-of-a-useful-intervention/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-work-that-can-travel","@type":"Article","name":"The work that can travel","headline":"The work that can travel","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["Sachait","YOJN Care","YojnAI","intellectual property","product continuity","platform and application ownership"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The work that can travel","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The work that can travel","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/"}}]}]},"description":"How emergency-response technology continued as YOJN Care, while YojnAI supplied a shared mechanism to independently owned applications.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-10-21","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The work that can travel\nAt Sachait Technologies Pvt. Ltd., I worked on reducing false alarms and turning detection into an emergency-response service. A model could flag an event, but somebody still had to inspect the evidence, decide what to do and hand the situation to another person. If the next handler could not see what had already been checked, the service could lose the benefit of the first person's work. The product had to carry the situation between people, not just send another notification.\nI exited successfully through patent acquisition, with safe returns for investors and co-founders, and the work continued as YOJN Care at DAO Studio. The acquired IP concerned a patent application, not a granted patent. The emergency-response problem continued beyond the venture in which I had worked on it.\nWhat travelled was more specific than an ambition to make people safer. Care's design connects detection to evidence, human review and response coordination. An operator who cannot obtain the evidence has a different problem from one who has inspected it and dismissed the alert. Someone taking over needs that distinction, along with what has already been attempted and what still needs attention. Carrying the mechanism forward means preserving those differences, which are easy to lose in a demonstration that ends when the notification arrives.\nWhat belongs to the platform?\nAt DAO Studio we made three IP filings. I also built YojnAI, bringing models, tools, agents and checked playbooks into applications whose intermediate work people could inspect. The platform reached pilots in investment banking, operations and the CFO office. Here, useful work travelled through a shared mechanism into products with different owners and purposes.\nYojnAI belonged to DAO Studio Private Limited, the company behind DAO Studio. The finance products built with it belonged to their partners. That arrangement let a team use the platform's composition and tracing capabilities without making its application part of the platform maker's own product catalogue. The application's documents, configuration and particular task still mattered: the same model call could be a small step in quite different work.\nSuppose an application extracts the wrong reporting period from a document. A fluent final answer might conceal the mistake, especially if the arithmetic that follows is internally consistent. Being able to find the extraction gives the reviewer somewhere useful to intervene. The correction concerns a particular document and question; the means of locating that step can be useful in many applications.\nYojnAI's improvement design followed the application and its version down to a trace and a step. Feedback could enter an improvement project, and a chosen change could be reviewed before being pushed to the application. The composition model gave that process objects to work with, rather than treating every correction as a reason to replace the whole system.\nBut reusing the tracing mechanism and reusing what it traced are different acts. Another application may need the same way to locate an extraction without having any use for the first customer's financial document, much less permission to take it. In YojnAI's arrangements, diagnostic and platform-improvement use of customer data required consent. Supplying the platform did not make the partners' products and records ours.\nThis is a different form of continuity from Sachait's technology becoming part of Care. A platform can remain with its maker while helping someone else's product develop. The reporting-period correction belongs to the task that needed it; the next application can still benefit from a mechanism that makes its own mistakes easier to find.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/the-work-that-can-travel/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends","@type":"BlogPosting","name":"What remains when an experiment ends","headline":"What remains when an experiment ends","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["venture learning","founder experience","Sachait","DAO Studio","career transitions","continuity"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What remains when an experiment ends","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Building products and teams","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"What remains when an experiment ends","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/"}}]}]},"description":"A career can retain the useful work of an unsuccessful venture without keeping its old promises alive.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-11-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What remains when an experiment ends\nAfter Smart Joules, the path became less clear. There were jobs, partnerships, consulting conversations and attempts to build something of my own. Sachait Technologies Pvt. Ltd., a set of 2022 proposals for linked programmes and DAO Studio belonged to that period, alongside explorations that now look much less mature than they felt at the time. I was searching for a way of working as well as a problem to solve.\nGenuine problems were solved within arrangements that did not all become sustainable. That makes the period harder to describe than a sequence of either successes or detours. The outcome of the venture and the value of the work inside it can differ, leaving a difficult question about what remains when the larger system does not work out.\nA problem can outlive the vehicle\nAt Sachait, I joined a team working on emergency response and led product and engineering. A detector was only the beginning. The event had to reach people who could interpret it, carry its context and act. That work continued at DAO Studio as YOJN Care. The setting changed, while the problem of connecting an observation to a useful response remained.\nThe same principle appears at smaller scales. A design can be worth keeping even when the business model around it fails. A customer problem can remain important after a proposed product is abandoned. A team can have done valuable work without the organisation reaching the outcome it promised. A venture asks us to put so much under one name that, when the name stops working, we can mistakenly discard everything beneath it, or defend everything because we cannot bear to discard the name.\nThe organisation on the diagram and the work in front of us\nIn earlier proposals of my own, I was exploring how consulting, education, communications, financing and an accelerator might support one another. DAO Studio later carried its own service, product and community ambitions. There is a real attraction in that kind of arrangement: service work reveals useful problems, products can make capability reusable, and teaching can help people join the work. The diagram becomes dangerous when these relationships begin to substitute for the work. The prospect of a partner can make a team appear larger than the people actually contributing; an intended funding loop can conceal the absence of continuing income. The activities need arrangements of their own before their connection can sustain anything.\nAn ambitious system gains life through nearer commitments. A small useful service can be examined, a product can be tried in a defined setting and a learning programme can have a mentor who continues to show up. Those commitments make the relationships in the larger picture meaningful. They also let the picture change when the work reveals something the diagram did not contain.\nFounding meant doing some of that nearer work while also making room for other people to carry it. I could be building a technical path and still need to resolve who would review it, which delivery took priority or whether a proposed collaboration had a useful division of work. Complementary skills do not become a team merely because a diagram connects them. The arrangement becomes real through what each person can contribute, what they take responsibility for and how the result reaches somebody who needs it.\nThe shape of the contribution helps determine what sort of arrangement it needs. Some help can clarify a decision without owning the implementation. Some work needs a continuing team. A reusable capability can emerge from a delivery, but the delivery still has a person waiting for its particular result. The larger ambition is useful when it helps those commitments reinforce one another; it is a burden when it asks each one to justify the whole picture.\nMoving on is not the same as becoming someone else\nThe rest of life continued through those years: learning skills, meeting people, trying different practices and reconsidering the way I wanted to live. A change in relationships, family or priorities can alter what a professional opportunity means. A role that once looked like the obvious next step can become less attractive than work that leaves room for a different kind of life. These are not interruptions in an otherwise professional story; they are part of how a person chooses what to do.\nTreating everything as a sprint made intensity feel like the answer to every difficulty. Recognising the longer distance changes the question: what kind of life lets the person doing the work continue after the latest milestone? Ambition can remain strong while its place among relationships, family and other interests changes.\nSome choices in an exploratory period are naive. Some respond to constraints that become harder to remember later. I can recognise a question becoming clearer without needing every earlier step to have anticipated the answer. That gives me a more useful way to look back than making the past either inevitable or irrelevant.\nWhat deserves to travel forward\nA problem can outlive the pitch built around it. The pitch may have promised an entire network, a new category or a future in which the pieces fitted together effortlessly. The problem is often more durable: people need to understand a decision; a service needs continuity when its handler changes; an AI application needs better integration points than a final answer. A failed attempt can make those questions more precise by revealing an inadequate mechanism, a difficulty in adoption or an arrangement that demanded more trust than people were willing to give.\nA useful component can become harder to sustain when it has to support an entire imagined world. Looking at where that burden came from can change how much the next venture needs to do. The reason a promising route was left behind can be as useful to that decision as the polished work it produced.\nA continuing practice\nRawThoughts began as blogging and exploration during my transition out of Smart Joules, then developed into a consulting practice. Across those settings, I kept moving between engineering, product and the people living with the result. Looking back over my trajectory, the questions connect more clearly than the organisations through which I pursued them.\nThe present gives me a clearer place to continue without requiring the past to have anticipated it. An experiment can end while leaving a better question, and sometimes carrying that question forward, with less machinery around it, is the most useful outcome available.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/what-remains-when-an-experiment-ends/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-building-products-and-teams","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Building products and teams","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-energy-efficiency","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Energy efficiency","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"What building energy products at Smart Joules taught me: savings that keep the service, controls that reach the plant and advice an operator can use.","text":"When I joined Smart Joules as its founding engineer in 2016, the brief was short: commercial buildings were doubling every decade, around 40% of their energy was wasted, fix it. I knew nothing about energy efficiency. So I went to a hospital, got permission to experiment with its cooling plant, read the machines over Modbus and plotted what they were doing.\nTwo things became hard to ignore. Most of the waste sat in the HVAC system, and the operators running it were pressing red and green buttons with no way to see what those buttons did to the plant. The product thesis grew from there: make the controls simple enough for the people on shift, and use what they do as the context for optimising the plant.\nThese pieces follow that thesis in order: what a saving must preserve, whether a command reached the equipment, and when advice actually helps the operator.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-system-behaviour-to-product-judgement","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From service to control","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-system-behaviour-to-product-judgement","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From service to control","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Energy efficiency","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From service to control","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}}]}],"description":"An energy service gives a control its purpose, and observed consequences give advice something useful to learn from.","text":"A lower meter reading is easy to celebrate. The building still needs cooling, and changing one piece of equipment can move work elsewhere in the plant. Before choosing a control, we need to know which service the change should preserve and what comparison would tell us whether it helped.\nThe control then has a physical path to follow. A request can arrive without producing the intended response; a stale observation can make yesterday's state look current. Following that path brings the operator into the engineering account. Their intervention is part of what the system needs to understand.\nOnly then does advice have a useful basis for learning. A recommendation can be accepted, acted on and still fail to achieve its purpose. Each stage tells us something different about what to improve. Purpose, intervention and consequence give the sequence its order.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-energy-efficiency","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Energy efficiency","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/"}],"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-less-power-is-not-always-a-better-result","@type":"TechArticle","name":"The service behind the saving","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-successful-command-is-not-a-physical-result","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Did the command reach the pump?","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-advice-that-meets-the-operator","@type":"Article","name":"Advice that meets the operator","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/"}}]}},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-advice-that-meets-the-operator","@type":"Article","name":"Advice that meets the operator","headline":"Advice that meets the operator","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["energy operations","fault diagnosis","JouleMind","contextual recommendations","outcome feedback"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Advice that meets the operator","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Energy efficiency","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From service to control","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Advice that meets the operator","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/"}}]}]},"description":"Turning a recurring deviation into useful assistance requires a diagnosis, context and somebody able to respond.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-26","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Advice that meets the operator\nThe same deviation appears every morning. We can plot it, perhaps even predict it. Tomorrow, the operator will still have to work out what it means. Have we made the plant easier to run, or merely become very good at announcing its familiar difficulty?\nAt Smart Joules, the JouleMind and Nudges direction asked how infrastructure, process and user context could support better energy operations. The ambition went beyond another graph: measurements, command history, maintenance and schedules could help determine which suggestion mattered, and to whom. I am interested in that design problem because more information is only helpful when it changes the work of understanding or acting.\nFour explanations for the same morning\nConsider a constructed example. A cooling system uses more energy than expected during the first hour of operation. The pattern might follow an earlier start, a different load, changed equipment performance or an unreliable measurement. A schedule could explain a legitimate difference. A maintenance event could suggest somewhere to investigate. An incorrectly mapped sensor could make the apparent difference disappear when the measurement is repaired.\nThe repeated deviation selects a question; it does not choose between those explanations. A predictor that reproduces the pattern accurately has learned when it appears, not necessarily why. Recommending the same intervention for all four cases would turn a useful detection into an unhelpful certainty.\nThe next step becomes narrower when the explanation connects measurement quality to the operating period, service requirement and intended reference. Maintenance and command history can help distinguish the remaining possibilities, revealing which further observation would resolve a particular uncertainty. “Check whether this schedule still matches today's use” can be a better suggestion than “reduce energy” because it points to something a person can establish, rather than announcing a conclusion the pattern has not supplied.\nThe US Department of Energy's measurement and verification guidance similarly treats equipment conditions, operating hours and varying loads as part of establishing a meaningful baseline. In this example, those conditions matter before the deviation becomes a diagnosis, not just afterwards when a saving is calculated.\nWho can answer this question?\nThe person receiving the suggestion may know why the schedule changed but have no access to alter it. Another person may have access but lack the local context. Sending the same message to both does not solve the split. The application needs to distinguish a request for information, a recommendation to a decision-maker and work someone has agreed to carry.\nThis changes the shape of the suggestion. It can present the observed interval, the expected comparison and the reason a schedule check is relevant. The recipient can confirm a changed operating requirement, report missing information or pass the question to someone who can decide. A closed alert then has a meaning: was the difference explained, the measurement repaired, an intervention tried or only the notification dismissed?\nThe distinction also applies to priority. An unusual pattern may be less important than a smaller recurring loss, while a condition affecting service may deserve attention for reasons an energy score misses. NIST's fault detection and diagnostics research includes interactive diagnosis and prioritisation by energy impact and occupant comfort. That is a useful external example of why detecting a fault and deciding what deserves attention require different information.\nTomorrow's alert should remember today's answer\nSuppose the operator replies, “The building opened an hour earlier today.” That answer changes the comparison. The extra consumption belongs to an extra hour of required service, not necessarily to worse equipment performance. The application can retain the changed opening time and compare the operating interval on that basis. It has learned something about this morning without deciding that every future early start is harmless.\nTomorrow's question should depend on what changed. If the same opening requirement applies, repeating the original alert adds little. If the building returns to its ordinary hours but the plant still starts early, there is a new discrepancy worth examining. The operator's answer needs to remain attached to its schedule and period so it can reduce repetition without silencing a useful later question.\nIf an intervention is tried, its evaluation has to return to the service behind the saving. Did the useful work continue under comparable conditions? Was the result short-lived? Did the change merely move consumption elsewhere? A positive rating about a clear message cannot answer those questions, although it can tell us something about the interaction. The system needs both kinds of feedback without treating them as substitutes.\nThis is the learning loop I wanted the assistance to make possible: preserve a reason for the suggestion, connect it to an examined response, and use that result to make the next question more relevant. It does not require every proposal to become automatic control. Sometimes useful intelligence is knowing what to ask, retaining the answer in its proper context and not making the person reconstruct it again tomorrow.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/advice-that-meets-the-operator/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-system-behaviour-to-product-judgement","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From service to control","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-successful-command-is-not-a-physical-result","@type":"TechArticle","name":"Did the command reach the pump?","headline":"Did the command reach the pump?","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["industrial controls","command acknowledgement","plant feedback","distributed control","operator interfaces"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Did the command reach the pump?","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Energy efficiency","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From service to control","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Did the command reach the pump?","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/"}}]}]},"description":"Following an industrial control from the operator's button through its dependencies to the equipment's actual response.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Did the command reach the pump?\nPress a button. The application says the command succeeded. What, exactly, have we learned about the pump?\nOne button makes the request feel like one event. Behind it, the application accepts an instruction, a controller receives it and equipment may respond. The success message may belong to any one of those steps. Meanwhile, the operator needs to know what happened in the plant. Reading equipment and plotting its behaviour was part of my work on energy products; talking to operators made the other half unavoidable. They needed somewhere to act, not a lesson in our packet network.\nFollowing the command through that gap changes how I think about the interface. It has to make the next useful check visible. A plant keeps running outside our description of it, and the software has to find out what that independent world did.\nOne rule, several places to fail\nImagine a recipe that changes a pump setting when a measured condition is met. One plant, one rule, one action looks like a local operation. Its observation, evaluation and output may nevertheless live in different places. A reading can come from another controller; the rule can run locally while its command crosses the network; or observations can travel to a server before a decision returns.\nThose arrangements fail differently. With local evaluation and output but a remote observation, the controller can remain healthy while the rule loses the information it needs. With local observation and evaluation but remote output, the condition can be recognised while the action remains unconfirmed. Moving evaluation closer to the equipment can remove an internet dependency without removing the local-network dependency or making a missing reading valid.\nLocality is therefore a property of a particular path, not a reassuring adjective for the whole system. During an outage, “the controller is running” helps only after we know which parts of this control still depend on something else.\nThe zero that fills a gap\nA value arrives with an interpretation already attached. Which device produced it? Which parameter does it represent? What unit, scaling and age apply? Chiller performance can depend on power, flow and temperature obtained through different paths. One current input cannot make the others current, and receiving a packet cannot establish that the mapping to a physical component is correct.\nThe distinction between an off asset, an unavailable reading and an invalid calculation matters here. A zero can be a real observation. Substituting zero for a missing input changes the meaning of the calculation while making the chart look complete. The graph should express the state of the evidence rather than reward the interface for having something to draw.\nThe same plant can need several representations. Its geographical layout helps locate an asset. Its physical connections explain where flow or power travels. A logical group identifies the observations needed to understand a process. Collapsing these into one hierarchy makes the display simpler at the cost of hiding the dependencies that explain a failure.\nChiller power passes through controller A, while flow and temperature readings pass through controller B before meeting in the performance calculation.\nThe dashed paths carry readings from the plant to the performance calculation.\nHow far did the request get?\nThe command can be followed as a sequence of observations.\nThe application accepts the request. It has entered the software; did the controller receive it?\nThe controller acknowledges receipt. It received the instruction; did the equipment act on it?\nThe equipment reports a state that matches the request. Is that report current, in the expected unit and mapped to the right component?\nPhysical feedback changes. Something in the plant changed; did it deliver the intended service?\nThe service is examined. We can compare the output with what is needed; is it adequate for the current load?\nEach stage gives the operator somewhere to stand. The next check is narrower than “did it work?”, and the path can remain partly known without being called either a complete success or a complete failure.\nA requested speed and a reported speed can agree while a distant part of the building still receives inadequate service. Conversely, equipment can respond while feedback is delayed or interpreted incorrectly. “Failed” can be as misleading as “successful” when either label compresses the entire path into one status.\nThis changes the meaning of recovery. Repeating a command because its confirmation is absent can duplicate an action already taken. Accepting a successful request as the physical result can leave a real failure unnoticed. The installation, the action and its observations must inform the response; the interface cannot invent that knowledge through a generic retry rule. A statement such as “request sent, equipment state unconfirmed” gives the operator a useful location in the process, from which the product can help identify the missing check instead of offering only an optimistic green indicator or an unexplained error.\nThe plant can change without us\nManual mode can override a network request. Somebody can change the plant during an outage while the application's last observation remains unchanged. A retained reading helps reconstruct the earlier state, but reconnecting requires finding out what is there now, not automatically restoring the old setting. That is a product problem as much as an engineering one: the operator needs the distinctions that affect a decision, not every implementation detail.\nA recipe also has a life beyond its creation. Date eligibility, enabled state, current evaluation, command transmission and physical effect can diverge. The calendar can permit execution while the recipe is paused. Evaluation can begin without a valid input. A command can be sent while its output path is unavailable. Deployment gives the rule a place to operate; commissioning and continuing observation establish how it is operating.\nAlerts extend the path to another person. A locally detected condition may still rely on a server and a communication channel to reach them. Delivery, reading, taking responsibility and resolving the problem then become separate achievements. The person and the manual route belong to the operation, not outside it as an afterthought. Whether the path ends at equipment or reaches another operator, showing where it has reached and which check would let the work continue is more useful than making an entire physical operation look finished because the software successfully sent a message.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/plant-light.svg","description":"Chiller power passes through controller A, while flow and temperature readings pass through controller B before meeting in the performance calculation.","caption":"The dashed paths carry readings from the plant to the performance calculation.\n"}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/did-the-command-reach-the-pump/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-system-behaviour-to-product-judgement","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From service to control","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-less-power-is-not-always-a-better-result","@type":"TechArticle","name":"The service behind the saving","headline":"The service behind the saving","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["energy efficiency","cooling systems","service boundary","measurement and verification","energy performance contracting"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The service behind the saving","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":3,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Energy efficiency","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"From service to control","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"The service behind the saving","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/"}}]}]},"description":"Energy efficiency needs a service boundary and a fair comparison, not only a smaller reading.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-08-22","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The service behind the saving\nSwitch the equipment off and the consumption chart looks wonderful. We have found a very effective way to save electricity, and a rather poor way to cool a building. The absurd case is easy to see. It becomes less obvious when the pump is still running, the saving is smaller and the part of the building losing service is far from the screen.\nWorking on energy products made me suspicious of a lower number offered without its surrounding work. What did the plant deliver before, and what does it deliver now? Once that question enters the comparison, a saving becomes a much more interesting engineering problem.\nWhat did we keep cooling?\nIn cooling, the output is not simply that a machine is running. The spaces being served need an acceptable result under particular conditions, which means two readings cannot be compared fairly just because they have the same unit. If the load, weather, operating hours or service requirement differs, the comparison may contain more than the effect of the intervention. A department closing, a load being outsourced or equipment operating for fewer hours can all lower consumption without improving efficiency. These can be sensible decisions, but they answer a different question from doing the same work with less energy. A product that wants to advise people has to distinguish these mechanisms, or it learns to praise whatever makes the number smaller.\nThe pump borrows work from the rest of the plant\nA cooling system makes the boundary problem concrete. Changing a pump changes flow, which can alter the work at the chiller and the service delivered elsewhere. A cooling tower can consume more while allowing another component to consume less. The intervention must therefore be understood across the coupled operation: looking only at the device adjusted can miss both a benefit elsewhere and a harm elsewhere. The measurement boundary follows the question we are asking, rather than the convenience of the available meter.\nThe physical dependencies also complicate aggregation. A main meter and its subloads are not independent quantities to add. Two interventions can affect the same consumption, even if they have different names and appear on different pages of a report. Deciding what can be counted together is part of determining which improvement the engineering is trying to produce, not an accounting detail left until afterwards.\nThe experiment that changes the next experiment\nAn attractive intervention deserves a trial because the coupled behaviour is part of what we need to learn. Closing a valve and changing a pump setting are not equivalent interventions, even if both appear to concern the same flow. A suspected bottleneck may not be the limiting one; a component may behave differently from its nominal curve; the operating team may identify a service constraint the analysis missed. Testing the first arrangement can reveal why a second experiment should include another part of the system.\nThe possibility of stopping gives the trial value. A change that looks promising at one load may be inappropriate at another. Discovering that condition can improve the next experiment or show that continuing the disruption is unjustified. This is one reason I value the connection between product work and field observation: the field helps determine what the design should become. Retaining what was tried, what was observed and what condition changed gives the next experiment something to work from, without turning every discarded intervention into either an embarrassment or a delivered result.\nA few hours on the chart, a year in the spreadsheet\nA short observation can support a comparison for the hours it covers. It does not become a year of performance because a spreadsheet multiplies it by operating hours. Someone deciding whether to invest still needs an estimate of future benefit, but that estimate depends on the assumed hours, load, tariff, maintenance and continuation of the service. Keeping the measured comparison recognisable within the forecast prevents its uncertainty from disappearing exactly when the claim becomes larger.\nBefore-and-after charts need the same attention to their conditions. If the meter, the baseline or the way readings were collected changed, that belongs in the interpretation; a persuasive line cannot isolate a cause that the comparison did not isolate. Making those conditions available is a product challenge too. An operator should be able to understand why a comparison is relevant without having to reconstruct the analysis behind it.\nWho pays for the saving?\nEnergy services add another layer. A provider may invest in equipment and continuing operation in exchange for a share of savings. The customer may avoid capital expense while accepting a particular service relationship. Physical energy reduction, avoided maintenance expense and tariff-dependent monetary saving are not identical benefits: a lower bill can reflect a price change, and an efficient plant can still encounter a higher tariff. The arrangement therefore needs an agreed baseline and a way to handle changed loads and periods. Those agreements connect the physical benefit to its commercial value; calculating a saving does not by itself produce an accepted report, an invoice or a payment.\nMy work on product and technology supported that continuing operation, alongside the teams responsible for energy services and commercial delivery. Installation began a relationship with the plant: observations, changed conditions and the customer's experience continued to affect what counted as a useful result.\nWhen the chart starts giving instructions\nOnce a metric is visible and rewarded, people have a reason to optimise it. That makes its definition part of the product's behaviour. A system that celebrates consumption alone can encourage service loss; one that celebrates the number of successful commands can discourage investigation into what those commands achieved. The measure affects the work, so keeping it connected to the service is part of the design rather than a qualification added to the report.\nEnergy work gave me a concrete way to see this relationship between a measurable input and a contextual output. I find it useful in software too, though the mechanisms differ: a faster response can leave more work unfinished, or a lower ticket count can result from customers giving up. Understanding the work behind the number is what makes the comparison useful. In the plant, that means knowing what service continued before deciding what the saving was worth.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/the-service-behind-the-saving/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-system-behaviour-to-product-judgement","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"From service to control","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/energy-efficiency/from-service-to-control/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-learning-and-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Learning and practice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"How curiosity, study, mentoring and feedback change what a person can do.","text":"An answer can become familiar long before it becomes understood. I noticed that in questions about time, in customs around texts and in practical work where I could repeat the explanation but still could not use it. Questioning the answer opened a space. The harder part was learning what to do with that space.\nStudy gives curiosity something to meet: a passage, a definition, a different interpretation, a result that resists the explanation. Practical work does the same in another setting. A learner might understand the pieces of a dashboard and discover, only when someone uses it, that the important decision is still difficult to make. Another person's question can bring the missing part into view.\nThat is why the educational ambition and the work of mentoring belong together for me. An institution can sustain an inquiry longer than one person's enthusiasm. A mentor can help someone continue through the confusion that an initial explanation leaves behind. Both need patience, criticism and a setting in which asking a better question counts as progress.\nThe same need for a useful starting point appears in technical writing. The reader needs a way into the problem, the contributor needs enough context to develop it, and neither is well served by an explanation that only its original author can use.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-question-we-can-work-on-together","@type":"Article","name":"A question we can work on together","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief","@type":"Article","name":"Learning with a real problem","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against","@type":"Article","name":"The discipline after unlearning","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-question-we-can-work-on-together","@type":"Article","name":"A question we can work on together","headline":"A question we can work on together","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["research communities","volunteer contribution","reproducible experiments","shared research","UPAI"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A question we can work on together","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Learning and practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A question we can work on together","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/"}}]}]},"description":"Shared research becomes possible when a question has a tractable experiment, connected contributions and a record that lets other people continue.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-11-03","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A question we can work on together\nIn community work, I keep returning to the distance between having an interesting conversation and finding something we can do together. One person wants to study learning, another is interested in a new architecture, a third wants to build something useful immediately. I recognise something of myself in each position. The interests may belong to the same broad field without yet belonging to the same piece of work.\nI volunteer with and help lead UPAI, the Uttar Pradesh AI community. Lucknow AI Labs, or LAI, is its founding Lucknow chapter. That association has given this question a practical setting. Helping a community grow and developing my own architectural ideas can happen in the same conversation, but they ask different things of the people listening. Someone may want to learn, offer a few hours, challenge the idea or simply observe. I do not want my enthusiasm for an architecture to decide which of those contributions counts.\nLearning with a real problem gave my teaching a useful centre. I want shared research to have something similarly tangible: a question, an experiment or a stubborn result around which different people can contribute. The attraction is not merely distributing my plan among volunteers. It is finding an observation none of us could obtain merely by exchanging explanations.\nMaking the question small changes who can contribute\nTime, interest and experience need separate attention. A person can know the subject and have little time; another can be a beginner with the patience to prepare examples carefully. Organisation, teaching, design and documentation also help research continue. Asking everybody to contribute code narrows the community before the question has even been chosen. I would rather find a piece of work small enough to carry, and clear enough that someone else can rely on it.\nConsider an imagined group interested in whether retrieved context helps a language model use unfamiliar domain terms. “Build a system that understands the domain” is a large ambition. It leaves nearly every important choice open. A smaller question might ask whether short definitions improve answers to a fixed set of questions when the model and prompt are held constant. The result would not settle domain understanding. It would give the group a particular effect to investigate.\nThat reduction changes the work. Someone can read the relevant literature and clarify what previous comparisons actually measured. Someone can construct a small, permitted set of examples. Another person can implement the baseline and treatment. A reviewer can inspect whether the answers were judged consistently and whether the definitions accidentally contained the answers. These roles connect through the question. They are not separate personal projects placed under a common name.\nIt also gives disagreement somewhere useful to land. A participant might object that the questions are too easy, or that the definitions contain cues unavailable in ordinary use. The objection can improve the sample or narrow the claim. With a very broad ambition, the same disagreement often becomes an argument about the eventual architecture, long before the group has an observation to organise it.\nA small question is not automatically an insignificant one. It can expose a dependency the larger ambition concealed. If the model succeeds only when the answer is effectively supplied in the context, the group has learned something different from successful transfer to new cases. If a simple baseline works equally well, a proposed mechanism may not yet have a useful job. The scale is modest enough that the disagreement can become an experiment.\nContribution needs a place to meet another contribution\nGiving each volunteer a task does not guarantee that their work will connect. The reader of a paper may extract a definition that the implementation never uses. The person creating examples may make assumptions the reviewer does not know. The person writing the script may change the input format and unintentionally change the experiment. Coordination depends on making those interfaces intelligible.\nFor the imagined comparison, that could mean agreeing on the question format, the source of the definitions and what an acceptable answer contains. Each contribution can then be inspected where it joins the next. A participant who cannot write the model code can still expose a bad comparison or an ambiguous example. The experiment becomes a shared object around which people can develop different abilities.\nThere is a practical limit to how many things a volunteer can carry reliably. Research needs reading, data work, review and communication as well as the visible implementation. Expanding the proposal consumes all of those capacities, often before it consumes additional compute. A plan that leaves every spare evening occupied has made itself fragile. A smaller, repeatable commitment lets others build on a contribution without requiring perpetual enthusiasm from the same person.\nThis is different from making the work easy. Research will still contain confusion and periods when the result is hard to interpret. The useful arrangement gives that difficulty continuity: someone can explain the current state, another can take a small piece and both can return to the same question. It makes room for an observer to begin contributing without pretending that joining the discussion assigns them responsibility for the whole undertaking.\nThe artifact lets somebody else become a critic\nA result explained only by the person who ran it cannot yet support much shared learning. The next participant needs enough of the configuration, inputs, method and output to understand what happened and try to challenge it. The relevant account is more specific than “the new approach worked.” It identifies what was changed and what was observed under that change.\nThe NeurIPS paper checklist asks for claims that match the contribution, experimental details and a reasonable way to reproduce or verify the result. Those questions are valuable before a paper exists. For our small group, they reveal whether the record is carrying the experiment or merely reporting enthusiasm about it. They do not require every research problem to use an identical release package.\nThe inputs matter as much as the script. The Datasheets for Datasets proposal connects a dataset's motivation, composition, collection and intended uses. Even a small sample benefits from knowing why these examples were selected and what they omit. Otherwise a later contributor can reuse a convenient file while quietly changing the meaning of the comparison.\nMaking an artifact inspectable is also a way of distributing intellectual responsibility. A newcomer can rerun a baseline, find a parsing problem or propose a counterexample. Their contribution need not begin with accepting the group's explanation. That is one reason reproducibility is educational as well as technical: it gives another person a place to encounter the claim through their own work.\nA shared inquiry can change direction without losing itself\nMy own architectural exploration has changed as I have gone further into its foundations. A shared inquiry needs room for its explanation to change too. A community can help an idea become better precisely because it is not my team executing my product roadmap. Participants need enough of the question to disagree with it, and a way to make that disagreement useful without committing themselves to everything I want to investigate.\nSuppose the comparison reveals that the definitions help familiar question forms but not a different phrasing. The group could respond by enlarging the system immediately. It could also keep the finding visible and investigate the dependence. That second movement may require less machinery and teach more. A question narrowed enough to begin should remain open enough to change when the result resists it.\nThere are many useful outcomes short of a novel paper: a faithful reproduction, a discovered error, an explanation of a limitation or a reusable evaluation case. They should be named for what they contribute. The ambition to produce new research can continue without treating every intermediate artifact as a new scientific result.\nCredit needs a similarly careful account. Formulating the question, designing examples, implementing a comparison and interpreting its result are real contributions that can be made by different people. A community should make them legible rather than let the most visible speaker become the owner of everybody's work. That attention helps a participant understand both what they have contributed and what they still depend on others to do.\nThe deepest promise of shared research, for me, is that people can become more capable through the inquiry itself. The question gives them a common difficulty. The artifacts let the difficulty survive a particular conversation. Other people's criticism changes what each participant can see. A community begins to have continuity when that movement can happen again, even with a different person carrying the next part.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-question-we-can-work-on-together/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-learning-and-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Learning and practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief","@type":"Article","name":"Learning with a real problem","headline":"Learning with a real problem","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["JouleLabs","mentoring","problem-based learning","industry-academia collaboration","UPAI"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Learning with a real problem","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Learning and practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Learning with a real problem","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/"}}]}]},"description":"Mentoring connects a learner's next step to a useful problem, a review and a realistic commitment.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-06-18","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Learning with a real problem\nA tutorial usually knows where it is going. It has chosen the important details, prepared the inputs and arranged a route to completion. A real problem is less courteous. Someone needs something to work; the information is incomplete, the environment resists the example and the person asking for help may not yet know how to describe what is wrong. That difficulty creates a reason to understand, not merely a reason to finish the exercise.\nJouleLabs began close to the work\nI founded and ran JouleLabs while building the product-development team at Smart Joules. The question was how someone still learning could make a meaningful contribution to consequential work. A problem statement needed to explain why the work mattered, a mentor needed to continue beyond the initial explanation, and the student's project needed a route through review towards something another person could use. Learning, contribution and possible recruitment could reinforce one another because they met around that shared object. Without the continuity, the student could produce a deliverable and the organisation a certificate, while neither acquired much that changed the next piece of work.\nWe reached our first university memorandum of understanding in under two months from the idea. The programme eventually involved five universities and a cohort of sixty-five students, with more than forty moving into full-time employment. Those numbers describe the programme’s reach and the transition into work. The educational question was what made that transition possible: a problem worth entering, someone available to review it and a team with a use for the result.\nWhat is the dashboard supposed to change?\n“Build a dashboard” specifies a deliverable. “Help an operator recognise why this measurement cannot yet be trusted” gives the learner a problem to investigate. The second formulation opens questions about the measurement, the operator's task and the decision that follows. It also makes review possible in a more meaningful way: did the person using the result gain something that helps them act? A dashboard might be the answer, but it would now have a reason for its contents.\nThe problem still needs a boundary. A learner cannot be expected to reconstruct an entire industrial system before making a first contribution. A mentor can isolate a useful question, identify what is safe to examine and provide enough context to prevent the project from becoming arbitrary guesswork. Too much prescription reduces the work to following steps; too little support turns confusion into a test of whether the learner can survive it. The scaffolding earns its place by making the next act of understanding possible, then leaving room for the learner to make it.\nAfter the first explanation\nA real project brings interruptions. The learner finds a missing assumption, the source changes or a first attempt fails. A mentor helps distinguish a useful failure from a misunderstanding that should not consume another week. That means making the problem navigable: where to look, what to compare, how to express the unresolved question and when to ask for help. It takes actual time within the team's work, not just an initial promise that help will be available.\nThe organisation also needs a way to receive the contribution. If nobody can review or integrate it, the learner's effort ends in a demonstration that has no continuing home. Supporting the programme therefore means reserving capacity on both sides: time for the student to enter the work, and time for the team to examine and use what they bring to it.\nTwo hours a week is a real commitment\nIn my UPAI community participation, time and continuity have been recurring questions. People arrive with different interests, abilities and amounts of unpaid time. A beginner asking for a learning path and an experienced volunteer able to help for two hours a week need different ways into the work. Enthusiasm is valuable, but scheduling it as though it were a permanent team can turn a broad assignment into something nobody can sustain.\nA small commitment that someone can finish and another person can rely on can create more continuity. It gives a mentor something concrete to review and a learner a way to experience their contribution becoming useful. Designing participation around the support and time that exist need not exclude those still learning. It can make it easier for them to become capable of work they could not have done alone.\nA contribution that keeps travelling\nJouleLabs later inspired Sustainability Mafia’s Climate Ninja programme; I did not run that later programme. The influence interests me because the idea can travel beyond its first institution: practitioner-led learning around live problems gives people a route into useful work. A later programme can develop that route in ways its original setting could not.\nThis nearer form also connects to the longer ambition in The discipline after unlearning. A learner needs more than access to explanations. They need an environment in which a question can become a capability, and a capability can become a contribution.\nThere is something lovely about a learner discovering that their work has become useful to somebody else. That encounter asks for more care than a certificate does: a problem they can enter, somebody who stays through the confusion and a team willing to receive the result. Participants will want different futures, and some projects will lead somewhere other than integration. The point is to give curiosity enough contact with the world that the person can find their next contribution.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/a-real-problem-is-a-better-learning-brief/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-learning-and-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Learning and practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against","@type":"Article","name":"The discipline after unlearning","headline":"The discipline after unlearning","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["unlearning","inquiry-based education","Katha Upanishad","independent thinking","learning institutions"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The discipline after unlearning","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Learning and practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The discipline after unlearning","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/"}}]}]},"description":"Questioning an inherited explanation is a beginning; replacing it needs study, comparison and a way to be wrong.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-06-05","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The discipline after unlearning\nIt all started with “time”.\nI could learn the engineering language for measuring a passage of time without feeling that I had answered what time was. The distinction sounds abstract until it unsettles a familiar explanation: a clock tells us something important, but naming the instrument does not finish the question.\nThat curiosity led me through technical study, philosophy and traditions I had grown up around. It also made me suspicious of a particular kind of education: the kind that gives us answers so efficiently that we stop noticing which questions those answers leave open.\nReading what I was told not to read\nI grew up in Lucknow in a Hindu Brahmin family, with roots in Almora. A custom kept the Katha Upanishad away from ordinary reading, associating it with death. Being the rebel and curious soul I was, I read it anyway. What I found was, in a way, a podcast: a young boy, Nachiketa, in conversation with Yama about death, desire, what endures and what deserves to be pursued. The text became something I could examine for myself, rather than something defined by the inherited restriction around it.\nI also wrote about the Nirvana Shatkam and its movement through negation: not this, not that. Reading it as a hymn was one encounter; following its negations as an exercise was another. Take away the identification with a body, a thought, a role or a description. What remains? In my reading, its movement towards Shiva and the bliss of consciousness was a process to go through, rather than a conclusion to repeat. It asked me to notice how quickly I had attached an identity to each thing it was taking away.\nOther readings, including the Saundarya Lahari and Vigyan Bhairav, opened further paths. Ancient podcasts and publications, if you will, encountered through dialogue, negation, description and practice. Kaal, MahaKaal and Kaali became part of that inquiry: what were these texts saying about time through those names and images? All because I had been trying to find out what time was.\nI began to wonder whether a civilisation organised around these inquiries understood something we have become poor at recognising. I called ours a civilisation of electromagnetism, a species of Maxwell, and imagined those older worlds through legends whose meanings might have been lost in translation. That is an intuition inviting study, not a historical demonstration supplied by the resemblance. What I wanted to recover was a scholarly encounter with the texts: enough freedom to question a custom, and enough patience to discover that the passage was not saying what I expected.\nUnlearning, for me, began less as a declaration against a tradition than as a refusal to let the inherited instruction be the last thing said about it.\nThe comfort of a new allegiance\nOnce an inherited belief becomes questionable, rebellion can feel like clarity. The emotional reward arrives before the harder work. I can stop trusting an institution and begin trusting whichever voice most strongly opposes it, or adopt an unfamiliar explanation because its novelty feels liberating. Replacing the vocabulary leaves the old habit intact if I still accept conclusions without examining how they were reached. A question opens possibilities; the first alternative deserves examination too.\nWhen I ask what time is, there are several kinds of work available. I can study what established physical theories say, examine how duration is experienced, read a story in which time is imagined differently, or propose a model and ask what observation would distinguish it from another. These inquiries can illuminate one another without supplying interchangeable answers. A compelling experience does not establish a physical mechanism; an equation does not tell me everything about how time is lived. Understanding what each encounter offers makes their connection useful, rather than merely exciting.\nGetting very good at the teacher's answer\nEducation often rewards what can be repeated: a teacher needs some way to judge whether a learner has acquired the material. Yet a person can learn the vocabulary of independent thinking while becoming very skilled at anticipating what the teacher wants to hear. The same difference appears in technical work when a completed tutorial leaves the next unfamiliar condition unnavigable. Acquiring the explanation has value; making it one's own asks for another kind of encounter with the subject.\nI am interested in learning that makes the learner more capable of noticing: what is missing from the explanation, which assumption was invisible before, or what they need to observe, build or experience to understand the question better. The result does not have to be certainty. A more precise doubt can give the next act of learning a direction.\nThe school I was imagining\nThe question led me to imagine an institution: a place where curiosity could develop through examining one's conditioning, meeting unfamiliar ideas and building something from the encounter. Individual advice can encourage inquiry, but the setting has to make it possible. The institution would need to support a learner through both the inherited answer and the uncertainty that opens when they question it.\nThat is a much longer project than improving a course outline. A school carries incentives, selection, authority, habits of discussion and an account of what counts as success. It can praise curiosity while punishing the questions that disrupt its own image.\nThe same scholarly detachment matters in modern research. I see it in open-source systems that let someone else examine the work, and in high-quality peer review that tries to judge an argument without favouring its desired outcome. Chasing a journal’s impact factor can displace that purpose. So can p-hacking: choosing and reworking analyses until the data appear to deliver the statistical result one wanted. An institution cannot teach detachment convincingly while rewarding the appearance of a successful result over an honest inquiry. It has to leave room to question even the concepts on which its own standing rests.\nThe difficult design problem is how to make inquiry ordinary. Learners need enough structure to become capable, enough freedom to discover a question of their own and enough care that disagreement does not become humiliation. Teachers need room to say they do not know without making every claim equally valid.\nI imagine building such a place on a horizon longer than my own lifetime. Centuries of work, perhaps, and no guarantee that those who start it will live to see what it becomes. Detachment from the outcome is therefore part of the institution's design, not merely advice to its students. What would let later people examine its assumptions, rather than preserve the founder's answers? A school devoted to inquiry would eventually have to welcome a question that unsettled its own account of what inquiry was for. Otherwise the next generation would inherit another instruction not to read.\nPractical teaching has given the question a nearer form. A real problem, a continuing mentor and work that somebody can use can connect curiosity to consequence. They do not fulfil the entire institutional ambition, but they create a place to begin.\nA question that changes what I build\nTime led me towards traditions, models and the creative inquiry I now pursue in Sleepwalker. Product work led me towards learning and institutions. Each encounter gave the next question more substance: an unexplained experience could prompt study; an incomplete explanation could prompt a design; teaching could expose an assumption I had stopped noticing.\nThese encounters keep the world from feeling exhausted by the answers I already have. They give curiosity something to return to, study and build upon. Questioning an explanation opens a space; the patient work that follows lets something richer grow there without making it another allegiance.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/unlearning-needs-something-to-test-against/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-learning-and-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Learning and practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/learning-and-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"Personal inquiries into intelligence, time, memory and the forms through which a question becomes thinkable.","text":"Some questions remain interesting after their first explanation stops working. I can let go of an equation and still want to understand the intuition that made me write it. A picture can open a question that needs mathematics, or offer something a mathematical account was never meant to supply. The work is finding a form that lets the inquiry deepen without requiring it to pretend to be something else.\nIntelligence, time and memory keep returning in that work. Where does the useful capacity of a whole reside? What happens to a person's sense of scale when the account becomes larger than a lifetime? Which part of an experience is worth retaining, and what would let that experience change a later judgment? I meet these questions in ordinary experiences too: returning to a note, waiting for someone to answer, discovering that a drawing has changed what I wanted to say.\nMaking things adds another pressure. A future can look technologically advanced and feel as though it has forgotten every person who might inhabit it. An image can repeat the words of an idea while showing a relation the words never contained. Form is not only the final container. It can change what becomes possible to think.\nA familiar image can make an unfamiliar thought approachable. Then the image starts answering for us, and its convenience becomes a limit. I want to keep the reach of the analogy while letting the question ask for something the analogy cannot supply: a relation made explicit, a passage read more closely, or a scene whose meaning changes when I draw it.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"hasPart":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-future-that-remembers","@type":"Article","name":"A future that remembers","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-lifetime-is-not-a-rounding-error","@type":"Article","name":"A lifetime in the larger frame","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-intelligence-between-the-parts","@type":"Article","name":"The intelligence between the parts","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-thinking-in-more-than-one-medium","@type":"Article","name":"Thinking in more than one medium","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/"},{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-i-want-a-memory-to-do","@type":"Article","name":"What I want a memory to do","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-future-that-remembers","@type":"Article","name":"A future that remembers","headline":"A future that remembers","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["cultural continuity","retrofuturism","repairable technology","quiet interfaces","technology and everyday life","image composition"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A future that remembers","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A future that remembers","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/an-image-is-a-beginning/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/continuity-is-more-than-a-seed/"]},"description":"Why I imagine technology growing through cultural life: repairable objects, ordinary rooms and an inherited form capable of doing something surprising.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-06-09","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A future that remembers\nImagine a room with a terminal, a lamp, books and a cup somebody has left beside their work. Beyond the window, an immense machine hangs in the distance. Nobody in the room treats it as a spectacle. The lamp still has to light the desk; the person still has a page to finish. I find this future more interesting than one in which every ordinary object has disappeared into an immaculate wall.\nWhat draws me to it is the possibility of technological change without cultural amnesia. An inherited form need not be a relic, and an advanced instrument need not look as though it arrived from outside the life around it. A civilisation can maintain its objects, rituals and places while discovering new things they can do. The future I want to imagine has had time to become familiar.\nA machine with a history\nA perfectly seamless device invites me to treat it as a finished surface. A housing, a switch, an instrument panel or a visible connection gives it a body. Someone may have opened it, changed a part, understood its behaviour well enough to keep it running. Those possibilities alter the world implied by the image. The object belongs to a history of use rather than merely a catalogue of the latest technology.\nThat is part of my attraction to analog technological futures. Rounded screens, deep bezels, lamps and physical controls are forms I can imagine being inhabited, maintained and passed on. Their significance is not that old hardware was universally better. It is that technology occupies a place in human life. When everything becomes a frictionless pane, I can lose the sense of how a person relates to what sustains them.\nThe same thought changes how I imagine a sacred or civic building. A temple containing an advanced instrument need not be a temple with a futuristic accessory pasted onto it. What if its material, spatial customs, maintenance and observation had developed together? A shrine and a terminal room could come from the same technological tradition. I want to discover how that continuity would feel in a world, rather than make a historical claim by drawing the two beside each other.\nRecognition, followed by surprise\nThere is a particular encounter I keep trying to design: an object first appears familiar, then turns out to be doing something I did not expect. At rest, it has the discipline of an artifact. In use, a held state, a movement or a change of light reveals that it is alive as a system. Recognition gives the surprise somewhere to land. Without it, the experience can become another sequence of impressive effects with nothing to hold onto.\nThat ambition was behind my earlier studies of programmable geometry, interfaces and expressive physical objects. I wanted an identity that could move between a screen, a light and a piece of sound without becoming a logo repeatedly applied to different surfaces. The more difficult question was when that range should appear. If everything moves, movement stops telling me anything. If the system is so restrained that it never reveals its character, the restraint has become a loss of possibility.\nThe dragon drainpipe is a useful image for what I mean. It carries water away and still has a face, a story, a little delight. Its poetry has not excused it from doing the ordinary job. Its ordinary job has not required it to abandon the poetry. I want technology to hold that conjunction more often: something exact enough to trust and alive enough to care about.\nA quiet frame for a vivid world\nThe frame around an experience can demand attention that the experience needed for itself. A reader may arrive to follow an argument and spend their first moments learning a new navigation ritual. An image may be crowded by surrounding effects, each announcing that the interface is special. I want the machinery to spend that attention carefully. A clear, addressable interaction can remain quiet until it is called, then respond directly.\nThe terminal interests me in this sense: naming, querying, changing a state and seeing a response. I do not need a screen full of fake prompts to preserve that manner. In a publication, the reading surface can carry stable habits while the world around the work changes through material, geometry, type and light. The reader should not have to relearn how to move every time the atmosphere becomes different.\nAn image then has room to carry more of the expressive burden. A cool field and one warm source can establish a relation before detailed textures establish materials. Overlap, distance and a silhouette can make a place dimensional without making every surface equally explicit. This is why I care about the composition of a human witness within a larger world. What matters first is where the person stands in relation to the light, the opening, the thing that exceeds them. An inventory of beautiful surfaces can leave that relation strangely absent.\nOrdinary life gives the immense world weight\nA monumental image can make a person useful only as a ruler. A small figure demonstrates that the structure is large, then has no more work to do. I want the figure to carry something of a life. A desk, a return path, a recognisable object or a lit window can suggest that the place has uses beyond the moment we are watching. The extraordinary becomes more convincing when the world can afford to treat some of it as ordinary.\nThat conviction reaches into how I think about an actual working space. Books, music, research and making need room alongside the rest of a household's life. The design should absorb difficult services and connections so the room can remain open, usable and quiet. I do not want every technical possibility to become something its inhabitants must constantly operate. Nor do I want the quietness to depend on pretending the systems underneath it do not exist.\nA future acquires depth when some things persist through its transformations. In a sequence, a remembered object or a stable threshold can carry that persistence even as the surrounding register changes. In a technological world, an inherited custom or repairable instrument can do similar imaginative work. It gives the new thing a relation to what was already there.\nI want a future with enough memory that people can belong to it, and enough possibility that belonging does not become a demand to leave everything unchanged. The books and the terminal can share a desk. The enormous machine can remain outside the window while someone finishes a page. The future has happened, and life has continued within it.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-a-lifetime-is-not-a-rounding-error","@type":"Article","name":"A lifetime in the larger frame","headline":"A lifetime in the larger frame","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["lived time","cosmic scale","attention","human significance","Markandeya","observer perspective"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/","@type":"WebPage","name":"A lifetime in the larger frame","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"A lifetime in the larger frame","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/breath-of-cosmos/","https://getharsh.in/blog/knowing-understanding-and-realising/","https://causality.in/blog/the-record-and-the-arrow/","https://sleepwalker.in/blog/the-world-inside-a-breath/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/vedic-world-inquiry/through-markandeyas-encounters/markandeya-and-the-size-of-a-world/"]},"description":"A breath and an immense span of time make me wonder why changing the scale of a life should change its significance.","datePublished":{"@value":"2024-02-08","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"A lifetime in the larger frame\nImagine someone looking at an entire life and calling it a moment. Inside it, there were mornings that would not end, years that passed before anyone noticed, promises, work, boredom and people who mattered. From the larger frame, all of that fits into barely a pause. Have we discovered that the life was insignificant, or only that we were looking from far away?\nThe question returns whenever I place a familiar interval beside an unfamiliar scale. A breath has a beginning and an end I can attend to. A cosmic period can be written as a number so large that my attention has no sensible way to inhabit it. Repeating breaths gives me a ratio, then exhausts my imagination long before the arithmetic runs out. I am interested in the point where a perfectly intelligible calculation stops being a satisfactory encounter with what it describes.\nWhat the larger frame leaves out\nA year can contain an ordinary day that will later matter enormously. At the time, I may not know which day it is. A short conversation can change how I understand years of work; an afternoon can begin a question that keeps returning. Looking backwards makes a few moments appear inevitable because the rest has fallen out of the account. The life as experienced was less selective. It included the waiting, false starts and intervals whose significance was not yet visible.\nThat makes scale more than a matter of zoom. A career summary, a personal recollection and a calendar arrange the same duration around different questions. The summary selects contributions. The recollection follows what it felt like to become the person who made them. The calendar keeps a common measure through both. None needs to contain everything, but I lose something when I forget why a particular account was made and ask it to stand for the whole life.\nEven a very ordinary wait makes the difference felt. Waiting for an important reply and becoming absorbed in a piece of work can occupy the same measured interval. The difference is not a defect in the clock. Attention is organised around a different possibility: in one interval I keep returning to what has not happened; in the other, the work keeps offering another relation to follow. Duration meets a person through what they are doing, expecting and unable to settle.\nRecognition inside an impossible world\nIn the Markandeya account, the change of scale does not dissolve the familiar. In the Bhaktivedanta rendering of the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, the witness enters the child's body and finds a world within it; 12.9.30 brings recognition as close as his own hermitage. The world becomes impossible around a place he knows. Its immensity reaches him through recognition.\nVastness can be represented by making a figure smaller and smaller until the figure hardly matters. Keeping enough of the figure's world offers another possibility: the change of frame becomes an encounter. A place, a remembered route or an ordinary object can carry a relation that sheer magnitude cannot. That is the attraction of the familiar surviving within Markandeya's immense frame. It lets recognition deepen the bewilderment instead of ending it.\nFor a drawing or an imagined scene, this suggests a question: which familiar detail should survive as the frame expands? A place the witness knows could hold more of the encounter than another dramatic display of magnitude. The intimate remains intimate after the frame expands, and its persistence changes how the expanded world feels.\nThe observer brings a small world\nAn observer never arrives empty. Even an instrument records particular quantities at a particular resolution; a person also brings memory, expectation and attachment. I can ask precisely how a record retained a distinction in the research on recoverable observations. Here I am asking what it means to carry a small world into a larger encounter. The fact that a description can omit that world does not make it disappear from the person undergoing the experience.\nA cosmic perspective can be generous when it loosens an immediate problem's claim to be everything. It can also become a convenient way of dismissing the problem. A deadline is small against a century. Someone waiting on its result may still have a good reason to care. A disagreement can become less absolute when placed in a longer life without making every participant's cost imaginary. I want the wider frame to change my judgment, not excuse me from looking at what remains close.\nThis is why I keep returning to Breath of Cosmos. That argument takes an unbounded duration and a finite, seven-second breath: the count of human intervals grows without bound, while one interval's share tends to zero. The breath itself remains nonzero. Its smallness belongs to the comparison, which is precisely why I do not want the comparison to erase the experience. Sleepwalker's world inside a breath follows the image into a creative question: what can a witness recognise as the frame expands?\nThe larger view is not a place where I can finally put life down and describe it without involvement. I am still the person doing the looking. A lifetime may occupy a tiny fraction of the span I can imagine, yet that is the fraction through which I encounter the span at all. Calling it small has measured a relation. What it means to live it remains to be understood.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-lifetime-in-the-larger-frame/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-the-intelligence-between-the-parts","@type":"Article","name":"The intelligence between the parts","headline":"The intelligence between the parts","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["composable intelligence","reusable intelligence","learned capacities","agents and playbooks","distributed memory"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/","@type":"WebPage","name":"The intelligence between the parts","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"The intelligence between the parts","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/from-unit-to-swarm/emergence-needs-a-comparison/"]},"description":"An operating-system image led me from composing AI applications to a harder question: what could make several learned capacities useful together?","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-06","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"The intelligence between the parts\nImagine finding a useful piece of intelligence and putting it beside another. One recognises a situation; the other knows what to do when that situation appears. Connecting them sounds almost embarrassingly straightforward. Recognition goes in, action comes out. But what exactly passed between them? A label, a history, a degree of uncertainty, the thing one of them noticed and the other would otherwise miss? The connection may be carrying more of the intelligence than the diagram admits.\nThis is a question I have been following through different kinds of work. At DAO Studio, the operating-system image helped me think about applications, agents and playbooks. A capability could have an interface; a sequence could retain what an earlier step had found; a later step could resume when the missing information arrived. Those were useful ways to arrange work. Underneath them was another ambition that arranging calls could not satisfy: could learned capacities themselves become composable, and could their organisation change what the whole system was able to do?\nThe missing part of the handoff\nSuppose one component reads a passage and reports that a person promised to return. Another selects the next action. The report may be accurate and still be too small. Was the promise conditional? Did it concern tomorrow or a date that has already passed? Was it spoken sincerely, quoted from someone else or inferred by the reader? If the second component needs one of those distinctions, an impeccably delivered label cannot supply it. The apparent quality of the first component conceals an unanswered question about what the two can share.\nAn application can often solve this through an explicit representation. Carry the relevant fields, retain the source, let the next step ask for what it needs. The agent and playbook work gave that approach a concrete home. I still find it valuable: a visible sequence can expose the point at which interpretation, missing information or a person's decision matters. Yet the more interesting research possibility begins when I cannot specify the whole useful representation in advance. What would let two learned components discover a productive exchange rather than merely conform to the exchange I designed for them?\nThat question changes the object I am trying to build. A pipeline can work because its author anticipated the intermediate states. A population of learned units would have to make useful states available through its own limited interaction. It might discover a better division of labour, or spend all its time communicating. Giving each unit a name and drawing arrows between them tells me very little about which of those happened.\nA capacity has a setting\nThe idea of reusable intelligence is attractive partly because software reuse makes it feel familiar. A library function can arrive with a declared input, output and contract. A learned capacity also arrives with a history: examples, surrounding representations, assumptions about the other parts and the resources under which it worked. I cannot treat that history as incidental packaging if changing it changes the capacity.\nConsider moving a summariser from a short exchange to a long negotiation. It may still return a fluent account, but the important distinction might now be which proposals were accepted, which were withdrawn and which party could make them. “It summarises” is a poor description of what I need to reuse. A richer account asks what the capacity preserves, when it fails and which downstream decisions remain possible. This is the pressure behind keeping context inside a reusable capability: the useful unit is larger than the attractive output.\nI work on the practical side of that question through Pale: make a person-described world operational, with relationships and capabilities the system can already inspect. Counsel supplies local semantic judgment inside that arrangement. The deeper architectural question is how much of the useful organisation could itself be learned, instead of requiring me to specify every exchange.\nMy research in Causality separates the question of one unit from the question of many. MUON asks what a small adaptive unit might be; HENA asks what organisation among units might make possible. The relationship between Counsel and MUON, and between Pale and HENA, gives me two scales at which to follow the ambition. A better unit could change the judgment within an arrangement; a better many-unit architecture could change how the arrangement itself reasons and organises. The surrounding experience could become simpler as the intelligence beneath it learns more of that work. That separation gives the ambition something to work against. If I change a unit and the whole improves, did the unit improve, did its communication become easier, or did the arrangement give it more resources? I want to understand the contribution, because the answer determines what is reusable.\nThere are narrower ways into the compatibility question. Git Re-Basin studies aligning hidden units through permutations before merging independently trained models in weight space. It gives me something more precise than the wish to move a useful piece of intelligence: what transformation would make two representations compatible, and what behaviour should survive the combination? That is not yet the same as locating a separable capacity to transfer, but it makes one part of the difficulty available for investigation.\nMemory makes the arrangement matter\nImagine a clue arriving early, before anyone knows why it is important. Much later, another part receives a request for which that clue would be decisive. The first part can keep the clue, but keeping it privately does not help the second. Broadcasting everything would make it available and defeat the point of a limited system. A useful arrangement has to make some combination of retention, routing and later recognition work together.\nThis is why distributed memory interests me more than a large bag of messages. The arrangement decides what can become relevant across time. A small unit may have enough capability for its local task while the population fails because nobody can find what another unit kept. Conversely, a simple unit can become useful through a well-placed connection. The early-clue construction makes that problem small enough to examine: local competence and collective usefulness are different things we can actually compare.\nThere is a personal attraction here too. I work across fields in which something encountered casually can become useful much later. The temptation is to call that cross-pollination and leave its operation mysterious. Research lets me ask a narrower question without losing the attraction: what survived the interval, what made it accessible and what did the later work gain from it? A system that simply repeats more of its past has not necessarily become more capable of making those connections.\nThe operating-system image remains useful to me, but it now opens onto several different problems. There is the arrangement of work that I can describe explicitly. There is the learned capacity that I would like to understand well enough to move. There is the organisation that might make partial capacities useful together. I do not need one familiar metaphor to finish all three. What keeps me following them is the possibility that intelligence can live partly in how an encounter is carried forward, not only in the component that first understood it.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/the-intelligence-between-the-parts/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-thinking-in-more-than-one-medium","@type":"Article","name":"Thinking in more than one medium","headline":"Thinking in more than one medium","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["creative thinking","visual composition","sound and attention","narrative sequence","programmable geometry","cross-disciplinary inquiry"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/","@type":"WebPage","name":"Thinking in more than one medium","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Thinking in more than one medium","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://getharsh.in/blog/an-analogy-can-start-an-argument/","https://sleepwalker.in/blog/information-architecture-for-a-story/","https://sleepwalker.in/collections/creative-methods/from-frame-to-sequence/the-gutter-is-an-inference/"]},"description":"A diagram, an image, a sound and a sequence invite different acts of attention. Moving between them can change the question I am able to ask.","datePublished":{"@value":"2025-05-19","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"Thinking in more than one medium\nDraw a person beside a doorway. Then draw the doorway so large that the person nearly disappears. The objects are the same; the encounter is not. Put a light inside the opening and the person's relation to it changes again. I can describe those changes in words, but making the image gives them a form my explanation might never have asked for.\nThat is why creative work matters to my thinking beyond its eventual artifact. An image, a piece of sound or a sequence is not merely a delivery format for an idea already finished elsewhere. The form can make something available to attention that I had not yet understood. I want room to discover that difference before deciding whether it belongs in a story, a design, an argument or a more precise investigation.\nWriting has mattered to me in a more personal way, too. Taking it seriously helped me with anxiety. An upsetting experience can consume attention, but it can also become a prompt for creative work. My instinct has been to observe and move on, spending that attention where something can grow, personally or professionally. Writing can be an outlet for that movement.\nThe diagram asks for a relation\nA diagram is often useful because it refuses some of the richness of the situation. Give it events and arrows, and I have to decide what an arrow means. Dependence? Order? A message? A person's inference? When I cannot answer, the drawing has exposed something that an expansive paragraph could keep pleasantly vague. The small representation has made a particular question unavoidable.\nYet the diagram can also become too persuasive. A line drawn between two things makes the connection look settled. Equal circles make different objects appear interchangeable. Putting one box above another suggests a hierarchy that might belong only to the page. I try to keep the act of drawing available to revision: erase an arrow, change a boundary, ask which relation disappeared. In the inquiry into changing descriptions, that difficulty becomes explicit. A language can answer everything it knows how to ask and still leave the needed relation outside it.\nCreative work lets me encounter a related difficulty from another direction. What if the relation is visible in an image but has not yet become a clear statement? An illustrated threshold might give me hesitation, invitation or dread before I can say exactly which arrangement produced it. I can develop the composition while trying to understand the effect. Naming it too early may narrow the work to an interpretation the image had already exceeded.\nSound gives a thought an interval\nA static mark can be encountered almost at once. Sound asks for an interval. A returning tone, a sustained note or a silence has a relation to what came before and to what I expect next. Consider an imagined instrument that changes its sound when it enters a held state. If every movement produces another alert, I may stop hearing the state and start hearing the interruptions. If the change is too subtle, the relation may never become available at all. The expressive problem is also a problem of attention.\nMy earlier creator studies explored that relation through programmable geometry, music visualisers, lighting and an expressive lamp. These were ways of asking how one form could become active across different media. A shape could move with sound; an object could reveal its orientation; a light could hold a quiet state. The attraction was not simply to synchronise effects. It was to discover which transformations kept a recognisable character while making the present encounter meaningful.\nMusic remains part of my personal creative practice, alongside drawing and technical work. I do not need to make each practice justify itself as a product. Nor do I want to flatten them into one universal method: a sound's temporal relation and a picture's spatial relation give me different things to work with. Their exchange becomes useful when I can hear or see the difference, rather than declare in advance that everything is geometry.\nA sequence gives the reader some of the work\nNow put the person outside the doorway in one frame and inside in the next. Something happened in the interval nobody drew. The reader supplies it. Change the second frame, delay it or place it after a turn of the page, and the supplied relation changes. I find that participation more interesting than a sequence of increasingly spectacular images. The page can make the reader discover something instead of announcing how it should feel.\nThis is one of the reasons the space between panels matters to the work I am developing in Sleepwalker. The image has a place within an order of attention. A beautiful frame can become the wrong frame if it reveals what the sequence needs to withhold. A quiet detail can become decisive when the reader returns to it with another understanding. The work is partly in the relation the page leaves open.\nA narrative has more than one arrangement to hold. An event happened; somebody understood it incorrectly; a reader encountered that account before encountering the event. Revising the reader's discovery need not revise what happened. I am drawn to these several orders of a story because they let an idea retain its consequences while changing how it is encountered. The model gives the author more room to compose. It does not choose what the composition should mean.\nLet the crossing change something\nA mathematical question can make me notice a visual relation. A traditional passage can give an imagined scene a difficulty to hold. An image can make me ask why a person still matters in an enormous frame. Those crossings are part of one person's inquiry without making all the forms interchangeable. A scene can be exact about what a witness encounters while leaving its world physically impossible. A formal construction can be exact about a small question while leaving most of the witness's life outside it.\nI used to reach for large pictures because they gave an unfamiliar thought somewhere to begin. I still want that reach. What changes as the work develops is the willingness to let a form answer back: a calculation exposes the false step, a scene asks for the omitted person, a sequence reveals that the order made the meaning. Each can send me back to the question with something I did not bring into the first attempt.\nKeeping those practices alive together is different from deciding that every drawing is research or every curiosity needs a public project. Some work should remain play. Some questions need the patience of study. Some acquire a useful technical form; others become more honest through an image. I want a working life in which I can follow those changes without asking one title to explain them all.\nThe artifact is where somebody else may eventually meet the inquiry. Before that, making it is already a way of thinking. The doorway can remain a doorway while becoming something I did not know I was trying to understand.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/thinking-in-more-than-one-medium/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/#article","identifier":"content:getharsh-what-i-want-a-memory-to-do","@type":"Article","name":"What I want a memory to do","headline":"What I want a memory to do","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"keywords":["memory and learning","note-taking","rereading","retention","surprise","context selection"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/","@type":"WebPage","name":"What I want a memory to do","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/","breadcrumb":[{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","numberOfItems":2,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"What I want a memory to do","item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/"}}]}],"relatedLink":["https://causality.in/reckon/","https://causality.in/collections/composition-and-learning/what-should-a-surprise-change/","https://rawthoughts.in/collections/systems/context-that-helps-the-work/when-context-helps/"]},"description":"Keeping a thought is easy; recognising when it becomes useful, and what must change when it stops helping, is the learning problem that interests me.","datePublished":{"@value":"2026-10-12","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date"},"dateModified":{"@value":"2026-11-21T09:00:00+05:30","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime"},"text":"What I want a memory to do\nSuppose I write something down because I cannot yet understand why it interests me. Months later, another question arrives and the earlier thought becomes useful. The note did not improve while it waited. Something changed in the relation between what I had kept and what I was trying to understand. That is the part of memory I find most difficult to design for: I would like to preserve the possibility without preserving everything as though everything were equally important.\nIt is easy to confuse the size of an archive with the reach of a mind. More material can give a question more to meet. It can also give an old explanation more ways to keep returning. A memory that retrieves the same persuasive account whenever I encounter difficulty may help me continue, or prevent me from noticing that the difficulty has changed. What I want from it is closer to a productive encounter than to a reliable repetition.\nA note waiting for its question\nImagine keeping a description of how two pieces of equipment connect. For months, the description is less useful than an ordinary stock count. Then a repair fails because the available replacement does not fit. The old connection detail becomes decisive. The memory did not need to predict the exact repair in order to retain something valuable, but the retained detail still needs a route into the new question. An archive can contain it and leave the person asking about it none the wiser.\nMy interest in this goes beyond searching by a better phrase. A new question may reorganise what the old material means. The word “available” might once have meant “present in the store”; it now has to mean “usable for this machine”. The stock-and-compatibility construction makes that change visible. If my account has no place for compatibility, a more confident answer about stock does not help. I need to revisit the description, not just retrieve a better value within it.\nThis is also what makes rereading worthwhile. I can return to the same passage with a question I did not have before and notice a relation that familiarity concealed. The words remain, yet the encounter changes. A note is most valuable to me when it can reconnect me with that possibility: enough of the source, enough of the question I was following, enough of what I had not settled. A conclusion detached from all three can be memorably phrased and remarkably hard to use.\nThree different ways to change\nWhen an experience does not fit my expectation, several responses are possible. I can keep the exception alongside the account. I can adjust the account. Or I can discover that its categories were too small for the question. Those responses operate at different depths. Treating every exception as a reason to rebuild everything makes learning unstable; treating every exception as another item to file makes the original explanation almost impossible to disturb.\nThe distinction has become a research question for me. In MUON's surprise inquiry, retaining something, changing a learned parameter and proposing a structural change are separate responses. I am interested in the choice between them. A discrepancy is valuable because it creates work for the explanation, not because its intensity tells us which explanation should replace it. The inconvenient observation may be a mistake, a rare event, a changed situation or the missing distinction that makes the rest intelligible.\nThat keeps the ambition alive without making the machinery a portrait of a person. A learning experiment can define precisely what a retained cue, an update or a change of structure means in its small world. My own experience is messier: I do not have a loss function for everything I care about, and usefulness can change because my commitments changed. The experiment gives one part of the difficulty a form I can examine. It does not need to explain the whole of learning to be worth doing.\nUseful after the moment has passed\nA cue can matter long after the response that preserved it. Suppose a system sees a signal early, receives an unrelated stream, and is asked much later to act on that signal. A method that excels at the intervening work can still fail because it discarded the one earlier distinction the later task needed. I find this a more revealing memory problem than asking whether a stored phrase can be repeated immediately. The interval gives retention a consequence.\nReckon gives that interest a laboratory setting: retained records and their use can be varied within its benchmark tasks. The related question of updating after feedback asks how a later outcome should change an earlier estimate. An old success need not outweigh a changed situation indefinitely; a recent failure need not erase everything before it. The shape of the update expresses what we think the history still tells us.\nOutside the laboratory, I often want to keep an unfinished thought because it may meet another field later. That possibility is a reason to retain its distinctive question, not to call every collected fragment important. A future connection needs something specific to connect with. When I compress a thought to “interesting idea about learning”, I have preserved my enthusiasm and discarded most of what another inquiry could use.\nWhat difference did remembering make?\nFinding the note feels like success because something familiar returned. The more interesting question is what I could do after finding it. Did it expose the assumption I had forgotten? Did it change a decision? Did it save me from repeating a failed approach, or simply lend another quotation to the approach I already favoured? An opened document is a weak answer to those questions.\nScope brings that question into the working environment I am building. I want it to remember more than relevant passages: the people around a task, the long game of a project and the context that makes the same words mean something different in another relationship. The descriptive picture should help an agent meet the situation without making it a permanent judgment about the people in it.\nThis is the pressure behind learning which context actually helps. Frequency can tell a system what I tend to retrieve; it cannot by itself tell the system whether my habit was useful. Something rarely used can be indispensable when its particular condition appears. Something frequently opened can have become a comfortable distraction. I want a memory that becomes sensitive to that difference, rather than one that makes my previous preferences harder to escape.\nThe technical ambition brings me back to an ordinary personal difficulty. A thought can survive as a sentence and lose the question that made it alive. It can return so familiarly that I mistake recognition for help. I do not want every unfinished thought to become a lifelong obligation. I want the ones that survive to carry enough of their encounter that, when another question arrives, they offer more than the fact that I once found them interesting.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/what-i-want-a-memory-to-do/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}],"isPartOf":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/","identifier":"collection:getharsh-meaning-models-and-creative-practice","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Meaning, models and creative practice","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/privacy/","identifier":"content:getharsh-privacy","@type":"WebPage","name":"Privacy notice","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"Website hosting, email contact, reading preferences and external reading assistance on getharsh.in.","text":"Privacy notice\nHarsh Joshi is responsible for deciding how personal data is used on getharsh.in. RawThoughts Enterprises Private Limited operates the website and handles that data on his instructions. This notice explains the information handled when you read the website or use its contact and reading tools.\nVisiting the website\nGitHub Pages hosts this publication. Serving a page involves connection information, including your IP address and the page requested. GitHub logs visitors' IP addresses for security, including when a visitor is not signed in. GitHub handles that information under its privacy statement and may process it outside India.\nSearch runs in your browser against this website's own index, so what you type is not sent to a search service. Your search is kept in the search page's address, so like any address it can reach GitHub with a page request, appear in your browser history and go with the address if you share it.\nIf your browser has an assistant that supports it, each page offers that assistant two read-only tools: the text of the page you are reading and the same in-browser search. They work on what the page already shows and send nothing to us; what the assistant does with what it reads follows its provider's terms.\nContacting us\nA contact link opens a new message in your own email application. The website does not receive or store the message as a form submission. If you choose to send it, your email service and the recipient's mail service handle the correspondence. Any professional work that follows has its own correspondence and agreement.\nFor privacy questions, a grievance or a request concerning your personal data, write to info@getharsh.in. Include enough information to identify the request, but do not send passwords or unnecessary identity documents. We respond within 30 days and may ask for proportionate verification before disclosing or changing personal data.\nReading preferences\nThe display settings remember your colour and motion choices in your browser on this device. You can change them in Settings or clear the website's stored data through your browser. These preferences are used to display the page; they stay in your browser and are not sent to us.\nAsking about a passage\nWhen you select text and choose a reading assistant, the link opens that provider with the selected passage, nearby context, the section and article titles, the publication's name, and the article and publication addresses. The passage is limited to 4,096 characters and the nearby context to 1,024. Selecting text alone prepares the link in your browser; choosing the provider sends it there.\nThe assistant is an external service. The request is included in its link, so it can also appear in your browser history. The provider's own terms, privacy policy and your account settings govern what it does with the request and any conversation that follows. Reading the article does not require using an assistant.\nHow long information is kept\nWe keep no record of visits on our own systems; GitHub keeps its connection logs for the periods its policy sets. Correspondence you send is kept for as long as the matter it concerns needs it and as the law requires.\nSecurity and incidents\nThis website holds no accounts and receives no form submissions. If a personal-data breach affects you, we inform you and the authorities as applicable law requires.\nYour choices\nFollowing another external link takes you to that service under its own data practices.\nYou may ask us about your data, request correction or deletion, withdraw consent or raise a concern using the contact above. Requests are handled under applicable law; information needed to comply with a legal obligation or resolve a dispute may need to be kept. Withdrawing consent does not undo processing that has already occurred lawfully.\nChanges to the website's data practices will be reflected in this notice. A new optional use that requires consent will ask for that consent rather than treating continued reading as agreement.\nLast reviewed on 22 November 2026 by Harsh Joshi.\n","url":"https://getharsh.in/privacy/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/projects/","identifier":"content:getharsh-projects","@type":"WebPage","name":"The projects I pursue","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"description":"Different homes for my applied, formal, economic and creative work.","text":"A sequence of sandstone arches leads towards a suspended golden crescent.\nRawThoughts\nRawThoughts is where I build useful systems and consult through RTEPL, alongside essays that explore the product and engineering questions behind the work. Two inquiries branch from it: Causality on the mathematics and Post-AGI Economics on the economy.\nFine lines connect a field of points.\nCausality\nI explore how intelligence changes its understanding through mathematical descriptions, adaptive reasoning units and collective architectures, connecting representation, memory and learning.\nA woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street.\nPost-AGI Economics\nI study how cheaper intelligence changes production constraints, ownership and household income, and whether the gains reach the people who need to buy what is produced.\nA reader sits at a window overlooking a teal city and its circular tower.\nSleepwalker\nI am developing a mythic science-fiction graphic novel about seven children, an impossible book and dreams that awaken ruins on distant worlds. Vedic inquiry and studies in image, language and sequence explore the questions and craft behind it.\nA mechanical owl with camera-like eyes and intricately connected parts.\nDAO Studio\nI co-founded DAO Studio in 2022 with the ambition of helping people create decentralised autonomous organisations. The work developed into coordinated emergency response with YOJN Care, inspectable AI applications with YojnAI, and DAOnet’s design for carrying reusable capability between teams.\nFurther questions\nA place to think\nMoving between a research question and a build should not mean reconstructing the whole context each time. I return to what a working environment must remember so that a thought has somewhere to go.\nRead the essay\nMeaning across different forms\nA model, a memory and a creative practice hold different parts of a question. I explore what each lets me notice, how the parts connect and what is lost when one representation is asked to carry everything.\nExplore the inquiry\nChoosing a place to start\nAre these all parts of one project?\nThey share one question: how a person, a model, an organisation or a society with limited knowledge and resources forms a workable picture of reality, acts through it, learns from what follows and keeps agency and accountability as scale and capability grow. The home page asks it in three forms: how does understanding become useful, what should a working system remember, and what can a story let us see that a model misses? Each home follows it in a different way: Causality as mathematics, Post-AGI Economics as economics and Sleepwalker as myth, with RawThoughts where the answers get built and used. A problem in a working system can become a research question; a mathematical idea can suggest a story; an economic change can alter what is worth building. Each needs room to develop its own argument and show what it has actually found.\nWhere should I start?\nRawThoughts follows useful engineering and collaboration, and its products are the place to see that work as one system. Causality takes the formal questions, Post-AGI Economics follows economic consequences, and Sleepwalker opens into a graphic novel and the inquiry and creative work behind it. DAO Studio brings together coordinated response, inspectable AI applications and reusable capability. Product and implementation enquiries go through RawThoughts’ services.\n","image":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/content/getharsh/media/pursuits-reference/rawthoughts.webp","description":"A sequence of sandstone arches leads towards a suspended golden crescent."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/content/getharsh/media/pursuits-reference/causality.png","description":"Fine lines connect a field of points."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/content/getharsh/media/pursuits-reference/post-agi-opening-light.png","description":"A woman stands still amid a crowd moving along a city street."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/content/getharsh/media/pursuits-reference/sleepwalker-opening.webp","description":"A reader sits at a window overlooking a teal city and its circular tower."},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://getharsh.in/assets/content/getharsh/media/pursuits-reference/dao-studio-owl.png","description":"A mechanical owl with camera-like eyes and intricately connected parts."}],"url":"https://getharsh.in/projects/","publisher":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person","identifier":"person:harsh","@type":"Person","name":"Harsh Joshi","url":"https://getharsh.in/","sameAs":[{"@id":"https://github.com/getHarsh"},{"@id":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/getharsh/"}]},"author":[{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/#person"}]},{"@context":{"@version":1.1,"@vocab":"https://schema.org/","url":{"@id":"https://schema.org/url","@type":"@id"}},"@id":"https://getharsh.in/search/","identifier":"content:getharsh-search","@type":"CollectionPage","name":"Search","creativeWorkStatus":"published","inLanguage":"en","usageInfo":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/terms/"},"mainEntity":{"@type":"ItemList","numberOfItems":15,"itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","@type":"CollectionPage","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/building-products-and-teams/a-career-through-problems/","identifier":"journey:getharsh-a-career-through-problems","name":"A career through problems"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"item":{"@id":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-creative-practice/a-future-that-remembers/#article","@type":"Article","url":"https://getharsh.in/collections/meaning-models-and-cre